{"raw": {"https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/German-Language-Tutor_JR109695": {"title": "German Language Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student\u2019s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student\u2019s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator. Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training. Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. Set up their STELLIC office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application. Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus. WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: U$29.20 This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a Problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon). We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-General-Student-Assistant-FWS_JR109286": {"title": "E4 General Student Assistant FWS", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.\u00a0 Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0 and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Stage-Manager_JR109692": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Stage Manager", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Parsons, Mannes, Jazz, Drama\u2019s Production Department at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Stage Manager to join its team. Reporting to the Event Production Manager, the Overhire Stage Manager is directly responsible for supporting and facilitating the production of concerts and special events presented by Parsons, Mannes, Jazz Drama. The Production Stage Manager is responsible for creating and distributing show paperwork, calling performances, keeping soundcheck rehearsals on schedule, and coordinating with the creative and production teams to ensure all events run smoothly and efficiently.\u00a0 Formed in 2026, Parsons, Mannes, Jazz, Drama unites all academic degree programs concentrating in artistic and creative disciplines offered by The New School. Parsons, Mannes, Jazz, Drama students are designers, architects, technicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, musicians, arts managers, multidisciplinary artists, and more. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Production department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Create, edit, manage, and distribute show paperwork in collaboration with the Production Manager Track and call lighting, projections, sound, and deck cues during performances as necessary following run-of-show timelines Keep soundchecks and performances on task and on schedule Oversee the success of all onstage and backstage activities including stage moves, artist wrangling, and other logistical needs Communicate with creative and production teams, performers, crew, and house management to ensure cohesion between all departments during assigned projects and events Possess a strong familiarity with all aspects and specific needs of each individual production Direct and supervise the Assistant Stage Manager(s) and any stage crew, including student workers (as needed) Attend all advance meetings to prepare for events Other duties related to the successful execution of the event as assigned Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS One year previous experience as a production stage manager or three years experience as an assistant stage manager Strong analytical skills and ability to develop creative approaches to complex issues Excellent oral and written communication skills Interpersonal skills that include management, teamwork and collaboration, and conflict resolution Strong leadership capabilities and the ability to remain focused and calm under pressure Attention to detail Time and task management skills, including re-prioritization and an ability to work graciously under strict time constraints Must be able to remain standing for long periods, bend down and/or lift and move up to 50 pounds repeatedly Experienced or able to learn and develop fluency in The New School\u2019s suite of applications including Google Suite, Microsoft Office, WorkDay, Asimut and 25Live. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS\u00a0 Three years previous experience as a production stage manager First Aid/CPR certification Familiarity with working on concerts and/or special events Experience reading stage plots Ability to read music WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite HOURLY RATE: $35.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Graphic-Design-Assistant-Specialist_JR109611": {"title": "Student Graphic Design Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nThe New Store, The New School's official campus retail store, is seeking a Student Graphic Design Assistant Specialist to support marketing and merchandise initiatives through graphic design, content creation, social media and email marketing.\u00a0\u00a0 The position reports to the Associate Director. A successful candidate will be reliable, detail-oriented, customer-focused, and able to work collaboratively in a fast-paced environment. This position will create graphics for advertising and merchandise, assist with social media planning, photography, video, and posting, and contribute to email and mass marketing campaigns. This is an opportunity to gain hands-on experience creating real-world creative content while helping shape The New Store\u2019s visual identity and connection with the university community.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Graphic Design & Creative Development Create digital and print marketing materials for The New Store, including social media graphics, email graphics, advertisements, posters, signage, and promotional materials. Develop original artwork for merchandise and assist with mockups, adaptations, and production-ready files for apparel, accessories, gifts, and special collections. Maintain organized creative files and ensure designs follow The New School's brand standards, visual identity, and licensing requirements. Collaborate with Store staff to brainstorm and develop creative concepts that support merchandise, marketing, and business objectives. Social Media & Content Creation Assist with planning and executing social media campaigns, including content calendars, photography, video, posting, and promotional copy. Capture and create engaging content featuring merchandise, Store activities, events, product launches, and other initiatives, including occasional motion graphics or animation. Schedule and post approved content, track social media analytics, and provide insights and ideas based on audience engagement. Help maintain a consistent visual identity and voice across The New Store's social media channels. Email & Mass Marketing Assist with planning and developing email and mass marketing campaigns promoting merchandise, events, promotions, and Store initiatives. Create graphics, promotional copy, and other visual assets for email and digital marketing campaigns. Assist with formatting, scheduling, and distributing approved communications. Track campaign engagement and help identify opportunities to improve future marketing efforts. Merchandise & Marketing Support Assist with creative development for new merchandise collections, special projects, product launches, and seasonal campaigns. Research design trends, student interests, and creative opportunities to inform merchandise and marketing initiatives. Assist with product photography and visual assets for online merchandise listings, promotions, and displays. Collaborate with Store staff and university partners on creative and promotional projects as assigned. Community & Professional Development Take on increasing responsibility for creative projects while developing skills in graphic design, marketing, social media, content creation, and merchandise development. Build professional portfolio experience through real-world design and marketing projects, where appropriate. Participate in training and professional development opportunities and set developmental goals with a supervisor. Communicate creative ideas, receive and incorporate feedback, and work collaboratively with professional staff and student employees. \u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Portfolio or examples demonstrating graphic design skills and an understanding of typography, composition, color, and visual communication. Familiarity with Adobe Creative Cloud, particularly Photoshop, Illustrator, and/or InDesign; experience with Canva or other digital design tools is a plus. Interest or experience in social media, digital marketing, photography, video, and/or content creation. Basic understanding of digital and print file formats and the ability to prepare files for various applications. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to create clear and engaging promotional copy. Strong attention to detail, organization, and ability to manage multiple projects and meet deadlines. Creative, curious, and self-motivated, with the ability to contribute ideas and take initiative. Ability to work independently and collaboratively, receive feedback, and make revisions through the creative process. Ability to learn new software, platforms, and marketing tools and adapt to changing priorities. Professional, dependable, and able to represent The New Store and The New School positively when working with students, faculty, staff, vendors, and the university community. \u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience creating social media content, including photography, video, short-form content, or motion graphics. Experience with merchandise design, apparel graphics, product photography, or other creative work prepared for physical production. Experience with email marketing, content planning, social media analytics, or digital marketing platforms. WORK MODE Hybrid Position:\u00a0 This is a hybrid role requiring on-site presence to facilitate key team initiatives, event coverage, and retail tasks. Digital assignments may be handled off-site, while strategic alignment and group check-ins will occur on campus or virtually. #LI-DNI \u00a0 HOURLY RATE: $20.00 per hour \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This position will create graphics for advertising and merchandise, assist with social media planning, photography, video, and posting, and contribute to email and mass marketing campaigns. This is an opportunity to gain hands-on experience creating real-world creative content while helping shape The New Store\u2019s visual identity and connection with the university community.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/Materials-Exchange-Student-Assistant_JR109593-1": {"title": "Materials Exchange Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. RESPONSIBILITIES: Management of space & material: Opening, closing space, ensuring that materials are stored safely, and are accessible to students, and keeping space tidy.\u00a0 Executing material exchange transactions: Assisting students with dropoff / pickup of materials, including accounting of points on the material exchange ledgers.\u00a0 Management of volunteer program: Scheduling and training of students volunteering time in the Material Exchange. Collaboration: Work with students, faculty, and staff to create an engaging space where materials are housed for students. Communications: Managing announcements on Material Exchange website, social media.\u00a0 Team Meetings: Being available to meet with Material Exchange director and volunteers MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Interest and Knowledge: Strong interest in the built environment is encouraged. Design Skills: Graphic design experience (Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, etc.). Communication and Teamwork: Strong communicator and team player. Proactivity and Independence: Enthusiastic, proactive, and independent. Time Management: Skilled at executing multiple projects simultaneously with efficient time management and exceptional attention to detail. Feedback Responsiveness: Responsive to feedback. Appreciation: Appreciation for arts, design, student life, and higher education. Availability: Available during office hours, with occasional evening hours to support students Knowledge of SCE: Familiarity with SCE\u2019s disciplines is a plus but not required. WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. This position will require 2 days of on campus work. There will be times that this position can be remote however, this will be discussed as projects come in. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This role will require the management of space & material in addition to opening, closing space, ensuring that materials are stored safely, and are accessible to students, and keeping space tidy.\u00a0This position will also be executing material exchange transactions. Including however not limited to assisting students with dropoff / pickup of materials, including accounting of points on the material exchange ledgers.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---One-Stop-Student-Service-Center_JR109673": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant - One Stop Student Service Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nThe One Stop Student Service Center is seeking to hire a Student Assistant to work in-person at the One Stop location at 2 West 13th Street, Room 506. The One Stop Student Service Center is a frontline team that assists students, their families, and alumni with questions about financial aid, billing, payment, financial holds, student records, and ordering transcripts. We prepare enrollment verifications and a variety of different types of forms for students. We offer assistance in person, by phone, and by email. We work collaboratively with colleagues across the university to resolve questions that students present to us. Our partner offices are Financial Aid, the Registrar\u2019s Office, and Student Accounts.\u00a0 We specifically seek a Student Assistant who can work with us on Tuesdays and Thursdays from 9:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. and on another weekday, as well. Ideally, the Student Assistant will work for 10 hours per week. RESPONSIBILITIES Welcoming students, their guardians, and alumni to the One Stop Student Service Center. Checking in students who have made appointments via the New Queue system, and contacting colleagues to alert them of the students' arrival. Helping students to check in or make appointments in the New Queue system Listening to voicemail messages and logging them into a shared spreadsheet Assisting with in-person, telephone, and email inquiries Assisting with office and event support for the One Stop team as assigned Assisting with promotion of the One Stop to students Creating a variety of documents in Google Suite and Excel Students must understand the importance of and maintain confidentiality in daily work operations. Students will be required to engage in training regarding the Family Educational Rights & Privacy Act, (FERPA) and abide by all university policies and procedures. This is a part-time, hourly position with a schedule of 16 hours per week.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Full-time student status at The New School Commitment to student service\u00a0 Prior office experience Friendliness Strong organizational skills Excellent oral communication skills Strong writing skills Proficiency in Google Suite Familiarity with Excel Strong follow-up skills with attention to detail Ability to work independently\u00a0 Ability to adhere to University COVID-19 policy and requirements PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience in and interest in building & maintaining a knowledge base Experience in and interest in creating a digital manual Experience in and talent in creating a variety of artifacts using Notebook LM Ideas about and delight in improving the student experience HOURLY RATE: $17.00 per hour WORK MODE : On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Special Instructions: To apply, please submit a r\u00e9sum\u00e9 and a cover letter. Be sure to note the days and times you are available, and your phone number. Our office hours \u00a0currently are 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. Monday-Friday. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Welcoming students, their guardians, and alumni to the One Stop Student Service Center. Checking in students who have made appointments via the New Queue system, and contacting colleagues to alert them of the students' arrival. Helping students to check in or make appointments in the New Queue system, listening to voicemail messages and logging them into a shared spreadsheet and assisting with in-person, telephone, and email inquiries. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/Associate-Director-for-Experiential-Learning_JR109646": {"title": "Associate Director for Experiential Learning", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a08,300 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Center for Career Development is dedicated to helping students make connections between their academic experiences and professional paths. We empower students in their career journeys by providing comprehensive resources, guidance, and support. Our aim is to assist students and alumni to explore career options, developing essential skills, and navigating the job market effectively. Through personalized counseling, workshops, networking opportunities, and access to relevant tools and information, we strive to foster confidence, resilience, and success in every stage of career development. The Associate Director for Experience reports to the Senior Director of the Center for Career Design and Development and serves as a key member of the Center's leadership team. The Associate Director assists in the management and long-term strategic planning of experiential learning initiatives across the university. The primary role of the Associate Director, Experience is to provide leadership and oversight of The New School's experiential learning and internship programs, including compliance, assessment, employer engagement, student support, and cross-functional collaboration with academic and administrative partners. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Leadership & Operations Support Strategic Development: Support the strategic development and expansion of cooperative education, internships, and other experiential learning initiatives for the university in collaboration with Center leadership, academic programs, employers, and university partners. Oversight & Compliance: Provide strategic leadership and operations for university-wide experiential learning processes, policies, procedures, and systems, including the Experiential Learning Agreement (ELA) process, to ensure efficient service delivery and institutional compliance. Strategic Projects & Technology: Collaborate with Center leadership on special projects, strategic initiatives, and the implementation of technologies and digital resources that advance student success, career readiness, and experiential learning outcomes. Industry & Regulatory Trends: Identify emerging trends, best practices, and relevant changes in experiential education, employment, labor, and immigration regulations, and recommend innovative approaches, policy updates, and operational improvements as appropriate. Reporting & Assessment: Prepare reports, assessments, and recommendations for Center leadership regarding experiential learning participation, student engagement, service delivery, and outcomes. Academic & Campus Partnerships Academic Affairs Alignment: Partner closely with Curriculum Learning and Academic Affairs (CLAA) to ensure alignment of experiential learning initiatives with academic policy, curriculum, assessment, international programs and partnerships, and other university-wide academic priorities. Faculty & Curriculum Integration: Collaborate with faculty, academic departments, and university partners to promote experiential learning opportunities, strengthen student participation, and integrate career-connected learning across the student experience. International Student Support: Collaborate closely with International Student and Scholar Services (ISSS) and other university partners to support Curricular Practical Training (CPT) and experiential learning opportunities for international students. Advisory & Resource Point: Serve as a primary resource for students, faculty, staff, employers, and campus partners regarding experiential learning opportunities, internship administration, policies, procedures, and related compliance matters. Employer Relations & Partnerships Employer & Partner Engagement: Develop and maintain relationships with employers, nonprofit organizations, community partners, and industry representatives to expand high-quality internship, cooperative education, and other experiential learning opportunities, in collaboration with Center leadership and Industry Engagement colleagues. Quality & Standards Alignment: Collaborate with employers and campus partners to ensure experiential learning opportunities align with university standards, student learning objectives, and applicable policies and regulations. Student Advising, Training & Programming Student Preparation & Programming: In collaboration with Center leadership, design and implement programming, workshops, and resources that prepare students to successfully pursue and engage in internships and other experiential learning opportunities. Student Advising & Equity: Provide advising and consultation to students regarding experiential learning opportunities, workplace expectations, and professional development, while advancing equitable access and participation among diverse student populations. Resource Development: Develop training materials, resources, and guidance for students, faculty, staff, and employers to promote understanding of experiential learning policies, procedures, and best practices. Team Supervision & Staff Development Direct Report Leadership: Establish goals and priorities with direct reports and support their professional development and performance in alignment with Center and University objectives. Student Staff Supervision: Assist in the supervision and development of student workers supporting Center operations, programs, and experiential learning initiatives. Additional Responsibilities Other Duties: Other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Master\u2019s degree or equivalent experience required.\u00a0 5 years of professional experience, preferably in a discipline related to fields offered by The New School with previous experience in higher education, employer relations, or career services. Experience working with Curricular Practical Training (CPT), SEVIS, and international student employment regulations. Demonstrated experience supervising staff and managing complex programs or operations. Knowledge of experiential learning practices, internship administration, and student development. Demonstrated ability to work independently and on a team, working under deadlines. Excellent writing, networking, and communications skills.\u00a0 Demonstrated knowledge of current hiring trends in career development.\u00a0 Strong project management, organizational, assessment, and event planning skills. Proficiency in MS Word, Excel, and PowerPoint and G-Suite;\u00a0 Experience with career management systems (Handshake, 12Twenty). Experience with cooperative education. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee.#LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $83,500 - $85,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/FWS-Research-Assistant---People-s-AI---Data-Lab-for-Social-Research_JR109651": {"title": "FWS Research Assistant - People\u2019s AI & Data Lab for Social Research", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The People\u2019s AI & Data Lab for Social Research is a hub for computational social science and responsible AI at The New School. The Lab explores how the social sciences can lead the development of responsible AI grounded in social justice and public purpose. It brings together researchers, students, and partners to co-create tools, methods, and training that center equity, context, and social impact, including through the KAIA Network (Knowledge and AI for All) and the Women in AI Fellowship. The Research Assistant will play a central role in the Lab\u2019s convening program during the 2026\u201327 academic year. Working closely with Lab leadership and staff, the Research Assistant will support the planning, coordination, and delivery of a two-day workshop on a Human Rights-Based Approach to AI, a one-day workshop on AI in the Public Sector, and other convenings hosted by the Lab. The role blends event logistics with research support, from coordinating speakers and participants to preparing background materials and documenting workshop discussions. The successful candidate is highly organized, detail-oriented, and a clear, professional communicator who is comfortable engaging with scholars, practitioners, civil society, and public-sector partners. They bring a genuine interest in the social, ethical, and policy dimensions of AI. By helping these convenings run smoothly and capturing what emerges from them, the Research Assistant directly advances the Lab\u2019s mission and its visibility as a leading space for public-interest AI research. The Research Assistant reports to the Director of the People\u2019s AI &amp; Data Lab for Social Research. The Research Assistant is expected to work on campus 1\u20132 days per week and in person for the full duration of all workshops and convenings, with the remaining hours worked remotely. The anticipated schedule is approximately 15\u201320 hours per week, with additional hours around workshop dates as needed and within university limits on student employment. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Coordinate logistics and planning for Lab workshops and convenings Develop and maintain planning timelines, task trackers, and run-of-show documents for the two-day Human Rights-Based Approach to AI workshop, the one-day AI in the Public Sector workshop, and other Lab events Reserve rooms and coordinate catering, audiovisual equipment, and in-person, hybrid, or virtual participation setup in collaboration with the appropriate university offices Arrange travel and accommodation for invited speakers and participants, and prepare related vendor, honoraria, and reimbursement paperwork in accordance with university procedures Manage communications with speakers, participants, and partners Draft and send invitations, confirmations, reminders, and pre-workshop information packets in a timely and professional manner Build and maintain accurate contact lists, registration records, and RSVP tracking for each convening Respond promptly to participant and speaker inquiries before, during, and after events Provide on-site support during workshops and convenings Set up and break down event spaces and staff registration and check-in in a welcoming and professional manner Monitor the agenda in real time to keep sessions on schedule, and troubleshoot logistical or technical issues as they arise Take detailed notes during sessions, capturing key discussion points, questions, and recommendations Conduct research to support workshop content Identify, compile, and summarize academic literature, policy documents, and resources on human rights-based approaches to AI, AI governance, and the Prepare briefing materials, speaker biographies, reading lists, and participant resource packets ahead of each convening Research and recommend relevant scholars, practitioners, and organizations as potential speakers or participants Document and synthesize convening outcomes Draft workshop summaries, key takeaways, and reports for internal use and for sharing with participants and the public Design and distribute post-event feedback surveys, and summarize results to inform future convenings Organize and archive event materials, presentations, recordings, and notes in the Lab\u2019s shared drive Support Lab outreach and general operations Draft event announcements and content for the Lab\u2019s website, newsletters, and social media channels Coordinate event listings and promotion with university communications staff Maintain shared calendars and project documentation, and assist with additional Lab research projects as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Minimum qualifications: Current enrollment at The New School in good academic standing; graduate students in the social sciences, public policy, international affairs, human rights, data science, or a related field are strongly encouraged to apply Demonstrated interest in the social, ethical, human rights, or policy dimensions of AI and data Strong organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to track many details and manage multiple deadlines at once Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including professional correspondence with faculty, external experts, and public-sector partners Ability to locate, synthesize, and clearly summarize academic and policy Proficiency with standard productivity and collaboration tools, such as word processing, spreadsheets, shared drives, video conferencing, and online registration or survey platforms Ability to work both independently and collaboratively, exercise sound judgment, and handle participant information with discretion Availability to work in person for the full duration of scheduled workshops, including the two-day Human Rights-Based Approach to AI workshop and the one-day AI in the Public Sector workshop, with occasional early morning or evening hours around events. Preferred Qualification: Prior experience with event planning, conference coordination, or program administration Familiarity with international human rights frameworks and/or AI governance and public-sector technology debates Experience working in international, multilingual, or cross-cultural settings WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE : $31.37/hr This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. Responsibilities: The Research Assistant supports the end-to-end planning and delivery of the Lab\u2019s workshops and convenings, including a two-day workshop on a Human Rights-Based Approach to AI and a one-day workshop on AI in the Public Sector. Responsibilities include coordinating event logistics, managing communications with speakers and participants, and providing on-site support, as well as conducting background research and producing post-event documentation such as notes, summaries, and reports. The Research Assistant also provides general research and administrative support to advance the Lab\u2019s broader research and convening agenda. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/FWS-Student-Assistant---Student-Health-Services_JR109653": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant \u2013 Student Health Services", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nPart time\n\nThe Work Study Student Assistant provides administrative and operational support that contributes to the efficient daily functioning of Student Health Services. Working collaboratively with staff across multiple service areas, the student assists with office organization, inventory management, wellness initiatives, and general administrative projects while gaining valuable professional experience in a healthcare setting. RESPONSIBILITIES Provide project-based support for all areas of Student Health Services, including Counseling, Medical, Insurance, and Wellness. Organize and restock office materials, educational brochures, and pamphlets in reception and waiting areas. Monitor, organize, and maintain inventory of office and program supplies. Retrieve Student Health Services mail and packages from the campus mailroom. Perform general administrative duties including copying, scanning, shredding, filing, and organizing storage areas. Update and maintain community resource lists used by the Medical and Counseling Services teams. Organize and prepare wellness kits, outreach materials, and health promotion resources. Complete additional departmental projects as assigned, including watering office plants and assisting with sustainability initiatives such as compost disposal. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Approved to participate in the Federal Work-Study Program. Ability to maintain strict confidentiality in accordance with Student Health Services policies. Strong organizational and time management skills with the ability to prioritize multiple assignments. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively as part of a team. Strong computer skills, including proficiency with Microsoft Word and Microsoft Excel. Excellent interpersonal, communication, and customer service skills. Ability to adapt to changing priorities and perform a variety of administrative tasks with attention to detail. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Previous administrative, office, customer service, or healthcare-related experience. Experience organizing supplies, maintaining inventory, or completing clerical tasks. Interest in healthcare, wellness, counseling, public health, or higher education. Familiarity with Microsoft Office applications and general office equipment. WORK MODE On-Campus Position \u2013 Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $21.00 Applicants must be approved to participate in the Federal Work-Study Program. Please submit a current resume outlining your relevant experience and qualifications We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Work Study Student Assistant supports the daily operation of Student Health Services by assisting with office organization, inventory management, wellness initiatives, and administrative projects. Working alongside healthcare staff across multiple service areas, the student gains valuable professional experience while ensuring efficient service delivery. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/Assistant-Director--Study-Abroad-Advising---International-Education_JR109645": {"title": "Assistant Director, Study Abroad Advising & International Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a08,300 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Reporting to the Assistant Provost for Academic Advising, the Assistant Director is responsible for coordinating all student-facing functions of the university\u2019s global, exchange, and study abroad programs, as well as acting as the primary advising contact for students participating in these programs. Partnering with faculty, staff, and administrators, the Assistant Director will oversee communications, policies, and programming university-wide for international educational experiences. This is a full-time position and supervises part-time student assistant(s). CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Student Advising & Program Management Oversee study abroad and exchange advising and support services for all students across the university. Manage all aspects of the study abroad application process, maintaining a regular advising schedule for students. Develop and execute comprehensive pre-departure and re-entry programming series for all study abroad participants. Serve as the university representative and primary advisor for prestigious international scholarships and fellowships (e.g., Gilman, Fulbright, Boren), including direct support for Fulbright recipients. Institutional Collaboration & External Partnerships Serve as primary liaison with university offices (Admissions, ISSS, Student Financial Services, Registrar, Housing, Dean of Students) and external partners regarding mobility students. Manage exchange student nominations and intake processes while serving as the primary university point of contact for global partner institutions. Work closely with third-party program providers to ensure optimal, high-quality student experiences. Provide training to relevant campus colleagues on study abroad programs, policies, and office functions. Operations, Risk Management & Systems Oversee and update the Terra Dotta (Go Abroad) application management system. Manage and track information, logistics, and safety protocols for all international student travel (including non-study abroad research, fieldwork, and faculty-led excursions). Serve as the primary institutional point of contact for international emergency response and crisis management. Assist the Curriculum, Learning, and Academic Affairs team in assessing international education opportunities through accurate record-keeping and data tracking. Outreach, Events & Special Initiatives Develop, arrange, and lead in-person and virtual information events and outreach initiatives throughout the year for students, staff, and faculty. Coordinate major campus event programming, including study abroad orientations and admitted student Welcome Days. Perform other duties and projects as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent work experience related to the position.\u00a0 Three (3) years of progressive experience in relevant areas. A demonstrated student-centered approach and an understanding of student development, higher education, and the education abroad field. Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills. Preferred: Experience living/working outside the U.S. Preferred: Prior experience managing Terra Dotta, or similar software. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $75,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/N---66-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Assistant--Equipment-Resource-Center---Photography-and-Media-Resources_JR109649": {"title": "Student Assistant, Equipment Resource Center - Photography and Media Resources", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Photography Labs and Equipment Resource Center within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff. Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor. Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them. Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures. Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously. Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests. Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Darkroom and Alternative Process Labs - Students are responsible for setting up and maintaining equipment, mixing and preparing photographic chemicals, and keeping the space clean and organized. Assist other students with darkroom procedures, manage supplies, enforce safety protocols, troubleshoot issues, and document processes. Additionally, you might train new users and help with routine maintenance and repairs to ensure everything runs efficiently. Print and Scanning Labs - In the scanning and print lab, student workers will be responsible for setting up and operating scanning and printing equipment, managing and organizing digital files, and ensuring high-quality output. They will assist users with file preparation, troubleshoot technical issues, and maintain and calibrate equipment. Additionally, the role includes managing inventory of paper, ink, and other supplies, keeping the workspace clean and organized, and providing support and guidance to fellow students on lab procedures and equipment use. Equipment Resource Center - As a student worker assisting technicians in the daily operations of an equipment resource center, you will support the setup, maintenance, and operation of various equipment and tools. Responsibilities include effectively using Webcheckout for managing checkouts and returns, helping with routine inspections, troubleshooting technical issues, and ensuring equipment is clean and in good working order. You\u2019ll assist in managing inventory, including tracking and restocking supplies, and provide support to users by explaining equipment functionality and usage procedures. Additionally, you\u2019ll contribute to organizing and maintaining a safe and efficient work environment, and collaborate with technicians to streamline daily operations and address any operational challenges. Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery. Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness. Complete special projects as assigned. Participate in training sessions and staff meetings. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from the supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users. Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail. Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Ability to lift items up to 25lbs. Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI \u00a0 HOURLY RATE: $17.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests, assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/Social-Media---Publicity-Assistant-for-Social-Research-Journal---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109650": {"title": "Social Media & Publicity Assistant for Social Research Journal - Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Social Research: An International Quarterly, an academic journal based at the New School for Social Research, is seeking an advanced undergraduate student or graduate student to fill an hourly part-time (up to 10 hrs/week) position running the journal\u2019s social media and helping with publicity and marketing efforts. Start date is as soon as possible, with expectation to commit to the position through Academic Year 2026/27 and preferably longer. This position comes with room for growth and career mentorship to learn about and gain experience in marketing & publicity, editorial management, and journal production. The position reports to the journal\u2019s managing editor. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Draft copy and create visuals for several social media posts a week (X, Bluesky, Instagram, Facebook) Develop schedule and identify content for posts Schedule posts weekly via a scheduling platform Research best algorithmic practices and trends in content creation and communications for various platforms Keep track, evaluate, and share social media analytics with the journal team Participate in other publicity effort (e.g., regular Mailchimp newsletters, paid advertising) QUALIFICATIONS College-level coursework in social sciences and/or humanities and/or liberal arts Experience in social media management and strong knowledge of various social media platforms (experience with X and Instagram a must) Strong written and verbal communication skills Experience using Google Suite Design capabilities to create graphics for social media using Canva, Illustrator, and/or Photoshop Detail-oriented multitasker Current TNS student WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI RATE: $20/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. Responsibilities: Under the direction of the journal\u2019s editor and managing editor the social media publicity assistant develops and implements social media strategy for the journal and assists with other publicity efforts. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Assistant-Director-of-Alumni-and-Volunteer-Engagement_JR109231": {"title": "Assistant Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. \u00a0 POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW \u00a0 The Assistant Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement creates, strengthens, and manages volunteer initiatives that cultivate dynamic, supportive alumni networks and meaningful opportunities for engagement in support of Development and Alumni Engagement efforts at The New School. \u00a0 Reporting to the Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement, the Assistant Director develops opportunities for alumni at every career and life stage\u2014from recent graduates to established alumni\u2014to contribute their time, expertise, networks, and resources in support of students, fellow alumni, and the university. \u00a0 The position leads university-wide alumni volunteer programs, with particular emphasis on alumni-to-student mentorship, career engagement, reunion host committees, admissions volunteer opportunities, regional and affinity engagement, and online alumni communities. The Assistant Director is responsible for recruiting, training, and supporting volunteers while building programs that foster lifelong connection. The role combines relationship management, communications, data analysis, and hands-on program execution. The Assistant Director works closely with both external constituents and internal colleagues across The New School.\u00a0 \u00a0 Alumni Volunteer Programs Manage university-wide alumni volunteer programs, including annual goals, strategies, timelines, participation targets, and measures of success, that advance institutional priorities and strengthen lifelong alumni connection. Create inclusive opportunities for alumni at all career and life stages to contribute their time, expertise, and professional networks and engage with students, recent graduates, fellow alumni, and the University. Develop and maintain a comprehensive volunteer-management framework covering role development, recruitment, screening, onboarding, training, communication, support, recognition, stewardship, retention, and participation tracking. Support the annual Alumni Reunion by designing and managing reunion volunteer operations, including committee recruitment, onboarding, training, role development, outreach plans, participation goals, communications, and ongoing support. Assist the Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement with developing and managing the Alumni Council of The New School, including member recruitment, onboarding, communications, meeting preparation, and ongoing engagement. Act as lead coordinator for the Alumni and Student Engagement Committee of the Alumni Council by setting annual priorities, developing agendas, facilitating meetings, coordinating follow-up, and reporting outcomes to leadership. \u00a0 Alumni and Student Engagement Design and manage alumni-to-student mentoring, career networking, and professional-development initiatives in partnership with Career Services, the schools, faculty, and campus offices. Support the Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement in developing a comprehensive regional and local engagement strategy by developing priority alumni lists, segmented engagement strategies, and industry-based communities based on expertise, affiliation, career stage, volunteer history, and engagement interests. Build and support mentoring, career, industry, regional, affinity, and interest-based communities across The New School Network and other approved digital platforms. Represent Alumni Engagement at university programs, volunteer activities, alumni events, and institutional gatherings. \u00a0 Digital Engagement, Communications, and Data Manage alumni digital platforms, including LinkedIn and Graduway, and develop digital strategies that promote interaction, knowledge sharing, professional connection, and engagement. Partner with schools and campus offices to increase awareness, adoption, and effective use of alumni digital platforms; monitor participation, assess engagement, gather feedback, and recommend improvements. Write, edit, and proofread clear, compelling, inclusive, and accessible alumni volunteer communications. Enter and review alumni data in the Salesforce CRM to improve the accuracy and completeness of alumni career, employer, title, industry, volunteer, event, committee, mentoring, engagement, and contact information. \u00a0 QUALIFICATIONS A minimum of three years of relevant professional experience in higher education, nonprofit advancement, alumni relations, community engagement, volunteer management, or another mission-driven environment, with a demonstrated record of directing and growing community volunteerism, engagement programs, communications, and events in an education or nonprofit setting. Exceptional organizational, verbal, written, and interpersonal communication skills, with strong attention to detail. Strong relationship-building and collaboration skills, with the ability to work effectively across departments, academic units, and teams. Demonstrated proficiency with CRM systems, event registration tools, online community platforms, or comparable technologies. Experience developing career, mentoring, professional networking, or student-to-alumni engagement programs preferred. Ability to independently and collaboratively manage multiple programs, events, communications projects, partnerships, stakeholders, and deadlines in a frequently evolving environment. Commitment to accessible, inclusive, and audience-centered engagement.. Availability to work some evenings and weekends and travel occasionally, as required. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE $70,000-$78,000 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. \u00a0 BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff . We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Props-Artisan_JR109620": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Props Artisan", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nThe College of Performing Arts (CoPA) at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Props Artisan to join its Performance Operations \u2013 Props Department Staff. The Props Department is responsible for the creation, alteration, and sourcing of all props for productions in the Mannes School of Music, School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and School of Drama.\u00a0 The Overhire Props Artisan is a qualified and organized collaborator who can follow instructions and work independently. Someone who thrives in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly team-oriented environment.The Overhire Props Artisan is delegated as needed to work on prop builds, alterations, storage pulls, and/ or shopping runs. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the props department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Act as an artisan on show builds and alterations Develop familiarity with to-do lists, prop lists, and project specific paperwork Ability to act as the designated Production representative during tech rehearsals Travel to vendors or storage locations to procure and cart supplies and materials as needed Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS One to three years previous experience in theatrical properties required Excellent written and verbal communication skills The ability to work productively, graciously, and professionally under strict time and space constraints Comfortable with power tools and heights Strong carpentry, sewing, and upholstery skills Experienced or able to learn and develop fluency in The New School\u2019s suite of applications including Google Suite, Microsoft Office, Workday, and Adobe Photoshop Demonstrated commitment to Equity, Inclusion, Social Justice, and the transformative power of the arts and education PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Driver\u2019s license and comfort driving a cargo van Experience in an educational or teaching environment WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. HOURLY RATE: $27.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Gig-Office--Student-Assistant_JR109628": {"title": "Gig Office, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nPOSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Gig Office at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Gig Office, Student Assistant. We\u2019re looking for a(n) motivated, organized, communicative individual(s) to support initiatives within the Gig Office. This position reports to the Program and Administration Manager at the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in booking and artist management. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with booking students for Gig Office performance opportunities. Communicate gig details and availability with students and clients. Help send and track gig agreements and other required documents. Help maintain the Gig Office Artist Roster and gig records. Assist with basic data entry, scheduling, email communication, and other Gig Office tasks. Support the Jazz Office with other administrative tasks as needed. QUALIFICATIONS Ability to manage multiple tasks, deadlines, communications, and documents accurately and efficiently. Strong written and verbal communication skills Capability to work independently and collaboratively with a team Proficient with standard office technology and software Strong attention to detail WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $17 Hours per Week: 5, or as needed. Not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek while classes are in session. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * (if applicable) Applicants should submit both a resume and cover letter outlining their interest, skills, and experience pertaining to this position; and a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Assist with booking students for Gig Office performance opportunities by communicating event details and availability with clients and performers while tracking required agreements and documentation. Maintain the Gig Office Artist Roster and performance records while handling general data entry, scheduling, and administrative tasks to support both the Gig and Jazz Offices as needed. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/Design-Workshop-Exhibition-Build--Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109623": {"title": "Design Workshop Exhibition Build: Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Responsibilities: Evaluate and document the exhibition space at 72 5th Avenue. Design in plan and in elevation the exhibition materials, based on materials produced during the spring 2026 academic year. Adjust and finalize materials from the Spring 2026 Design IV studio (Design Workshop) in conformance with the exhibition space, design, and intent.\u00a0 These include completing the full-scale material studies, scale models, and 2d representations, texts, graphics, and quantifiable information.\u00a0 Create final presentation boards and exhibition graphics as per design. Move all materials from 25 E. 13th street for exhibition. Organize, promote, and host an opening for the exhibition at a date TBD. When completed, de-install exhibition, and re-purpose materials as per circular design guidelines developed by each team for their ADU prototypes.\u00a0 Minimum Qualifications: Participation in the Design IV Design Workshop studio or related graduate-level design studios in Spring 2026 which provides the framework, skills and knowledge to engage this work as outlined above. Schedule requirements outside of regular business hours WORK MODE: On campus position\u00a0#LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $21/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Students will evaluate and document the exhibition space at 72 5th Avenue. Students will also design in plan and in elevation the exhibition materials, based on materials produced during the spring 2026 academic year. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---BFA-DT-Program-Assistant_JR109621": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist - BFA DT Program Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nThe BFA Design & Technology Program seeks to hire a Program Assistant to assist with the organization, cataloging, and maintenance of technology/hardware/tools, in order to facilitate better access for students and to prepare for our end-of-year events. Attention to detail, knowledge of technology & hardware, and experience using tools (such as assembling furniture) are preferred skills. RESPONSIBILITIES Identify and test computer hardware Maintain a catalog of our tech inventory Reorganize storage Assemble furniture Sort and organize cables MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with D12 and the BFA Design & Technology Program Proficient with tools Demonstrated attention to detail Knowledge of spreadsheet formula use WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RAT E: $20.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant will assist in the overhaul of BFADT\u2019s inventory of technology by testing, sorting, organizing, and cataloging all of the hardware and tools that are maintained for student use. Additionally, the Student Assistant(s) will assist in assembling furniture to facilitate end-of-year activities. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/Fine-Arts-Studio-Monitor_JR109619": {"title": "Fine Arts Studio Monitor", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nPart time\n\nStudio Monitors support the smooth operation of the studios for the BFA & MFA Fine Arts programs at 25 East 13th St. This position, intended for student worker employment, expects a commitment of 5 hours per week for 15 weeks during a regular (Fall or Spring) semester. Employees may be eligible for rehire during the following term. RESPONSIBILITIES Daily studio monitoring for all student studios on the 4th and 5th floors; Maintaining installation space reservation calendars for the 4th and 5th floors;\u00a0 Assist in maintaining and organizing art studio classrooms;\u00a0 Support fellow students in the cleaning and organizing of storage areas on the 4th and 5th floors;\u00a0 Ensuring students follow studio rules and best practices protocols;\u00a0 Assist with art gallery activities as needed. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Be enrolled in the BFA or MFA Fine Arts Program Organized and detail-oriented Quick understanding of existing guidelines and best practices for studio monitoring and functioning Ability to work and communicate with students faculty and staff from both the BFA and MFA programs as well as the broader University community, faculty and staff WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: \u00a0 $20.00/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The studio monitors are expected to support daily studio monitoring for all student studios on the 4th and 5th floors; maintain installation space reservation calendars and spaces, and assist fellow students in the cleaning and organizing of storage areas on the 4th and 5th floors. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Director-of-Strategic-Enrollment-Marketing_JR109566-1": {"title": "Director of Strategic Enrollment Marketing", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a09,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The New School is a progressive, historic university in New York City, celebrated for redefining the boundaries of premier design, performing arts, liberal arts, and social research education. It is transitioning to a dynamic two-college model, designed to integrate all art, design, and performing arts within one college and liberal arts and social research in a second. To support this model, the Marketing & Communication (M&C) department is creating a highly supportive, integrated, and collaborative administrative ecosystem which includes two complementary and interwoven types of operating structures: one designed to provide an enterprise-wide spine of service and support and another with integrated M&C teams dedicated to each of the two colleges.\u00a0 Reporting to the AVP for Parsons and Performing Arts Marketing & Communications, this Strategic Enrollment Marketing Director functions as an embedded leader dedicated to this college in our new university structure. They will be assigned to support specific schools and programs within this college, which is home to the Parsons, Mannes, New School Jazz and Contemporary Music, and New School Drama brands. This position bridges deep storytelling about our education in design, creative and performing arts with rigorous, data-informed marketing strategies to support undergraduate and graduate enrollment at our Parsons and Performing Arts college. This Strategic Enrollment Marketing Director ensures an authentic, compelling, and differentiating representation of our brands, academic experience, and scholarly initiatives to prospective students and key direct or cultural influencers. This professional must be a sophisticated marketing strategist, an expert relationship builder, and a dedicated advocate for the creative, scholarly, and progressive values that define The New School, and the hallmark brands of Parsons, Mannes, Jazz and Drama, and the centers, labs, and institutes affiliated with them. \u00a0 STRUCTURE & RELATIONSHIPS As a member of the Brand, Marketing & Communication department, this position reports to the AVP for Parsons and Performing Arts Marketing and Communication. Integrated as the strategic lead for assigned schools and programs with the BMC team dedicated to this college; leads marketing, positioning, and brand recruitment initiatives with this team for assigned schools and programs.\u00a0Collaborating closely with Event Marketing, PR, and Community Engagement Directors within the M&C team, builds integrated marketing programs that elevate and promote engagement and enrollment. Works in close, continuous partnership with Enrollment Management (EM) and Admissions Directors, College Deans, Academic Program Chairs, faculty, and the full suite of marketing functional teams (Creative, Editorial, Media Relations, and Digital Platforms). Collaborates with counterparts within Parsons and Performing Arts assigned to sister schools and programs, as well as with counterparts in the second university college housing Lang and The New School for Social Research, to tell the robust and compelling story of distinctiveness offered via an educational experience at Parsons and Performing Arts and at The New School. \u00a0 KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Positioning, Awareness and Recruitment Marketing Academic Brand Advocacy: Support academic and enrollment leadership in defining and articulating unique, progressive brand value propositions and creative and intellectual contributions of Parsons and Performing Arts to the university narrative. Strategic Brief Architecture: Author the foundational strategic and creative briefs that distill market data and college/program identities into actionable guides for Creative, Editorial, Paid Media, and Digital Content activation team members. Brand Stewardship & Recruitment Strategy: Collaborating closely with college-dedicated colleagues, formulate, execute, and manage comprehensive annual strategic positioning, marketing and communications plans tailored specifically to support brand awareness, reputation-building, and student recruitment and for assigned schools and programs. Enrollment Partnership Alignment: Work in close, parallel alignment with Enrollment Management (EM) and recruitment communications peers to ensure a seamless, high-converting prospect experience that builds enrollment for undergraduate and graduate degree offerings. Targeted Campaign Strategy: Lead the development and deployment of prospect-focused marketing campaigns across owned and paid channels to meet institutional enrollment targets. Assessment & Optimization: In collaboration with EMSP and BMC partners, establish clear success metrics and tracking KPIs for marketing initiatives, evaluating performance against targets to continuously optimize strategies and activations. Audience Intelligence & Cross-Functional Collaboration Market Intelligence & Analytics: Initiate, monitor, and evaluate audience insights and competitive landscape trends in higher education and art, design and performing arts education to provide ongoing recommendations to academic leaders regarding program appeal and market trends. Cross-Functional Orchestration: Act as the core liaison for assigned schools and programs with the integrated, dedicated BMC team and central spine resources, coordinating with PR, Social Media, and Online Marketing Platforms to integrate creative-practice and scholarly content into web hubs, newsletters, external campaigns. Public Activations: Integrate with the strategic marketing of major performances, events, and academic milestones to elevate the external visibility of creative education and our brands within the broader New York City and global community. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Education: Bachelor\u2019s degree in marketing, communications, liberal arts, advertising, or a related field required. Professional Experience: 7\u201310 years of progressive experience in strategic marketing leadership, brand strategy, or conversion-driven communication roles within higher education, a non-profit organization, or an agency setting. Recruitment & Campaign Mastery: Proven track record of developing and executing multi-channel marketing campaigns (digital, social, print, and web) that influence audience behavior and achieve concrete student enrollment or audience acquisition goals. Scholarly Translation Skills: Demonstrated ability to comprehend complex academic information, graduate-level research, and progressive scholarly theories, translating them into accessible, inspiring narratives for prospective students and influencers. Collaborative Navigation: Exceptional interpersonal skills with the proven ability to build relationships and navigate a matrixed, shared-governance environment, earning consensus among academic deans, faculty, administrative staff, and central creative professionals. Communication Excellence: Persuasive, nuanced, and clear oral, written, and presentation skills. Technical Familiarity: Competency utilizing current marketing tools, presentation platforms (PowerPoint, Google Slides), and familiarity with user experience (UX) principles, SEO, and digital analytics platforms. \u00a0 WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $98,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Assistant-General-Counsel_JR109614": {"title": "Assistant General Counsel", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-05\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately 8,300 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Reporting to the General Counsel, the Assistant General Counsel will be a transactional generalist with primary responsibility for overseeing the university-wide contract management process. The position will provide strategic legal advice and support on a broad array of legal matters including compliance, risk management, real estate, international programs, intellectual property, privacy, information security, research administration, and university governance.\u00a0Must have strong experience in one or more areas and be eager to develop expertise in other areas.\u00a0This is an excellent opportunity for someone committed to becoming, or developing as, a higher education attorney and interested in being part of a dynamic university community. We recognize the potential of diverse legal experiences and encourage candidates who may not fulfill every criterion to apply. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee the university-wide contract management process, including drafting, reviewing, editing, and negotiating a broad spectrum of commercial agreements; continue to refine and improve university contracting procedures and templates. Serve as lead counsel and policy architect for the Office of the General Counsel on institutional policy development and compliance oversight; coordinate with university leadership to ensure clear, current, and enforceable institutional policies. Advise on legal aspects of international agreements and programs, including institutional MOUs, academic partnerships, and faculty/student mobility arrangements. Manage or assist with legal support for real estate transactions, leases, and facilities-related matters. Oversee or coordinate legal issues involving grants and sponsored research, including compliance with federal requirements. Partner effectively across the University (e.g., Provost\u2019s Office, Finance, Research, Facilities) to empower colleagues to achieve goals while managing risk and acting in the best interests of the university. Perform all job responsibilities with accuracy, efficiency, and high quality. Conduct legal research and analysis and draft legal memoranda. Remain current on higher education legal developments at the federal, state, and local levels. QUALIFICATIONS Undergraduate degree; law degree from an ABA-accredited institution; current admission to practice law in good standing in the State of New York. 5+ years of broad-based contracting agreement experience preferably in a university setting, or a combination of law firm and other in-house experience; experience or interest in one or more of the other practice areas described above. Familiarity with a wide range of legal issues, especially those pertaining to commercial contracts, but preferably also including information security, intellectual property, sponsored research and privacy. Diverse contract management experience preferably in a higher education setting, or a combination of law firm and other in-house experience; exposure to a range of other legal matters. Ability to manage a high volume and array of contractual agreements from routine to complex; adapt quickly to changing priorities in a dynamic environment. Familiarity with a broad range of legal matters; strong experience in reviewing, drafting, negotiating, and editing commercial contracts. A zealous commitment to higher education and a comprehensive understanding of the intricacies that universities encounter in the legal realm. Demonstrated ability to conduct thorough and detailed legal research and analysis; ability to draft well-written legal memoranda. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $160,000 - $185,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-Metal-Shop-Student-Assistant_JR109613": {"title": "E4 Metal Shop Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them.\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures. Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls/emails professionally and courteously.\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Help students check out appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing/recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.\u00a0 Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay, and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned.\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time-management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail.\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. WORK MODE : On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Assistant-Controller_JR108992": {"title": "Assistant Controller", "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 8,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. \u00a0 The Assistant Controller manages a team of staff responsible for the maintenance of the University\u2019s financial records. The Assistant Controller supervises the Accounting Director, Senior Accountant, Senior Accounting Clerk, and Junior Accountant. This position reports to the Assistant Vice President and Controller to ensure the accurate and timely closing of the general ledger and the successful completion of the annual financial statement, retirement plan, and financial aid audits. This position also works very closely with the Financial Reporting Director to ensure that the information in the university\u2019s internal and external financial reports are complete, accurate and issued on a timely basis. The Assistant Controller plays a pivotal role in supporting the financial integrity and operational efficiency of the University\u2019s finance department. Reporting to the Assistant Vice President and Controller, this position ensures compliance with institutional policies, regulatory standards, and generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP), while fostering a culture of transparency and fiscal responsibility. PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES \u00a0 Leadership & Strategic Oversight Oversee and monitor the goals and priorities of the department and promote staff development. Work with the AVP Controller on the development, codification, documentation, implementation, and training of staff on accounting policies and procedures. Special projects as identified. \u00a0 Financial Reporting & Analysis Facilitate the timely and complete monthly close of accounting records and the preparation of monthly and quarterly reports. Analyze and review monthly, quarterly, and year-end account reconciliations, and ensure the timely and accurate preparation of mid-year and year-end financial statements, including footnote disclosures. Distinguish, investigate, and explain financial statement variances against the prior year and the budget in consultation with other responsible departments and the budget office as needed. Collaborate with the Financial Reporting Director and the Assistant Vice President, FP&A and Strategic Initiatives to prepare a quarterly financial report that includes actual year-to-date amounts, projections for the full year with a comparison to the budget and the prior year, and explanations of significant variances. Identify financial statement trends and assist in identifying and implementing appropriate internal controls and best practices for accounting processes and systems in a higher education/not-for-profit environment. \u00a0 Accounting Operations & Compliance Maintain the Workday Finance General Ledger, including the maintenance of the chart of accounts, journal entry review, and all general ledger feeds from other University systems. Ensure compliance with generally accepted accounting principles and coordinate with other university departments regarding timely fiscal close processes. Aid in the design and maintenance of financial systems to support the related policies and procedures, particularly as they relate to segregation of duties. Certify that all contract revenue is appropriately recognized in accordance with revenue recognition principles. \u00a0 Audit & Regulatory Reporting Work with the Financial Reporting Director to complete the Single Audit, 403(b) audit, and financial statement audit, as well as the accurate and timely preparation of IRS Forms 990 and 990-T, IPEDS and Middle States reports, FISAP, and PCI compliance reports. Receivables & Revenue Management Lead and develop the regular monitoring of all receivables; oversee the preparation of the student accounts receivable aging and a schedule of all other receivables (contracts, leases, etc.) on a monthly basis. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor\u2019s degree in Accounting. Minimum of 10 years of progressively responsible experience including five years of experience in successfully managing staff. Higher Education or not-for-profit accounting experience, including state and federal laws and regulations. Experience successfully overseeing financial statement and other audits. Proficient in computer applications (Excel, Word, PowerPoint etc.). Certified Public Accountant (CPA Required) Experience with Workday. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. \u00a0 Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $135,000 - $145,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: \u00a0 Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) \u00a0 Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan \u00a0 Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Work mode & Telework Policy (if applicable) \u00a0 Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents \u00a0 Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---79-Fifth-Avenue/Research-Associate--Environmental-Justice-Fellowship---EJ-Disrupt-Design-and-Action-Based-Research_JR109525": {"title": "Research Associate: Environmental Justice Fellowship - EJ Disrupt Design and Action-Based Research", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nJoin us at the Tishman Environment and Design Center (Tishman Center) for an exciting student opportunity to work alongside climate and environmental justice leaders and organizations as they scale transformative projects and proposals to advance climate and environmental justice solutions within their communities. The Tishman Center is a collaborative community of practice that leverages research, policy, and design in accordance with the Jemez Principles for Democratic Organizing. We bring together research and action to tackle the root causes of climate and environmental injustice and commit to change in higher education practices within and beyond The New School. We envision a future of environmental justice- a world that is free from racism, in balance with nature, with care and respect for all beings. The Tishman Center\u2019s recently relaunched a one-year Environmental Justice Fellowship program (EJ Disrupt Design) focuses on implementing climate solutions in key regions. Each year, the program will select Fellows from across 2-3 regions with tested climate solutions, established regional relationships, and a track record of success for an opportunity to receive intensive support to accelerate the implementation of their solutions. Fellows will be supported by a design curriculum, technical accompaniment, coaching, and prototype funds and the Research Associate role will be part of that accompaniment and support.\u00a0 Research Associate selected for these positions will be assigned a diverse range of research and supporting tasks, for fellowship projects that will be developed with oversight from the Fellows and the Tishman Center staff. The RAs may accompany the work of regional Fellows throughout the year-long experience and also provide support to various team members at the Tishman Center in their efforts to support the Fellowship program as well. Research Associates should be available for anywhere from five to fifteen hours per week, averaging ten hours per week over the course of the year. Roles last a year, with an option to extend for an additional year.\u00a0\u00a0 When not working with Fellows, the Tishman Center is also looking for Research Associates to support research projects for its action-based research team, which provides technical assistance to grassroots organizations in a few key areas, including: exposing climate \"solutions\" that perpetuate environmental racism, catalyzing public policy to center climate and environmental justice, addressing disparities in resourcing and investment practices, and holding the government accountable for its environmental justice commitments. The Research Associate would assist with qualitative and quantitative information gathering (literature reviews, surveys, interviews), investigation into policies and activities relevant to environmental justice, writing and communication of findings, creation of materials to socialize outputs such as one-pagers or presentations, planning of dissemination activities, and organizing of topical or skills-based workshops. Other research operations and center-wide administrative tasks may also be requested. CORE CAPABILITIES & QUALIFICATIONS\u00a0 Research Associates for these roles should possess at least one of the following, but need not come with experience in all of these areas.\u00a0 Knowledge and/or experience working with climate and environmental justice organizations and communities. Alternatively, Interest and/or experience working with community based organizations and social movement organizations.\u00a0 Capacity for collegiate-level research and writing related to climate and environmental justice topics and social movements or participatory research and design.\u00a0 Understanding of spatial and environmental Design and/or capacity for using geospatial data with design, including using ArcGIS or QGIS to manage, analyze, and display location data. Experience and capacity with respect to Communications support for grassroots organizations and social movements, including writing and graphic design for social media assets, blogs, and newsletters. Final candidates will need to provide either writing, design or research samples.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Coursework in climate and environmental justice, especially Environmental Justice: Movements, Theories and Frontline Practices with Dr. Ana Baptista \u00a0 Commitment to the Jemez Principles and environmental justice principles: ability to listen deeply to, provide support to, and follow the lead of frontline and community based leaders\u00a0 Demonstrated solutions-oriented approach and high emotional intelligence Proven track record of strong project coordination and communication skills, including attention to detail, ability to effectively prioritize tasks with little supervision, and work independently to accomplish assignments and projects Comfortable engaging in an emergent, collaborative work-style with multiple partners, staff, and contributors WORK MODE This position is hybrid but will require on-campus participation at times in order to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. The role will also potentially include opportunities for travel, though travel is not required.\u00a0\u00a0 HOURLY RATE: $ 37.00/hr Responsibilities: Research Associates selected for these positions will be assigned a diverse range of research and supporting tasks, for fellowship projects that will be developed with oversight from the Fellows and the Tishman Center staff. The RAs may accompany the work of regional Fellows throughout the year-long experience and also provide support to various team members at the Tishman Center in their efforts to support the Fellowship program as well We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Lang-Lab-Assistant_JR109592": {"title": "Lang Lab Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant will provide essential support to the computer lab by ensuring the lab's smooth operation and readiness for student, faculty, and staff use. This role requires a proactive and responsible individual who can perform tasks independently while effectively communicating with lab users and the administration team. Key Responsibilities: Ensure all lab equipment is functional, including computers, printers, and other devices. Monitor and restock supplies such as paper, toner, and other materials as needed. Assist lab users with technical issues or general inquiries. Communicate equipment malfunctions, supply shortages, or other concerns to the Associate Director of Administration at Lang and IT support. Complete tasks independently, ensuring the lab is well-maintained and operational at all times. Qualifications: Ability to work independently and manage time efficiently. Strong communication skills to relay issues and requests to management and IT support. Basic understanding of technical equipment and willingness to learn troubleshooting processes. Punctual and reliable, with a proactive approach to resolving issues in the lab. Workmode : Onsite #LI-DNI Hourly rate: $19 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant supports the smooth and efficient operation of the computer lab by ensuring equipment is functional, supplies are adequately stocked, and the space is well-maintained. The role also involves assisting lab users with basic technical issues, communicating concerns to administration and IT support, and independently completing tasks in a reliable and proactive manner. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-General-Student-Assistant-OCSE_JR109588": {"title": "E4 General Student Assistant OCSE", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.\u00a0 Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate :\u00a0$17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant---Making-Center_JR109455-1": {"title": "Student Assistant - Making Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Textile and MFA FDS department within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIE S Support Textiles Making center facilities. Primary duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly. Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Overseeing and updating inventory and utilizing web checkout systems daily Domestic and industrial sewing machines, and ironing stations Daily Overseeing dye lab, Machine knitting lab, J3 sewing lab, and/or J3 textile sewing lab Floor looms, felting station, donation closets (In JJ1 Textiles Studios) Check students in/out of the space using Webcheckout (In JJ1 Textiles Studios) Check tools in/out in Webcheckout Maintain Silver Reed and Dubied Knitting Machines\u00a0 Answer basic questions about equipment and lab policies Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings \u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends For Machine Knitting support positions, completion of a machine knitting class or other previous machine knitting experience is preferred. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests, assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0 and complete special projects as assigned We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Technician--Print-Media_JR109532": {"title": "Technician, Print Media", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons, Mannes, Jazz, and Drama, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician in Print Media for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces, including a self-service digital printing space for: Laser, Inkjet, Plotters & Risographs, along with book-making, binding, trimming, and finishing equipment, and service-based specialty printing on: UV, Vinyl Cutting, Solvent & Dye pigment printing. This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters Professional union contract, that reports to the Facilities Manager, Print Media. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned and maintain a clean and safe working environment. Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff. Support students in their use of various printing and book-binding equipment, including laser printers, inkjet printers, inkjet plotters, risographs, and various mechanical book-binding machines. Independently output UV & Dye-Sublimation prints, along with vinyl projects submitted by students, faculty, and staff through our service bureau.\u00a0 Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, and continuous operation of a variety of hardware/software technologies and stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Support faculty and students Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment Make hardware/software purchases and process recommendations Create and update web page content Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff, through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, recordkeeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. Oversee tool and equipment checkout for both in-shop/lab use and off-site special requests. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special Making Center, program, school, or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; adjust approach and prioritize competing demands as necessary. Ability to be proactive in addressing facility needs, including but not limited to maintaining organized workspaces, tracking inventory locations, and monitoring Technician communication networks. Properly handle heavy paper/vinyl rolls and safely use ladders and other hardware to support facility needs. Acquire working knowledge of UV printers, solvent printers, dye pigment printers, and vinyl cutting, within the first 6 months of employment. Stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Upon hiring, completion of university-required staff trainings and necessary Certificate of Fitness for all Making Center Facilities. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule \u2013 ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. Become proficient in the current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Technical knowledge of various laser printers, inkjet printers, inkjet plotters, risographs, various mechanical book binding machines, hand book binding, and cold & hot lamination. Technical knowledge of independent and/or commercial publishing processes and practices. Technical knowledge of multi-page layout software and digital printing output for books. Full comprehensive experience running, maintaining, repairing, and troubleshooting necessary equipment/tools. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment, with a working knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployment, network systems, printer drivers, and color calibration/profiling. Familiarity with the Google Suite and Adobe Creative Suite (Photoshop, InDesign, Illustrator). Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. Experience in handling high-traffic customer service. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs. Knowledge of Google Calendar, or comparable equipment finance management and scheduling systems. Familiarity with pay-for-print and e-commerce platforms such as Paper Cut and Touchnet. Awareness of zine/booklet publishing techniques. Knowledge and interest in combining digital and analog printmaking processes. Knowledge of digital printer repair and maintenance. Experience with various print and booklet finishing options. Experience with running group workshops. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * For full consideration, please submit a resume and cover letter in your application. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Technician--Wood---Metal_JR109581-2": {"title": "Technician, Wood & Metal", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a09,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons, Mannes, Jazz, and Drama, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician in Wood and Metal for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, supporting experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces including, but not limited to, the N2 Wood Shop and associated open work areas, tool checkouts, material store, and storage, as well as the E4 Wood and Metal Shops and Wet Shops. This is an administrative position governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters union contract, reporting to the Assistant Manager and Facilities Manager of the N2 Woodshop.\u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned and maintain a clean and safe working environment.\u00a0 Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Work with other Making Center staff on troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, continuous operation, and support for a variety of hardware/software and technologies. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Support faculty and students. Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment. Make hardware/software purchase and process recommendations. Create and update web page content. Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours. Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff, through peer-to-peer training and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record keeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas Oversee daily operations of the Making Center facilities, including healthy, safe, and respectful making practices. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. Oversee tool and equipment checkout for both in-shop/lab use and off-site special requests. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; adjust approach and prioritize competing demands as necessary. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment with productive and positive workplace relationships. Stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Complete university-required staff training and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Become proficient in Web Check Out or the current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by the supervisor. QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. Knowledge of a variety of production and fabrication techniques; be familiar with practices related to current design and art practice including: fabrication, methodology, and processes; experience and knowledge of working with a wide variety of materials. Technical knowledge of wood and metal-based practices in all or some of the following areas: furniture making, sculpture (including ceramics and casting), product prototyping, model making, millwork, hot metal working, cold metal working, jewelry and non-ferrous metalworking, cold bending and steam bending wood, architectural or art installation. Full comprehensive experience in maintaining, repairing, and troubleshooting equipment/tools. Ability to work safely with and demonstrate proper use of a wide array of hand tools, power tools, and machinery. Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs, and on occasion, may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Basic familiarity with ceramic and casting processes, including plaster casting, handbuilding, wheel throwing, and ceramic firing processes. Familiarity with any of the following is a plus: CNC/digital fabrication equipment, CAD programs, vacuum forming, physical computing, finishes, plastics, foams, and/or fabrics. Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment.\u00a0 Experience with large-scale hardware and software deployments. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 For full consideration, please include a Resume and Cover Letter in your application. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Technician--Digital-Tools_JR109580": {"title": "Technician, Digital Tools", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons, Mannes, Jazz, and Drama, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician in Digital Tools for the Making Center . Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces including the Laser Lab, 3D Lab, CNC Machine Shops, Physical Computing areas, and open work areas.\u00a0 This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters union contract, that reports to the Managers of Digital Tools. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned and maintain a clean and safe working environment.\u00a0 Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Support students in their use of various Digital Fabrication tools, including: laser cutting, 3D printing, CNC milling, and digital prototyping. Independently output 3d prints, along with CNC milling projects submitted by students, faculty, and staff through our service bureau. Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, and continuous operation of a variety of hardware/software technologies and stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Support faculty and students. Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment. Make hardware/software purchases and process recommendations. Create and update web page content. Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours. Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff, through peer-to-peer training and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record keeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. B ecome proficient in Web Check Out or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special Making Center, program, school, or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; meet deadlines and anticipate facility and checkout center needs, adjusting approach and prioritizing competing demands.\u00a0 Completion of university-required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. Experience with various production and digital fabrication techniques; be familiar with practices related to current design and art practice - including fabrication, methodology, and processes; experience and knowledge of working with a wide variety of materials. Strong technical knowledge of additive and subtractive fabrication workflows including, but not limited to, setup, protocols, maintenance, and output, and experience maintaining, repairing, and troubleshooting equipment/tools as relevant to the position. Ability to work safely with, and demonstrate proper use of digital fabrication tools including laser cutters, 3D printers, and CNC routers/mills. Proficiency with CAD/CAM modeling/machining languages and 2D and 3D software such as Rhino, Solidworks, Maya, Fusion360, AutoCAD, Adobe Creative Cloud, and Google Drive, and an understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. Become proficient in Web Check Out or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and, on occasion, may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with any of the following: woodworking, metalworking, furniture making, millwork, architectural or art installations, vacuum forming, ceramics, casting, finishes, plastics, foams, textiles, and fabrics. Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems.\u00a0 Experience with large-scale hardware and software deployments. Experience with mesh repair, rendering tools, and other advanced 3D modeling and finishing processes. Experience designing for and operating analog machining equipment. Understanding of and experience working with electronics, physical computing, and technology design and their applications associated with the operation and maintenance of fabrication equipment and systems. Experience with digital media hardware associated with 3D design for both on-screen and physical applications including 3D scanning, motion capture, VR/AR, and other related input/output devices. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Please submit a resume and cover letter in your application.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Teaching-Fellowship-26-27-AY_JR108507": {"title": "Teaching Fellowship 26-27 AY", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nWorking under the supervision of the chairs of the departments or programs in which they teach, Teaching Fellows are the instructors of record for their courses and assume independent responsibility for these classes. They may also participate in college faculty orientations. Teaching Fellows may lead courses within their own disciplines or in related, interdisciplinary subjects. They may also teach courses that are intended to develop research skills and other proficiencies, such as mathematics and statistical methods. Please see the Provost\u2019s Office\u2019s Teaching Fellowship webpage for information about the specific teaching positions that are available and other useful information. While the Provost\u2019s Office coordinates the application process for Teaching Fellows, the departments and programs that sponsor these courses are responsible for making all hiring decisions. \u200b \u200b Before applying, carefully read the information below and review the TF Opportunities document . On the online application, you can indicate your preference for courses that require TFs. \u00a0 Please note: Graduate students may hold only one Teaching Fellowship per semester. Teaching Fellows are permitted to have additional appointments as Teaching, Research, or Course Assistants, but their combined hours of work must be consistent with the Policy on Maximum Student Employmen t.\u00a0 The application and selection process for Teaching Assistants takes place in the spring semester. Minimum Eligibility Requirements:\u00a0 Applicants must meet the following minimum requirements for appointment by the time of application. Please note: Some departments have additional required and/or preferred qualifications (these will be posted when the TF application is made available in the current application cycle). Teaching Fellowships are highly selective, and meeting the minimum stated requirements does not guarantee selection. Students in programs that offer doctoral degrees at The New School must be accepted into a doctoral degree program at the time of application. Students must be matriculated (i.e., enrolled in courses or maintaining status) in the semester(s) in which they will be Teaching Fellows. The chair or director of the hiring department or program may set additional preferred qualifications for Teaching Fellows (e.g., teaching experience or previous conferral of a master\u2019s degree). Students may not have a grade of \"incomplete\" for more than one third of their courses. Students must have achieved a minimum cumulative GPA of 3.4. Students must be within the time limit for attaining their degree. Preferred Qualifications The chair or director of the hiring department or program may set additional required or preferred qualifications for Teaching Fellows\u00a0 (e.g., prior teaching experience or previous conferral of a master\u2019s degree). A list of such requirements can be found here . We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Additional questions should be sent to gradstudentteaching@newschool.edu. For answers to the most commonly asked questions, please consult our FAQ and also refer to the CBA for the Academic Student Worker\u2019s union on our Labor Relations website. Work Mode: In person #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $59.49 Responsibilities: Duties as a Teaching Fellow: Working under the supervision of the chairs of the departments or programs in which they teach, Teaching Fellows are the instructors of record for their courses and assume independent responsibility for these classes. They may also participate in college faculty orientations. Teaching Fellows may lead courses within their own disciplines or in related, interdisciplinary subjects. They may also teach courses that are intended to develop research skills and other proficiencies, such as mathematics and statistical methods. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/DJ-Skills---Styles---Student-Assistant_JR109585": {"title": "DJ Skills & Styles - Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nTo be an assistant for DJ Skills & Styles student workers will need to assist with the setup and return of all dj coffins and speakers at the start of the first section of class and end of the last section of class. The attributes of a successful candidate are someone who is on time to work and somewhat knowledgeable about the ins and outs of the dj equipment. A successful candidate will impact the university in a positive way by showing professionalism to their fellow classmates in the form of efficiency and conduct. RESPONSIBILITIES A Student assistant in DJ Skills & Styles will be required to do the following Remove dj coffins and speakers from their lockers and set them up on the classroom tables Plug the power from the classroom tables to the wall outlets Check the gear to ensure it is ready for class Report irregularities and damage of the dj gear to the professor for repair and/or replacement Return dj coffins and speakers to their lockers Unplug the power cables for the classroom tables and stow them out of the way of foot traffic MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS To be a student assistant an employee would need the following qualifications Currently enrolled as a student at The New School Ability to lift dj coffins from lockers to classroom tables and back to lockers Understand basic signal routing to provide power to the dj stations and speakers PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Knowledge of the dj gear and software (Serato) is encouraged though not a strict requirement WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17.00-$19.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The DJ Skills & Styles Student Assistant supports the setup and breakdown of DJ coffins, speakers, and related equipment before and after class sessions. Responsibilities include connecting power, checking equipment, reporting damage or issues to the professor, and safely returning and storing all equipment. Candidates must be currently enrolled students who can safely lift the equipment and understand basic signal routing; familiarity with DJ equipment and Serato is preferred. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Event-Assistant---Lang-Arts_JR109586": {"title": "Student Event Assistant - Lang Arts", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nThe New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education. We are seeking student employees to support Lang Arts and large scale productions in various roles. Working some evenings and weekends is required as a portion of Lang Events take place in the evening. RESPONSIBILITIES In-person day-of event support Checking in attendees using Splash event registration tool (app) Other assigned duties to support Lang Arts productions/events, such as assisting Lang Arts staff/Stage Manager with the transition between student performance pieces MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated New School student Organizational skills Ability to occasionally carry/move items 40+ lbs Available up to 5 hours per week PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Previous experience in a theater production/arts space Attentive to detail Federal Work-Study Awardee preferred WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $19 an hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Students will assist in various support roles for Lang Arts live events including but not limited to running guest check-in utilizing Splash, ushering attendees to their seats, and assisting the production stage manager with performance transitions and costuming. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Senior-Student-Assistant-Specialist---2nd-Year-Psychology-Extern_JR109584": {"title": "Senior Student Assistant Specialist - 2nd Year Psychology Extern", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nPart time\n\nThe mission of Student Health Services is to promote the health and well-being of students to foster their learning and success. Reporting to the Associate Director of Clinical Training Programs, the 2nd Year Psychology Externs will work under supervision to provide services to New School Students at the Student Health Services Counseling Center for the academic year. This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. Students completing the full-time (15 hour/ week) externship will receive a stipend of $12,000. RESPONSIBILITIES Provide individual psychotherapy to the New School student body. Provide crisis care. Conduct assessments and triage visits to determine appropriate levels of care. Provide psychotherapy and psychiatry referrals for the New School student body as needed. Work collaboratively with all Student Success offices, including but not limited to Student Support and Advocacy, Student Conduct and Community Standards, Student Disability Services, Student Success Advisors, the Office of Academic Standards, among others. Provide group psychotherapy to the New School student body as needed. Complete confidential written clinical notes and reports in a timely fashion. Communicate to supervisors any clinical or interprofessional problems that may occur. Maintain confidentiality according to HIPAA laws. \u00a0 QUALIFICATIONS New School PhD Psychology Candidate in their 2nd year in good standing. \u00a0 WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. \u00a0 Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE Students completing the full-time (15 hour/ week) externship will receive a stipend of\u00a0$12,000. The employment period is 40 weeks, with two weeks paid vacation will be worked into the payment schedule, for holidays and spring break. As these positions are not research-related or teaching assistant work, they are not subject to SENS-UAW guidelines. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: 2nd Year Psychology Externs will work under supervision to provide services to New School Students at the Student Health Services Counseling Center for the academic year. This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Project-Manager-Graduate-Assistant-for-Admission-Communications_JR109576": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Project Manager/Graduate Assistant for Admission Communications", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nPart time\n\nThe New School is looking for an enthusiastic, responsible, creative individual to join our Admission Communications team as a Content Team Project Manager. This position is hybrid, with required on-campus content creation and mandatory monthly staff meetings. This position is an important, integral part of Admission Communications and Welcome Center work, and essential to the success of Enrollment Management\u2019s mission. Our Content Team members connect with and create content for prospective students, applicants, parents, and families on Instagram, ZeeMee, CampusESP, and other communications platforms. They also engage with our audiences at in-person and online events. Importantly, individual Content Creators work independently and collaboratively, contributing to a positive, enjoyable work environment. As a member of the Content Team, the Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will operate in a student administrative leadership and creative position, managing the work of the Content Team, preparing assignments, reviewing assignments, and managing workflows in Slack workspace. The Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will also build blog posts and communications within CampusESP parent and family communications portal, and provide writing guidance to the Content Team as they write blog posts. The Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will also assign content work and scheduling in ZeeMee, our social community space for prospective students and applicants. They will also assist in scheduling team meetings and updating the monthly shift schedule. The Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will contribute to content creation and complete additional assignments as needed. *Responsibilities: Successful candidates will: Operate in a student administrative leadership and creative position, managing the work of the Content Team, preparing assignments, reviewing assignments, and managing workflows in Slack workspace Manage the content team\u2019s assignments, ensuring adherence to deadlines and accessibility standards Create content briefs to ensure consistency from project to project Assign content work and scheduling in ZeeMee, our social community space for prospective students and applicants Assist in scheduling team meetings and updating the monthly shift schedule Manage work within Trello and Slack workspace Serve as part of the student administrative management team, working independently and collaboratively with other managers Provide leadership guidance to student content creators, ensuring content meets deadlines and is produced according to best practices Provide updates and platform information and statistics to Welcome Center full time administrative employees, serving as knowledge holders of current trends and use by the incoming class Build blog posts and communications within CampusESP parent and family communications portal Provide writing guidance to the Content Team as they write blog posts Assist in editing and producing multimedia content for different content platforms, including Reels, Stories, and other formats, from video clips provided by Content Creators and Welcome Center administrators as needed Direct, film, edit, and produce multimedia content for our different content platforms, online events, and as needed Assist in collecting and organizing photos provided by team members for Instagram carousel posts as needed Research trends and suggest opportunities for use on our social platforms Learn, apply, and educate team members in inclusive and accessible practices in content creation Meet all content deadlines, attend all content team meetings, and represent Admission Communications at in-person and online events Check in daily with our content team and Welcome Center professional administrators on our Slack workspace Be comfortable in front of the camera Create dynamic, authentic, approachable multi-media content for our communications platforms that provides an honest view and personal insight into student life Learn and understand our different audiences and platforms, including prospective students, applicants, parents/families, Instagram, ZeeMee, and CampusESP Enjoy connecting with and assisting our audiences, answering questions about student life and admission processes, and providing support Enjoy collaborating with professional staff and other student team members Contribute to a friendly, fun working environment Be a positive, dependable, professional resource for our team Complete other content creation and review duties as assigned Manage their own time Commit to working 9 hours per week Understand this is job position is an important, integral part of Admission Communications and Welcome Center work, and essential to the success of Enrollment Management\u2019s mission *Minimum Qualifications:\u00a0 Applicants must be currently enrolled in a degree program at The New School. Additional qualifications include: Familiarity with and enthusiasm for social network sites, social media tools, and associated apps and technology Excellent writing and editing skills Strong video editing skills with desire to learn and troubleshoot challenges Impeccable attention to detail and aesthetics Sound judgment Positive attitude Excellent communicator Highly organized--enjoys organization Self-motivated\u00a0 Enjoys assisting others and providing support Able to focus without being distracted by cell phones, personal social media, and competing responsibilities Familiarity with Instagram, Zeemee, and CampusESP Familiarity with Google applications including Sheets, Docs, Forms, and Slides Experience with Premiere Pro and Canva video, photo, and design capabilities Experience with Adobe Photoshop, Illustrator, and InDesign *Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $20 per hour\u00a0 *Work Mode:\u00a0 Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI *Required Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/CV Cover Letter Portfolio Link Special Instructions to Applicants Share a link to a portfolio of relevant work. Your portfolio can include website or email design, social media strategy and design, graphic design, media editing, and/or writing samples. Your portfolio can be a website, a collection of work on a Google Doc, or other format convenient for you. Applications without work samples will not be considered. Applications without a cover letter, resume, and work samples will not be considered. Candidates considered for this role will be asked to complete an assessment exercise. Finalists may be asked to provide professional references. Additional Documents (if applicable) Please upload your Federal Work Study Award letter if you have one. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: As a member of the Content Team, the Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will operate in a student administrative leadership and creative position, managing the work of the Content Team, preparing assignments, reviewing assignments, and managing workflows in Slack workspace. The Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will also build blog posts and communications within CampusESP parent and family communications portal, and provide writing guidance to the Content Team as they write blog posts. The Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will also assign content work and scheduling in ZeeMee, our social community space for prospective students and applicants. They will also assist in scheduling team meetings and updating the monthly shift schedule. The Content Team Project Manager/Graduate Assistant will contribute to content creation and complete additional assignments as needed. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---79-Fifth-Avenue/Path-Student-Assistant-Specialist---FWS_JR109579": {"title": "Path Student Assistant Specialist - FWS", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Reporting to the Senior HR Strategy Partner, the Student Assistant Specialist supports the team by assisting in responding to inquiries and assisting with hiring and I-9 verifications.\u00a0 This position provides prompt responses to general inquiries via the HR inbox and connects employees with resources or other HR colleagues for specialized assistance. This position provides a high level of customer service and professionalism in all interactions.\u00a0\u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES AND PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Student Hire Processing & Onboarding Review student hiring submissions for accuracy and completeness. Track outstanding onboarding requirements and proactively follow up on missing documentation. Maintain clear, consistent communication with hiring managers and campus partners regarding onboarding statuses and potential start-date delays. Identify and escalate compliance or onboarding bottlenecks to the appropriate HR team members. I-9 & Verification Compliance\u00a0 Assist with the completion of section two of the Form I-9 employment eligibility verification. Update and monitor the I-9 Appointment log and associated communications to new hires.\u00a0 Customer Service & Support:\u00a0\u00a0 Monitor multiple HR inboxes and associated responsibilities, resolve general inquiries with high professionalism, and escalate complex requests to specialized HR colleagues as needed. Assist with request form entry MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS One to 2 years of related professional-level work experience; OR equivalent work experience\u00a0 Effective written communication and customer service skills\u00a0 General working knowledge of HR policies, procedures, and practices\u00a0 Demonstrated ability to effectively balance multiple tasks and inquiries Federal Work Study Award PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience in higher education or a student employment office\u00a0 Basic familiarization or direct experience with Form I-9 completion (Section 2 verification), employment eligibility rules, and confidential record-keeping. WORK MODE : HYBRID #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17-21 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Reporting to the Senior HR Strategy Partner, the Student Assistant Specialist supports the team by assisting in responding to inquiries and assisting with hiring and I-9 verifications.\u00a0 This position provides prompt responses to general inquiries via the HR inbox and connects employees with resources or other HR colleagues for specialized assistance. This position provides a high level of customer service and professionalism in all interactions.\u00a0\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/Social-Media-Student-Assistant_JR109569": {"title": "Social Media Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a09,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. RESPONSIBILITIES Content Creation: Utilize a strong, creative, and media-savvy skillset to craft social media content for SCE\u2019s Instagram accounts, with plans to start a LinkedIn presence. Multimedia Production: Shoot and edit photo and video content for social media use, with a particular emphasis on short-form video. Graphic Design: Design graphic assets for use on social media, blogs, and newsletters. Copywriting: Write compelling, concise copy that follows marketing best practices and branding guidelines. Collaboration: Work with students, faculty, and staff to gather and create content, and liaise with students and alumni for takeovers and other content. Event Coverage: Attend school and program events to capture and create social media content. Community Engagement: Monitor social accounts, including tags, mentions, and direct messages, interacting with both the SCE community as well as broader external audiences (e.g., prospective students and their families, alumni, etc.). Research: Proactively research news items, events, and other content ideas to promote and celebrate the SCE community. Strategy Development: Think creatively about social media strategy and develop a social media schedule. Team Meetings: Participate in regular meetings with the program's social media team. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Interest and Knowledge: Strong interest in social media, storytelling, and community building; general interest in design and the built environment is encouraged. Multimedia Skills: Photography and/or videography experience. Design Skills: Graphic design experience (Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, etc.). Writing Skills: Strong copywriting and editing skills. Communication and Teamwork: Strong communicator and team player. Proactivity and Independence: Enthusiastic, proactive, and independent. Trend Awareness: Knowledge of the latest digital trends. Time Management: Skilled at executing multiple projects simultaneously with efficient time management and exceptional attention to detail. Feedback Responsiveness: Responsive to feedback. Appreciation: Appreciation for arts, design, student life, and higher education. Availability: Available during office hours, with occasional evening hours while attending events. Knowledge of SCE: Familiarity with SCE\u2019s disciplines is a plus but not required. Comfort on Camera: Comfortable on camera is a plus. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. This position will require 2 days of on campus work. There will be times that this position can be remote however, this will be discussed as projects come in. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $18/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The responsibilities for this role are, however not limited to content creation, multimedia production in addition to graphic design and copy editing. Additionally it's important to be available for events across campus to assist in promotion. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Digital-Content---Student-Assistant-Content-Creator_JR109521": {"title": "Digital Content \u2013 Student Assistant Content Creator", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Attention all digital storytellers: photographers, videographers, editors, and graphic artists! Do you enjoy working with digital technologies?\u00a0 Are you an organized, detail oriented, multi-tasker? Read on! The New School\u2019s Marketing and Communication (M&C) seeks a multifaceted student to assist the Digital Content and Engagement team. At Digital Content, we are responsible for coordinating, managing, and producing video and photography assets to promote the entire university. The student assistant will work under the supervision of the Senior Digital Content Producer in the primary functions of filming and editing content (video and photo) as well as assisting in the management and distribution of different media assets. The Digital Content Student Assistant Content Creator will have the opportunity to work on creative projects in photo and video while acquiring in-demand real-world skills of digital asset management and production. The right candidate will have a strong sense of taking initiative and is eager to learn new skills. The Digital Content Student Assistant Content Creator will work out a schedule with the Senior Digital Content Producer that is conducive to both, but one that could be flexible for fewer or more hours depending on academic calendar and need. Applicants should anticipate working up to 20 hours per week during the academic year. Applicants need to maintain a reasonable but dedicated schedule and preferably have the ability to commit to at least 1 year in the position. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Assist on all photo and video shoots for M&C Collaborate with all M&C departments and act as a creative partner\u00a0 Assist the in-house team and outside vendors in producing video and photo content. Assist with the digital processing of photography and video release forms. Assist with the intake and cataloging of assets to the Digital Asset Library Curate and manage collections of images for use by designers, editorial staff, and others Maintain and assign metadata for assets, incorporating industry standards to ensure accurate usage rights that meet The New School\u2019s unique requirements Other tasks as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Must be graduating in Spring 2027 or beyond. The job requires being on set & carrying equipment. Set experience required as a PA or AP. Basic experience in Camera operation. Experience recording with cellphone camera for social media. Ability to balance school and work assignments. Flexible and capable multi-tasker. Organized, precise, and detail-oriented. Communicative, cooperative, curious to learn new things, and able to take direction. A fast learner of creative software and online collaboration workflows. Proficient in working on Macintosh computers. Beginning to intermediate skill set in editing, videography, and on-set experience Commit to a typical workload of 15-20 hours per week during the school year. Experienced in data management and copywriting for branding.\u00a0 Confident in Adobe Premiere Pro (Creative Suite: photo image and graphics editing software a plus, e.g. Adobe Photoshop, Lightroom, and After Effects) Camera and sound mixing experience is a plus Experience using Sony FX3 and FX6 cameras, lavalier mics and zoom recorders.\u00a0 WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. \u00a0 HOURLY RATE $18.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 To be considered, please send a link to your website or a creative reel of work samples, a cover letter stating your intention as well as a current resume to digitalcontent@newschool.edu .\u00a0Open to Currently Enrolled New School Students ONLY. Responsibilities: The student assistant will work under the supervision of the Senior Digital Content Producer in the primary functions of filming and editing content (video and photo) as well as assisting in the management and distribution of different media assets. The Digital Content Student Assistant Content Creator will have the opportunity to work on creative projects in photo and video while acquiring in-demand real-world skills of digital asset management and production. The right candidate will have a strong sense of taking initiative and is eager to learn new skills. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/HEOP-ULC-Tutor_JR109570-1": {"title": "HEOP/ULC Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nPart time\n\nThis tutor role would support collaborative efforts between The University Learning Center (ULC) and The New School Arthur O. Eve Higher Education Opportunity Program (HEOP) to provide tailored support to students through one-on-one tutoring, skill-building workshops, and other resources. The ULC is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School\u2019s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals. HEOP is a partnership between the New York State Education Department and The New School. This program is designed for students with the potential and drive to further their education but, due to limited academic and financial resources, would not otherwise have the opportunity to attend college. The goal of HEOP is to retain and graduate talented students who will succeed at the college level and beyond. Job Responsibilities: Serve as the primary tutor for the HEOP Program, assisting students with a variety of writing projects and general academic skill-building, including presentations and time management (training provided). Writing assistance may include brainstorming topics, identifying questions students hope to answer, developing outlines, formulating and strengthening arguments, revising drafts at various stages of development, properly citing sources and understanding plagiarism avoidance, including responsible AI use, as well as cover letters, resumes, personal statements, theses, dissertations, and research proposals. There may also be opportunities to provide art and design feedback, which students may request based on the tutor\u2019s areas of expertise. In addition to working with students in the HEOP program, this role would also lead writing and time management tutoring sessions for other undergraduate and students who visit the ULC.\u00a0 Help students develop their academic skills and increase their awareness of additional university resources, connecting students to other departments when necessary.\u00a0 Develop and lead skill-building workshops and other programming and resources, including study halls and informational handouts.\u00a0 Create a professional and welcoming environment by modeling respect for students\u2019 diverse cultures, language skills, and experiences. Complete administrative tasks, such as writing brief descriptions of each session and emailing a brief summary of daily attendance. Minimum Qualifications: Tutors must be currently enrolled in a relevant program at The New School and have at least one year of tutoring experience or teaching writing/English in a formal academic setting. Must have a 3.0 GPA or higher Excellent communication skills Demonstrated reliability and punctuality Able to commit to 6 (or more) tutoring hours per week Experience working with students with ADHD a plus Preferred Qualifications: Federal Work Study preferred Experience working with ESL and first generation students strongly preferred Work Mode: In-person #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $29.20/hr through October 31 (new pay rate will be determined by next collective bargaining agreement) Special Instruction : Please send at least one faculty recommendation to learningcenter@newschool.edu . We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals. Tutors work one-on-one with undergraduate and graduate students of varying skill levels on writing, time management and general academic skills, as well as lead workshops and develop resources to support students\u2019 needs. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Student-Marketing-Model---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109546": {"title": "Student Marketing Model - Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-18\nPart time\n\nThis position is open to Matriculated New School Student Only At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. We are seeking Student Models to participate in Marketing and Communications (M&C) photoshoots and video shoots which will be used in advertising for recruitment for the Fall 2026 semester. The selected students will collaborate with The New School marketing staff and other selected students to gather useful photography and films to convey marketing stories. The photoshoots are expected to take approximately 5 hours (although some shoots may take longer), typically over the course of 1-2 days. CORE CAPABILITIES AND PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Pose for marketing photography / videography Advertise TNS products and services in marketing films. Participate in a photoshoot set up and provide input when requested. QUALIFICATIONS Current New School students in good standing and enrolled for the Spring 2026 semester Responsible and punctual, and able to meet deadlines and take direction WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. \u00a0 #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $20 per hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: We are seeking Student Models to participate in Marketing and Communications (M&C) photoshoots and videoshoots which will be used in advertising for recruitment for the Fall 2026 semester. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Lang---Lab-Assistant--Federal-Work-Study-_JR109551": {"title": "Lang - Lab Assistant (Federal Work Study)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Student Lab Assistant will provide essential support to the computer lab by ensuring the lab's smooth operation and readiness for student, faculty, and staff use. This role requires a proactive and responsible individual who can perform tasks independently while effectively communicating with lab users and the administration team. This position is exclusively available to students with Federal Work-Study (FWS) funding. RESPONSIBILITIES Ensure all lab equipment is functional, including computers, printers, and other devices. Monitor and restock supplies such as paper, toner, and other materials as needed. Assist lab users with technical issues or general inquiries. Communicate equipment malfunctions, supply shortages, or other concerns to the Associate Director of Administration at Lang and IT support. Complete tasks independently, ensuring the lab is well-maintained and operational at all times. QUALIFICATIONS Ability to work independently and manage time efficiently. Strong communication skills to relay issues and requests to management and IT support. Basic understanding of technical equipment and willingness to learn troubleshooting processes. Punctual and reliable, with a proactive approach to resolving issues in the lab. Federal Work Study Award is required. Must Be a TNS student. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RANGE $17.00 - $19.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant will support the daily operation of the computer lab by ensuring equipment is functional, supplies are stocked, and the space remains clean and ready for use. The role will also assist lab users with basic technical questions and communicate equipment issues, supply needs, and other concerns to Lang Administration and IT. The ideal candidate is reliable, proactive, organized, and able to work independently with strong communication skills. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Temporary-International-Student-Advisor_JR109534": {"title": "Temporary International Student Advisor", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Temporary International Student Advisor (ISA) for International Student and Scholar Services (ISSS) is a position that will report to the Senior Advisor for International Students and Scholars in ISSS. The International Student Advisor is being hired to support the ISSS team in key areas of processing. The ISA will also be responsible for advising students in basic immigration regulations and maintenance of their student status. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as Designated School Official (DSO). Process assigned requests from students using SEVIS, Sunapsis & Banner systems. Collaborate with the ISSS team to meet processing goals. Utilize Sunapsis, New School email, and virtual meetings to communicate with international students' requirements.\u00a0 Respond to student inquiries and requests through the ISSS general email inbox. Keep abreast of University, NAFSA, USCIS, DHS, and DOS guidance to ensure compliance. Participate in ISSS meetings. Other duties as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor\u2019s degree required. Ability to work in groups and independently.\u00a0 Strong computer skills with proficiency in Windows-based environments including Microsoft Office\u00a0applications (Word, Excel, etc.), Google Drive, Banner, Sunapsis and/or SEVIS.\u00a0 Excellent communication, organization, & customer service skills.\u00a0 Punctual & resourceful with a positive attitude.\u00a0 Must be a U.S. citizen or permanent resident and be able to serve as a Department of\u00a0 Homeland Security (DHS) Designated School Official (DSO) and a Department of State (DOS) Alternate Responsible Office (ARO).\u00a0 Ability to pass a required background check.\u00a0 Previous DSO and ARO experience. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. \u00a0 Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-DNI \u00a0 HOURLY RATE $28 per hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Brass-Area--Student-Assistant_JR109542": {"title": "Brass Area, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nThe Brass Area at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Brass Area, Student Assistant . We\u2019re looking for a creative and responsible individual to support initiatives within the Brass Area . This position reports to the Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the orchestral or classical music-related field. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with rehearsals and scheduling of Brass Department activities. Liaise and coordinate with the College of Performing Arts Production Office and Library as needed. Assist in Master Class coordination and promotion Assist with Departmental activities in coordination with Co-Chairs and as assigned by the Program & Administration Manager. Additional duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current student in the Brass area (BM, MM, or PDPL in Orchestral Instruments, with a concentration in Trumpet, Horn, Trombone, or Tuba). Previous administrative experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program Recipient of Federal Work Study award WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17.00 Candidate will work 1-2 hours per week on average (estimated 20 hours per semester) We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Assist with the scheduling, rehearsal coordination, and master class promotion for Brass Department activities while liaising with the College of Performing Arts Production Office and Library. Support departmental initiatives in coordination with the Co-Chairs and Program & Administration Manager alongside additional duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Core-Seminar-4-Course-Assistant_JR109529": {"title": "Core Seminar 4 Course Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nThe course assistant will bring an understanding of pedagogical approaches to professional art practices, a familiarity with contemporary art institutions, and a passion for supporting students\u2019 development as artists, researchers, and emerging professionals. This role focuses on fostering critical thinking, reflective learning, and active engagement with the art world. RESPONSIBILITIES Facilitate and support class discussions, peer critiques, and small group activities, helping students reflect on their internships and analyze the role of institutions in shaping art practices. Provide feedback and mentorship as students develop case study projects, connecting their hands-on experience with organizational research, critical inquiry, and written analysis. Assist students in developing professional materials, such as resumes, artist statements, and proposals, to strengthen their self-presentation and understanding of institutional expectations. Support experiential learning opportunities by helping to coordinate guest speakers, site visits, and peer presentations, reinforcing connections between course content and real-world practices. Guide students in critically examining curating, programming, and funding structures while considering how these frameworks intersect with individual artistic choices and broader cultural contexts. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Be enrolled and in good standing in the BFA or MFA Fine Arts Program WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE :\u00a0 $25.13/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: The course assistant will facilitate and support class discussions, peer critiques, and small group activities, helping students reflect on their internships and analyze the role of institutions in shaping art practices. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/BFA-Photo-Videography-Assistant_JR109522": {"title": "BFA Photo Videography Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nThe BFA Photography program is seeking a knowledgeable, dedicated, and enthusiastic student to join our team supporting programmatic events and End of Year festivities. This person will be working closely with program leadership and the program assistant, supporting the program on internal curricular events, including year-based Critique Days and the end of year Thesis Exhibition. RESPONSIBILITIES Primary duties for this position include: Support the BFA Photography Program in all public events, including the Visiting Artist lecture series and End of Year festivities. Create visual marketing materials (posters, digital content) for public and curricular events. Design and print wall labels for BFA Photography Thesis Exhibition.\u00a0 Attend, when possible, programmatic events. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Provide a bulleted list of the minimum qualifications that are necessary for someone to be considered for the position. All qualifications must comply with applicable state and Federal legislation. Acceptable qualifications include: Fluency in the Adobe Creative Suite (particularly Premiere Pro) Fluency in camera operation with video settings Strong communication skills and responsiveness to emails Timely submission of work WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.\u00a0#LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $20/hr for 15 weeks We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Videography Assistance is tasked with s upporting the BFA Photography Program in all public events, including the Visiting Artist lecture series and End of Year festivities by helping create visual marketing materials (videos, clips, shorts, posters, digital content) for public and curricular events. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/SAS-Making-Center-Sound-Office-Tech-Assistant_JR109535": {"title": "SAS Making Center Sound Office Tech Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Media Studies Film Office within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 \u00a0 Student Sound Office Techs: Support the Sound Office with the maintenance, organization, and day-to-day operation of sound equipment and resources. Assist faculty and students with the setup, use, and proper care of sound equipment, including microphones, recorders, and accessories. Maintain and support Sound Suits and recording booths, ensuring spaces and equipment are clean, organized, and ready for use. Assist faculty and students with the proper use and basic troubleshooting of Sound Suits and recording booths. Assist with equipment check-ins, check-outs, setup, and inventory. Support the use of additional production equipment, including lighting and camera equipment for in-class use. Use online equipment management and scheduling platforms to support Sound Office operations. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be an active continuing matriculated student Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE On-Campus Position\u00a0-\u00a0Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE :\u00a0\u00a0$17.00hr/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provided customer service and front-desk support in a diverse and inclusive learning environment, assisting students, faculty, and staff with equipment reservations, resource requests, and departmental services. Maintained shop, classroom, and equipment spaces through organization, upkeep, troubleshooting, and equipment management, while supporting daily operations, training sessions, and special projects. Assisted faculty and students with classroom and Sound Office equipment, including check-in/check-out procedures, technical support, and maintaining equipment resources. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Mannes-Prep--Student-Assistant_JR109519": {"title": "Mannes Prep, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Mannes Prep program at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Student Assistant, Mannes Prep. We\u2019re currently looking for responsible, proactive, and kind individual(s) with a musical background to support initiatives within the Mannes Prep Program. This position reports to the Director of Mannes Prep and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in\u00a0program management, music education, or production. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. Mannes Prep student assistants will work approximately 4-6 hours per week. Most work occurs at Arnhold Hall on Saturdays in direct support of Mannes Prep programs, students, and families. RESPONSIBILITIES Host Mannes Prep student recitals. Provide front-of-house assistance to Mannes Prep students and families, serving as ushers for Mannes Prep recitals and events. Assist full-time Mannes Prep staff with the varied Saturday operations of Mannes Prep. Assist ensemble directors with rehearsal setup, breakdown, and attendance. Create and keep track of ensemble parts, and work with Ensemble Directors for any new part needs.\u00a0 Additional responsibilities as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Strong background in the performing arts.\u00a0 Experience with instrumental music programs. Experience working in a customer service oriented environment. Comfortable working with K-12 students and their families. Demonstrated commitment to Equity, Inclusion, Social Justice, and the transformative power of the arts and education. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program. Federal Work Study award recipient.\u00a0 WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17.00 Hours per Week: 4-8 hours per week SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Host recitals, serve as front-of-house ushers, and assist full-time staff with Saturday operations for Mannes Prep students and families. Support ensemble directors with rehearsal logistics, attendance, and sheet music management, while performing additional duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/XMLNAME--FWS--Leadership---Centers-Student-Assistant---The-New-School-Student-Hubs_JR109524": {"title": "(FWS) Leadership & Centers Student Assistant - The New School Student Hubs", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director of Student Leadership & Centers , Leadership & Centers Student Assistants will support the purpose and mission of The Student Hubs and the division of Student Success within The New School. This position primarily focuses on the operational and logistical support of The Student Hubs and University-wide Events, Programs, and Initiatives.\u00a0 Student Success is dedicated to providing you with a dynamic student life experience that complements your curricular experiences and cultivates your intellectual curiosity. We work collaboratively with parts of the university to equip you with resources and opportunities that allow you to thrive personally, professionally, and academically. Our goal is to foster a sense of belonging and help you succeed in an ever-changing world.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Staff The Student Hub(s) \u2019 locations: Triaging visitors with professional customer service, answering questions, providing resources, referrals, campus directions, follow-ups, etc. Maintain bookings, equipment requests, & shift logs : Addressing all booking requests, updating our support platforms, troubleshooting overlaps or specialized needs, and maintaining hourly shift logs for team transparency, status updates, shared accountability, etc. Aid in printing and tech services maintenance : Including troubleshooting, supply replenishment, vendor communication, and setup/supervision of audio-visual equipment for events.\u00a0 Support administrative communications: Support administrative tasks including answering calls/emails, managing event registration, assisting with book and equipment requests, promoting programs, initiatives, and collaborations via The Student Hubs\u2019 platforms (website, monitors, social media, etc.). Support event logistics and campus engagement: assisting with check-in, equipment handling, space setup, and event communications; uplifting student-led initiatives; fostering collaboration across student, staff, and community groups; and participating in major university-wide events as a representative of The Student Hubs. Support internal operations: including inventory management, organizing storage and desk materials, maintaining lost & found, and recommending future equipment purchases.\u00a0 Engage in team development: through training, meetings, onboarding new staff, and maintaining confidentiality with high standards of customer service in all interactions.\u00a0 Other duties as assigned.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This Position requires a Federal Work-Study Award\u00a0 Must be proficient in Google Suite applications (G-cal, G-chat, G-sheets, G-forms, etc.) \u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Ability to creatively problem-solve, multi-task, and function in a collaborative work environment.\u00a0 Capacity to provide and receive constructive critique to improve job performance. This position in the context of Equipment Management and Organization frequently needs to traverse multiple levels of stairs and ladders, position oneself into various levels for reaching items, and move boxes weighing up to 20 pounds across offices, campus locations, and storage spaces for various needs. \u00a0 WORK MODE In-Person - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent time on campus and must report in-person to The Student Hub front desk in order to satisfy all job requirements. #LI-DNI \u00a0 Hourly Rate: $17-$19/hr\u00a0 \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) To be considered: Through the career portal, please provide your financial aid award letter and an up-to-date resume Responsibilities: As a Leadership & Centers Student Assistant , you will be responsible for staffing the front desk(s) and supporting the day-to-day operations of The Student Hub(s) by providing professional customer service, triaging visitors, responding to questions and requests, maintaining bookings, equipment and supplies, supporting printing and technology services, and assisting with administrative communications and recordkeeping. You will also support campus events and engagement initiatives through event logistics, space and equipment setup, student-led programming, university-wide events, and collaboration with students, staff, and community partners, while contributing to internal operations through inventory management, organization, lost and found, and equipment recommendations. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Student-Success-Advisor---College-of-Performing-Arts-Drama_JR109520": {"title": "Student Success Advisor - College of Performing Arts/Drama", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. \u00a0 The Student Success Academic Advisor (SSA) is responsible for providing academic advising to an assigned population of undergraduate and/or graduate students pursuing degree(s) in the School of Drama within the College of Performing Arts. \u00a0 Reporting to the Director, Student Success Advising - Parsons/Performing Arts, this position provides holistic support to students through building an understanding of degree requirements and university policies and systems, supporting registration and academic achievement, and connecting students to appropriate support services at the institution as needed.\u00a0Academic Advisors are active participants in university-wide programs and initiatives that promote student persistence, community building, and staff development. The Academic Advising unit is an integral part of the university\u2019s Student Success division. This position is a Local 1205 Professional Unit position covered by a collective bargaining agreement between Local 1205 Teamsters and The New School. \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Developmentally advise students with the goal of supporting their academic/artistic progress via scheduled and drop-in appointments, both in-person and remote. Maintain and update student records and data, including documentation of student interactions, in relevant institutional systems (Banner, DegreeWorks, Starfish) accurately and in a timely manner. Use critical and creative thinking skills to exercise decision-making and insightful judgment in order to help students chart potential paths to reach academic and professional goals. Assist students in defining and navigating obstacles to achieving academic goals through timely intervention and support. Establish and cultivate relationships with administrators and faculty to ensure accurate and timely communication of student needs, obstacles, and questions. Partner with program directors, department chairs and/or faculty in assigned programs regarding academic planning; attend and participate in regular meetings held by programs and schools. Provide guidance regarding University, school-specific, and program-specific policies with respect to student status, transfer credit evaluations, advanced placement and advanced standing requests, exceptions to degree requirements, degree audits to determine student eligibility to graduate, probationary status, and dismissals. Maintain strict compliance with all mandated policies and procedures, including but not limited to the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) and Title IX. Coordinate and collaborate on initiatives, events, and programs that promote student persistence, retention, and academic development. Maintain an accurate, up-to-date body of knowledge about curricula across the institution, particularly those relevant to the assigned population of students, and provide referrals to other departments or areas within the institution as needed. Exercise professional discretion and judgment to escalate student questions or concerns appropriately when necessary.\u00a0 Participate in orientation, academic, career services events, recruitment, student life and engagement initiatives, and graduation.\u00a0 Represent Student Success at programs and functions outside of normal business hours as needed. Stay apprised of national developments in the field of academic advising and engage in ongoing training and development opportunities. Incorporate tools and assessments into advising to help students understand their personal preferences, strengths, and weaknesses as they pertain to academic decision-making. Possess intercultural competency skills that promote a culture of equity, inclusion, social justice, and community engagement. Assist students in navigating co-curricular and student life opportunities within the University. Other duties as assigned.\u00a0 QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor\u2019s degree and 2 years of related experience with a focus on student engagement and advising. Demonstrated experience via study or employment working in or with programs in the dramatic arts, i.e acting, playwriting, directing. Demonstrated excellence in interpersonal skills, including sensitivity to cultural differences.\u00a0 Proven abilities in personal judgment and critical thinking skills to help others make values-based decisions regarding academic, career, and other success paths. Strong presentation, written and verbal communication skills.\u00a0 Demonstrated organizational skills and ability to work on multiple projects both independently and as part of a team.\u00a0 Proficiency in Microsoft Office and ability to learn new computer applications.\u00a0 Able to lift and carry objects, less than 40 pounds, on occasion. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. \u00a0 Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $66,300 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: \u00a0 Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) \u00a0 Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan \u00a0 Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) \u00a0 Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents \u00a0 Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/BFA-Illustration-Senior-Publication-Assistant_JR109497": {"title": "BFA Illustration Senior Publication Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nPart time\n\nThe Senior Publication Assistant role involves file checking, communicating with the professor in charge of senior publication and fellow students about corrections, assisting with layout design and typesetting and possibly typeface design RESPONSIBILITIES Clerical work related to senior publication design Assisting in installing the show's vinyl labels/signage Support fellow students in layout and typeface design MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be a senior in the BFA Illustration program Excellent skills with Indesign and Photoshop Comfortable working with Google Spreadsheets. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: \u00a0 $20.00/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Senior Publication Assistant supports the BFA Illustration program on c lerical work related to senior publication design, assisting in installing the show's vinyl labels/signage and support fellow students in layout and typeface design. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/MA-Media-Studies-Student-Event-Assistant_JR109492-1": {"title": "MA Media Studies Student Event Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nPart time\n\nThe MA, Media Studies program is seeking motivated and detail-oriented Event Support student workers to support the ongoing coordination and management of the MA Media Studies program-led and student-led events.\u00a0 This includes support for FINE CUTS student showcase, RUFF CUTS monthly events, SPEAKING OF MEDIA, CRITICAL THEMES, MEDIA WORKS! Alumni Spotlight, HIRSHON ARTIST IN RESIDENCE events, and any additional ad hoc events. The event support students will work with the event point people and faculty coordinator(s) as well as the MA, Program Director to support public-facing events, managing communications, facilitating the run of the show, and adhering to budget as appropriate. The position provides an opportunity for a student with demonstrated interest in event production and/or with professional skills in event organizing and program administration. RESPONSIBILITIES Schedule and hold weekly meetings, either in person or via Zoom with events team organizers and events team Be present at all scheduled events. Liaise among all students contributing to events, faculty event point people, and MA Director. Design, print, and distribute promotional posters and digital outreach materials including social media in cooperation with student Comms assistant. Organize and maintain events\u00a0 archives, including arranging video recordings as necessary. Support faculty producers of events in all aspects of event organizing. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current graduate student in good standing at The New School with a minimum GPA of 3.0 Must be within the time limit for attaining their graduate degree. Must not have more than 33% of attempted course credits as incomplete. Must not be employed at The New School as administrative staff. Strong organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet deadlines. Effective written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated interest in media programming and event production. Solid technical skills in recording and editing audio/video content. Experience with Adobe Creative Suite (Premiere Pro, Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign) or equivalent tools. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience producing events or assisting in public programming. Design skills for print and digital media. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate :\u00a0 $20.00/hr, max 75 hours per semester/5hrs a week. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The event assistant is responsible for supporting the planning, promotion and execution of events for the Master of Arts in Media Studies program, along with its faculty and administrative staff. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Media-Studies-Communications-Assistant_JR109495": {"title": "Media Studies Communications Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nPart time\n\nThe MA, Media Studies program is seeking a motivated and detail-oriented Communications Assistants to support the program\u2019s current, alumni, and prospective student outreach.\u00a0 As designer and editing assistant for the MediaLog monthly newsletter tailored to the MA, Media Studies community that includes current students, faculty, staff, and alumni, coordinating as well on social media production.\u00a0 The Communications Assistant will work closely with the MA, Program Director to solicit and aggregate news items, copy edit, manage design elements, and maintain an organized template for delivery to the AMT operations team for and deployment of the newsletter\u00a0 in a timely manner. They additionally send email blast reminders and maintain a social media presence via instagram and additional platforms. RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain a calendar for regular beginning-of-month publication of MediaLog newsletter. Be point person for any student-related news and events Maintain regular contact with with all student organizations and solicit updates Manage, in collaboration with the program director, the newsletter submission form. Share select submissions with the AMT newsletter aggregator. Design visual elements for publication. Collaborate on creating norms for a publication style guide. Create social media content according to M&C guidelines ready for approval by faculty Director.\u00a0 Edit submitted event announcements in keeping with a MediaLog style guide. Organize and maintain the newsletter archives. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current student in good standing at The New School with a minimum cumulative GPA of 3.0. Must not have more than 33% of attempted course credits as incomplete. Strong organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet deadlines. Effective written and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated interest in writing and publishing. Solid technical skills in web publishing and social media applications. Experience with Adobe Creative Suite (Premiere Pro, Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign) or equivalent tools. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience in communications work. Prior experience in news writing and/or digital publishing. Design skills for print and digital media. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate :\u00a0 $20.00/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Media Studies Communications Assistant will support the Media Studies MA Program in maintaining engagement through multiple platforms. In collaboration with the program director, they will also create social media content according to M&C guidelines. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/New-York-City-NY/FWS-Design-and-Communications-Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109283-1": {"title": "FWS Design and Communications Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nPart time\n\nThe Vera List Center for Art and Politics seeks a Federal Work Study student with graphic design, and photo/video production and editing experience to assist with various administrative, editorial, programmatic, and social media tasks, as well as supporting the presentation of public events such as the VLC Forum 2026, VLC Seminars, and New School New Books. The student should be available roughly 7\u201312 hours/week, averaging about 10 hours per week, at an hourly rate of $21/hour. The student will be expected to work in person at least 4 hours per week, including some evenings and weekends. The position begins in September and will run through December 2026, with the intention of being continued through the end of the academic year.\u00a0 The student will report directly to Re\u2019al Christian, Assistant Director of Editorial Initiatives.\u00a0 The Design and Communications Student Assistant Specialist will gain insight into the Vera List Center\u2019s organization, including its public events, publications, exhibitions, fellowships, and prize cycles. The Student Assistant Specialist will learn about the interdisciplinary contributions of the VLC to New York City\u2019s cultural communities and will be engaged with the Center\u2019s efforts to diversify its programs and make them broadly accessible. The position is open to undergraduate or graduate students in any program at The New School and seeks candidates who are passionate about the arts, how they intersect with current political issues, and often enhance social justice work and strategies.\u00a0 The Vera List Center for Art and Politics is an artist-centered organization at The New School that imagines and supports politically-engaged art, public scholarship, research, and community around the world. Founded in 1992, we are guided by the unwavering belief that artists play an essential role addressing issues that are urgent to communities around the world. We are: a vibrant locally, nationally, and internationally connected hub for art that advances equity, inclusion, and social justice; a forum for rigorous study, research, and teaching on the significance of art as a force for political empowerment; and a venue to support, present, and commission visionary projects. Across all our work, we galvanize resources to support artists and nurture public recognition of the extraordinary role they play in society. We contribute to a thriving arts ecosystem through: direct support of artists (prizes, fellowships, exhibitions, and other meaningful public presentations of artists\u2019 work); educational and public programs that are free and open to the public; and publications intended for a wide audience. www.veralistcenter.org RESPONSIBILITIES Duties of the position include:\u00a0 Creation of graphics in support of the Focus Theme, Matter of Intelligence , the related fellowship artists and their projects, upcoming seminar series and other initiatives using current and new design templates.\u00a0 General administrative support such as help with preparing mailings, website updates, filing and organization, putting together itineraries, etc. Basic editing of long-form video documentation from VLC programs for digital platforms including website, Vimeo, and Instagram. Creation of compelling short-form video excerpts of programs for social media usage.\u00a0 Occasional video or photographic documentation of VLC programs. Support of program evaluations, attendance, and statistics, and gathering other data relevant for outreach, PR, and development.\u00a0 Research and organizing information for VLC fellows and events Support of communication efforts in accordance with the VLC outreach calendar and social media, to include taking pictures and drafting posts.\u00a0 Assistance and design help with publication and other asset management and organization. Use of FileMaker database to archive the VLC projects and network, including creating new records for individuals, events, and programs; managing lists, updating prior entries; using it to export lists of contacts, addresses, etc. and saving backups of the FileMaker archive regularly (prior experience with FileMaker is not necessary, but past experience with a similar database is a plus). Other administrative and programmatic tasks as assigned. In-person event support, evenings and weekends. \u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must qualify for Federal Work Study stipend. Passion for engaging with diverse communities of different backgrounds through contemporary art and social justice work.\u00a0 Experience with video editing software, designing title treatments Ability to edit for multiple formats and aspect ratios (9:16, 1:1, 16:9, 4:5) for TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, CTV, and web. Skilled in Photoshop, InDesign, and Figma. Strong office, administrative, and organizational skills. Exemplary attitude and work habits, including diligence and attention to detail. Independent worker with great self motivation and initiative, who thrives working simultaneously across different tasks and platforms and can work without close supervision or alone. Commitment for two semesters and availability in the evening and weekends to help with events. Outstanding research, writing, and communication skills.\u00a0 \u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Curatorial and editorial experience or interest. Pursuing a degree in design-related field. Some knowledge of sound design and audio mixing to polish final cuts. Event planning and coordination experience.\u00a0 Social media and communications experience. Experience with video editing, graphic design, WordPress, and Adobe Illustrator software a plus. \u00a0 WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $21/hour \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Please provide a resume, cover letter stating your interest, and a federal work study award letter.\u00a0 Responsibilities: The FWS Design and Communications Student Assistant Specialist will work with various members of the VLC staff providing day-to-day support as well as working on longer term projects. This position is multifaceted and includes administrative, communications, publishing, and programmatic responsibilities, at times extending to archival work or database management. Specific work will include drafting event and communication graphics using VLC design templates, video or photography editing, staffing in person events and exhibitions, and sometimes producing original design materials. Candidates should have an interest in design and editorial work across publications, communications, and video.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/New-York-City-NY/VLC-Programs-Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109279-1": {"title": "VLC Programs Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Vera List Center for Art and Politics seeks a student to support our programs by assisting with various administrative, programmatic, social media, and design tasks, as well as supporting the presentation of public events such as the VLC Forum 2026. The student should be available 7\u201312 hours/week, including during the evening or weekend, at an hourly rate of $21/hour. The student will be expected to work in person at least 4 hours per week, some during the evening or weekend. The position begins in early September and will run through June 2027 with the possibility of being continued in future academic years. The student worker will report directly to Adrienne Umeh, the Director of Finance and Operations at the Vera List Center but will work closely with other members of the team at various times. The student assistant specialist will gain insight into the Vera List Center\u2019s organization, including its public events, publications, exhibitions, fellowships, prize cycles, development and operations. The student assistant will learn about the interdisciplinary contributions of the VLC to New York City\u2019s cultural communities and will be engaged with the Center\u2019s efforts to diversify its programs and make them broadly accessible. The position is open to graduate students in any program at The New School and seeks candidates who are passionate about the arts, how they intersect with current political issues, and often enhance social justice work and strategies.\u00a0 \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Duties of the position include:\u00a0 General administrative support such as help with preparing mailings, website updates, filing and organization, putting together itineraries, etc. Support of program evaluations, attendance, and statistics, and gathering other data relevant for outreach, PR, and development. Research and organizing information for VLC events, the VLC Fellows library and other initiatives. Organizing the VLC collections of books, papers, and art materials. Surveying the contemporary art landscape to produce a dataset of collectors whose interests overlap with the VLC. Supporting communication efforts in accordance with the VLC outreach calendar and social media, to include taking or organizing pictures and drafting posts.\u00a0 Assistance with publication and other asset management and organization. Using the FileMaker database to archive the VLC projects and network, including creating new records for individuals, events, and programs; managing lists, updating prior entries; using it to export lists of contacts, addresses, etc. and saving backups of the FileMaker archive regularly. Other administrative and programmatic tasks as assigned. In-person event support, especially from October 5\u2013November 1 to support the VLC Forum and Prize exhibition. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Passion for engaging with diverse communities of different backgrounds and an interest in contemporary art and social justice work.\u00a0 Strong office, administrative, and organizational skills. Exemplary attitude and work habits including diligence and an attention to detail. Independent worker with great self motivation and initiative, who thrives working simultaneously across different tasks and platforms and is able to work without close supervision or alone. Commitment for two semesters and availability to work in the evenings and weekends to help with events. Outstanding research, writing, and communication skills. \u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Curatorial and editorial experience. Event planning and coordination experience.\u00a0 Social media and communication experience. Experience with video editing, graphic design, WordPress, Adobe Illustrator, Adobe Photoshop and/or Adobe Indesign software a plus. \u00a0 WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split #LI-DNI \u00a0 HOURLY RATE $21/hour \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * Please submit a resume/CV. Responsibilities: The VLC Programs Student Assistant Specialist will work with various members of the VLC staff providing day-to-day support as well as working on longer term projects. This position is multifaceted and includes administrative, communications, development and programmatic responsibilities, at times extending to archival work or database management. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Parsons-Fashion-Study-Collection--PFSC--Student-Worker_JR109374-1": {"title": "Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC) Student Worker", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nPart time\n\nParsons\u2019 School of Fashion (SoF) leads the industry into a future where access, equity and sustainability are the standard. We offer a Bachelor of Fine Arts degree in Fashion Design, Associate Degrees in Fashion Design as well as in Fashion Marketing and Communication, a Master of Fine Arts in Textiles, a Master of Professional Studies in Fashion Management, and a Master of Fine Arts in Fashion Design and Society. Our students, faculty, and staff are committed to supporting Indigenous resurgence; honoring the beauty of all bodies; and living in harmony with animals and the earth in ways that advance climate justice. The School of Art and Design History and Theory (ADHT) engages students at all degree levels in the analysis of art and design as essential to the formation of knowledge and culture. ADHT offers the first-year undergraduate curriculum to all Parsons students as well as a new BFA program, Design History and Practice. Graduate programs from ADHT include the MA Fashion Studies and the MA History of Design and Curatorial Studies programs. These part-time positions and associated work is in relation to the Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC). The collection of approximately 4,000 items extends from the late 19th century to the latter half of the 20th century including noted designers: Sophie Gimbel, Claire McCardell, Donald Brooks, Norman Norell, Geoffrey Beene, Giorgio di Sant'angelo and Yves Saint Laurent. The ideal candidates will demonstrate a commitment to curatorial work, handling archival fashion items, collection management and administrative tasks associated with the Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC), including preparing /facilitating class visits from courses within ADHT and SoF. The candidates will report directly to the Associate Dean in the School of Fashion. RESPONSIBILITIES Primary job duties and responsibilities will include: Liaising with faculty for Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC) visits Selecting garments based on requests in preparation for class visits Facilitate class visits to the collection, schedule-permitting Return garments to the collection at the conclusion of class visits Cataloging/Image management Reconcile images of archived garments with the existing database Documentation of remaining images of the collection via photography Upload images to collection management system Past Perfect Curate onsite exhibits in the School of Fashion Hub as needed MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Acceptable qualifications include: Enrolled in one of the following undergraduate /graduate programs: BFA Fashion Design, AAS Fashion Design, MA Fashion Studies, MFA Fashion, Design and Society, MA History of Design and Curatorial Studies Strong verbal and written communication skills PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience working with collections and/or archives Experience working with Past Perfect We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $20/hour. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Resume / Cover Letter outlining expression of interest and suitability for the position Responsibilities: The position supports the Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC) by coordinating faculty visits and preparing garments for classroom use. Responsibilities include selecting, organizing, and returning garments for class visits. The role also involves cataloging and managing collection images, including photographing garments and uploading images to the PastPerfect collection management system. Student workers will help maintain accurate records and documentation of the collection. Additionally, they may assist with curating onsite exhibits in the School of Fashion Hub. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---Admission-Events_JR109513": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant - Admission Events", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nThe New School is looking for an Admission Events Graduate Assistant. Our events are designed to help prospective and admitted students and their families learn more about The New School\u2019s academic programs, social life, and application requirements. The Admission Events Graduate Assistant provides administrative support to professional staff charged with staffing the small-, mid-, and large-scale events that comprise our recruitment yield season. Our student team assists in event planning and implementation, including setup and breakdown, check-in, and guest management. They also assist in the creation of video and/or photo production and social media campaigns in support of admission events. Assistants are also contributors to the Welcome Center Instagram account, online admitted student communities, student panels, and the development of flyers, posts, and social media stories. Although often behind-the-scenes, Admission Events Student Assistants are responsible for acting as representatives of the university, warmly welcoming all campus visitors and establishing a quality first impression online and in-person. Our team must display pride and passion for The New School, maintain up-to-date information about our academic programs, student services and consistently represent ourselves and the university in a genuine, professional, and inclusive manner. Students with a Federal Work Study Award will receive priority in hiring. Responsibilities:\u00a0 Event setup and breakdown\u00a0 Check-in attendees and guests Warmly welcome and assist prospective/newly admitted students as well as their families and supporters\u00a0 Conduct event materials and supply inventory Support prospective communication experience using Canvas, Adobe Suite, Microsoft Office Suite, and video and Photo Editing Software Qualifications: Applicants must be currently enrolled in a degree program at The New School. Additional qualifications: Prior retail or event experience Impeccable attention to detail and aesthetics Sound judgment Positive attitude Excellent communicator Highly organized\u2014enjoys organization Self-motivated Enjoys assisting others and providing support Able to focus without being distracted by cell phones, personal social media, etcFamiliarity with Instagram Experience in graphic and/or video/production design Familiarity with Canvas, Adobe Suite, and Microsoft Office Suite Familiarity with Video and Photo Editing Software Work-mode: Hybrid/Remote #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $18 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Admission Events Student Assistants are responsible for acting as representatives of the university, warmly welcoming all campus visitors and establishing a quality first impression online and in-person. Our team must display pride and passion for The New School, maintain up-to-date information about our academic programs, student services and consistently represent ourselves and the university in a genuine, professional, and inclusive manner We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant--Making-Center--MFA-Textiles-MFA-FDS-_JR108877": {"title": "Student Assistant, Making Center (MFA Textiles/MFA FDS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the MFA Textile and MFA FDS department within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Overseeing and updating inventory and utilizing web checkout systems daily Domestic and industrial sewing machines, and ironing stations Daily Overseeing dye lab, Machine knitting lab, J3 sewing lab, and or J3 textile sewing lab Floor looms, felting station, donation closets (In JJ1 Textiles Studios) Check students in/out of the space using Web checkout (In JJ1 Textiles Studios) Check tools in/out in Web checkout Maintain Silver Reed and Dubied Knitting Machines\u00a0 Answer basic questions about equipment and lab policies Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends For Machine Knitting support positions, completion of a machine knitting class or other previous machine knitting experience is preferred. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests, assist Making Center Staff with lab upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of labs and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0 and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant--Communication-and-Events_JR109490": {"title": "Student Assistant, Communication and Events", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nEugene Lang College of Liberal Arts and The New School for Social Research \u2014 which includes all undergraduate and graduate programs \u2014 is seeking a Communications and Events Student Assistant Specialist to support the Associate Director of Communications and Events within the Executive Dean's Office. This role helps raise the visibility of the college's academic programs, faculty, students, alumni, centers, labs, and institutes through event staffing and Splash event page development. The successful candidate will report to the Associate Director of Communications and Events. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with event planning and provide on-site event staffing support. Build Splash event registration pages for programs across the college and schedule associated confirmation and reminder emails. Review and approve Splash pages for grammatical accuracy, correct formatting, properly hyperlinked links and bios, and complete event details. Manage newsletter subscriber lists in Mailchimp, updating existing contacts and maintaining the organization spreadsheet. Archive newsletters and maintain up-to-date archive records. Monitor Google Alerts and press mentions relevant to the college's programs, faculty, and initiatives. Communicate regularly with the Associate Director of Communications and Events via email and Slack, providing weekly updates on task progress. Track key communications metrics, such as newsletter open rates, social media engagement, and event attendance, as requested. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current matriculated student at The New School. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Interest in, or basic understanding of, communications principles and practices. High level of professionalism. Ability to work independently as well as collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students. Highly organized, with excellent attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple projects. Strong research skills. Proficiency with technical platforms such as Mailchimp, Canva, Google Workspace, and social media platforms (X/Twitter, Instagram, Facebook). PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Graduate student. Previous communications experience. Previous experience with website management systems. Familiarity with project management software platforms. Event planning experience. WORK MODE \u25cf Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have a regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $18 - 21/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student worker position supporting the Screen Studies Production Studio is essential for the successful teaching of hands-on workshops and technical demonstrations including how to operate a camera, light, use sound equipment, block scenes, film interviews, and other important skills. By supporting faculty through the maintenance of equipment, this role ensures that students meet the learning objectives outlined in their courses. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Ringer-Accompanist--Jazz--Pop--and-Improvised-Music-Ensembles_JR109501": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Ringer/Accompanist, Jazz, Pop, and Improvised Music Ensembles", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz and Contemporary Music at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Overhire Ringer/Accompanist, Jazz, Pop, and Improvised Music Ensembles to join its team. Reporting to the Part Time Operations Manager, the Overhire Ringer/Accompanist, Jazz, Pop, and Improvised Music Ensembles is directly responsible for supporting, accompanying, and performing with ensembles and performance based projects throughout the college.\u00a0 We\u2019re looking for professional, experienced, flexible musician(s) to support initiatives within the School of Jazz & Contemporary Music on an as-needed basis. This includes the New School Studio Orchestra, Improvisation Ensembles, Elective Ensembles, and other groups. Alumni of the College of Performing Arts are strongly encouraged to apply. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Attend all the rehearsals and performances as scheduled with the ensemble Bring one\u2019s own instrument(s) and gear as needed Analyze assigned music prior to rehearsal and performances (e.g., correct tempos, challenging transitions, dynamics) to ensure efficient use of rehearsal time Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with students, faculty, staff, and audience members Be prepared to play professionally with one\u2019s assigned part Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items including attire Advise management when a sub is needed QUALIFICATIONS Trained musician (genre depends on repertoire for the ensemble) Ability to play all assigned instruments, including auxiliaries Prior large ensemble experience Familiarity with American Vernacular genres Excellent sight reading skills PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with contemporary and non-traditional scores. Alumni of the College of Performing Arts Section leadership experience Professional level improvisation skills (level and genre depends on repertoire) WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Up to 15 hours per week, per cycle, variable commitment with no guaranteed hours. Rehearsals are typically on weekday afternoons/evenings between 1:00 PM - 3:30 PM or 4:00pm-10:00pm, depending on the ensemble schedule. SALARY RANGE: $45.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. \u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Red-Dog-Productions--FWS-_JR109511": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Red Dog Productions (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks student staff for the Red Dog Productions videography team. The assignments include University-wide live events, covering symposia and panel discussions hosted in the auditoriums as well as documenting conferences, exhibitions, social receptions, concerts and lectures. Red Dog Productions will train individuals and they will work alongside producers and faculty advisors to create professional quality, original content that can be added to career portfolio and resumes, while gaining professional experience. Red Dog Productions is currently seeking great candidates to perform the duties of the Production Crew. This is a non-academic worker student staff position that reports to the Manager of Red Dog Productions. RESPONSIBILITIES Shoot HD video and set up equipment including tripod and mics Using editing software, transfer and compress files; edit footage Audio mixing Lighting setup and design for interviews, streaming and still photography Web and social media for video and photography Training in different areas as needed MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current FT active student status Knowledge of film and video equipment Technical knowledge of film and video cameras, lenses, lighting, audio, and support equipment\u00a0\u00a0 Experience with film and video production procedures and protocols, set ups, studio and equipment maintenance Film editing and software experience Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Production experience\u00a0 WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE\u00a0: $17/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Shoot HD video and set up equipment including tripod and mics. Using editing software, transfer and compress files and edit footage. Create audio mixing, lighting setup/design for interviews, streaming and still photography. Create web and social media for video and photography. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Design-Strategies-Wide-Student-Assistant_JR109503": {"title": "Design Strategies Wide Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nThe Design Strategies Operations Office Student Assistant will provide administrative and operational support to keep the Design Strategies Operations Office running smoothly day-to-day. Reporting to the undergraduate Program Administrator, the Student Assistant will help organize digital files, manage shared inboxes, support event logistics, prepare communications and materials, and maintain internal systems.\u00a0 A successful candidate will be detail-oriented, proactive, and comfortable working independently while communicating effectively with staff, faculty, and fellow students. The role requires strong organizational skills, familiarity with digital tools (e.g., Google Workspace, Excel, project management platforms), and a willingness to take initiative in solving small but essential operational challenges. This position plays an important role in enhancing the efficiency and responsiveness of the Design Strategies Operations Office, contributing to a supportive infrastructure that benefits students, faculty, and staff across Design Strategies. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with general administrative tasks such as scheduling, file organization, and document preparation. Monitor and respond to inquiries in the Design Strategies Operations Office shared inbox, escalating issues as needed. Help coordinate logistics for internal meetings and school-wide events, including room reservations, materials preparation, and day-of assistance. Update spreadsheets, contact lists, and internal records to ensure accuracy and accessibility. Maintain and organize shared drives and folders, ensuring clear structure and easy navigation for the team. Track office supplies and help order and restock as needed. Support special projects and initiatives within the Operations Office, including research, scheduling, and communications tasks. Work collaboratively with staff and faculty, providing responsive, courteous support and clear communication on assigned tasks. Design graphics and posters for events Update related websites and organize pages with current events MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled as a student at The New School Strong organizational and time-management skills and the capacity\u00a0 to think strategically and to effectively move ideas forward into implementation Ability to communicate effectively on multiple levels across the organization Proficiency in Google Workspace (Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive) Familiarity with design software (Adobe Creative Suite, Figma, etc.) preferred Strong written and verbal communication skills Interest in urbanism, design justice, community-based work, or sustainability Ability to work independently and collaboratively Attention to detail and ability to manage multiple tasks WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on campus during regularly scheduled work hours because the work cannot be performed remotely without being infeasible or not in the best interest of the organization. Work hours are not fixed and may vary from week to week based on program needs. Student Assistants will support events taking place throughout the semester and should have some flexibility in their availability to accommodate scheduled program events. Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This role delivers comprehensive administrative support by managing inbox inquiries, organizing digital drives, updating records, and overseeing inventory and scheduling. It facilitates seamless event and meeting operations through logistical coordination, material preparation, graphic design, and website updates. The position also collaborates with staff and faculty to support special operational initiatives and day-of-event execution We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/MS-EPSM-Student-Assistant_JR109507": {"title": "MS EPSM Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nThe EPSM program seeks a Student Assistant to provide research and programmatic support that advances the program\u2019s curriculum, mission, and public engagement initiatives. Working closely with the EPSM Program Director and Program Administrator, the Student Assistant will support public-facing events, including webinars, workshops, and panels. In partnership with the Program Administrator, the Student Assistant will support the program's daily operations, including communications, event coordination, and data collection. This role is best suited for graduate students with prior EPSM experience and research expertise in areas such as environmental justice, climate justice, Indigenous ecologies, gender and ecology, and environmental policy.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Support the planning and execution of EPSM events (workshops, panels, webinars, etc.). Provide general program support as needed in partnership with the Program Administrator. Collect and organize data to inform program initiatives and reporting. Attend\u00a0 meetings with the Program Director and Program Administrator MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled as a student at The New School Strong organizational and time-management skills and the capacity\u00a0 to think strategically and to effectively move ideas forward into implementation Ability to communicate effectively on multiple levels across the organization Proficiency in Google Workspace (Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive) Familiarity with design software (Adobe Creative Suite, Figma, etc.) preferred Strong written and verbal communication skills Interest in urbanism, design justice, community-based work, or sustainability Ability to work independently and collaboratively Attention to detail and ability to manage multiple tasks WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on campus during regularly scheduled work hours because the work cannot be performed remotely without being infeasible or not in the best interest of the organization. #LI-DNI Work hours are not fixed and may vary from week to week based on program needs. Student Assistants will support events taking place throughout the semester and should have some flexibility in their availability to accommodate scheduled program events. Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment based on race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This role provides core operational support by partnering with program leadership to plan and execute events such as workshops, panels, and webinars. It assists with general program administration and meeting participation to keep initiatives running smoothly. Additionally, the position collects and organizes key data to inform reporting and guide ongoing program strategies. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/BFA-IDP-Student-Assistant_JR109509": {"title": "BFA IDP Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nThe IDP Student Assistant provides essential operational and leadership support for events hosted by the Integrated Design Program (IDP), helping ensure smooth execution from setup to breakdown. This role involves preparing event spaces, welcoming guests, assisting presenters and participants, and handling logistics during workshops, showcases, community gatherings, and some audiovisual and photo editing. The student helps create a positive, organized, and engaging environment for all attendees. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with setting up and breaking down event spaces, including furniture, signage, equipment, and materials. Provide basic audiovisual support, light IT support, photo editing, and printing.\u00a0 Greet and direct attendees, providing information and support as needed. Support facilitators, presenters, and performers with logistical and technical needs. Manage event materials such as name tags, programs, or handouts. Help monitor and maintain cleanliness and organization throughout the event. Document events through photos or notes for later use in communications or reports. Coordinate with staff to troubleshoot issues quickly and effectively. Assist with post-event tasks such as organizing supplies, debriefing, and evaluating outcomes. Ensure events are welcoming, inclusive, and reflective of the IDP mission. Perform other duties as assigned in support of successful event execution. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled as a student at The New School Strong organizational and time-management skills and the capacity\u00a0 to think strategically and to effectively move ideas forward into implementation Ability to communicate effectively on multiple levels across the organization Proficiency in Google Workspace (Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive) Familiarity with design software (Adobe Creative Suite, Figma, etc.) preferred Strong written and verbal communication skills Interest in urbanism, design justice, community-based work, or sustainability Ability to work independently and collaboratively Attention to detail and ability to manage multiple tasks WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on campus during regularly scheduled work hours because the work cannot be performed remotely without being infeasible or not in the best interest of the organization. #LI-DNI Work hours are not fixed and may vary from week to week based on program needs. Student Assistants will support events taking place throughout the semester and should have some flexibility in their availability to accommodate scheduled program events. Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This role ensures successful event execution by managing physical setup and breakdown, providing technical and logistical support to presenters, and troubleshooting issues alongside staff. It enhances attendee experiences by offering welcoming guidance, distributing materials, and keeping spaces organized and inclusive. The position also handles post-event operations, including capturing photos and notes, tracking supplies, and assisting with performance evaluations. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Ringer-Accompanist--Jazz--Pop--and-Improvised-Music-Ensembles_JR109463": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Ringer/Accompanist, Jazz, Pop, and Improvised Music Ensembles", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz and Contemporary Music at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Overhire Ringer/Accompanist, Jazz, Pop, and Improvised Music Ensembles to join its team. Reporting to the Part Time Operations Manager, the Overhire Ringer/Accompanist, Jazz, Pop, and Improvised Music Ensembles is directly responsible for supporting, accompanying, and performing with ensembles and performance based projects throughout the college.\u00a0 \u00a0 We\u2019re looking for professional, experienced, flexible musician(s) to support initiatives within the School of Jazz & Contemporary Music on an as-needed basis. This includes the New School Studio Orchestra, Improvisation Ensembles, Elective Ensembles, and other groups. Alumni of the College of Performing Arts are strongly encouraged to apply. \u00a0 This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Attend all the rehearsals and performances as scheduled with the ensemble Bring one\u2019s own instrument(s) and gear as needed Analyze assigned music prior to rehearsal and performances (e.g., correct tempos, challenging transitions, dynamics) to ensure efficient use of rehearsal time Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with students, faculty, staff, and audience members Be prepared to play professionally with one\u2019s assigned part Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items including attire Advise management when a sub is needed QUALIFICATIONS Trained musician (genre depends on repertoire for the ensemble) Ability to play all assigned instruments, including auxiliaries Prior large ensemble experience Familiarity with American Vernacular genres Excellent sight reading skills PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with contemporary and non-traditional scores. Alumni of the College of Performing Arts Section leadership experience Professional level improvisation skills (level and genre depends on repertoire) WORK MODE T his position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Up to 15 hours per week, per cycle, variable commitment with no guaranteed hours. Rehearsals are typically on weekday afternoons/evenings between 1:00 PM - 3:30 PM or 4:00pm-10:00pm, depending on the ensemble schedule. Hourly Rate: $45.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Assistant-Manager--Sound-Office_JR109214-1": {"title": "Assistant Manager, Sound Office", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks an Assistant Manager of the Sound Office for the Making Center. The Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0The Assistant Manager should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Assistant Manager will interface with students, faculty, and staff to support multiple processes and equipment resources for all users in a range of making practices including sound recording studios, equipment resources, and media production studios. This is an exempt administrative position that reports to the Assistant Director of Sound & Filmmaking Operations . \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain a clean and safe working environment and uphold a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Oversee daily workings of the Sound Office, a major resource open to New School students supporting sound, video, and related equipment available for on- and off-campus check-out.\u00a0 Management of full-time technicians and student workers, including interviewing, hiring, training, scheduling, tracking accountability, task assignment, scheduling/attending team and one-on-one meetings, and coordination and/or providing emergency coverage for unexpected absences.\u00a0 Manage a fixed budget, including tracking, planning, and prioritization of expenses related to inventories, schedules, projects, and repairs. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special initiatives as needed. Hardware/software purchase recommendations as they pertain to the curriculum. Maintaining policies and systems for checkout, satellite store, scheduling, record keeping, and inventories, in addition to generating macro and micro levels of usage data and statistics - evaluating and updating these as needed. Organize calendars and communications for facilities, including creation of website updates, shop signage, safety orientations, technical/instructional documentation for labs/equipment, workshops, class demos, deliveries, special events, and modified holiday hours. Work across systems in the Making Center, connecting separate areas in order to streamline efficiency and communication. Facilitate equipment repair and maintenance, including in-house repairs and coordination with external vendors to manage repair processes.\u00a0 Adapt to changes in the work environment; adjust approach and prioritize competing demands as necessary. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment with productive and positive workplace relationships.\u00a0 Stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and 3-5 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to scheduling shifts with semester changes. Completion of university required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Advanced knowledge of a variety of sound practices; be familiar with equipment and software related to current sound design practices - including: studio-based and experimental processes, digital input/output, and industry-standard software applications.\u00a0 Experience maintaining, repairing, and troubleshooting equipment/tools. Full technical knowledge of sound-based analog and digital technologies, including but not limited to monitors, microphones, control panels, preamps, mixers, media players, Mac mini(s), tablets, and other smart devices. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment, with a working knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployment, network systems, and color calibration/profiling. Familiarity with Adobe Creative Cloud, Pro Tools, Avid, and Google Docs. Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Proven track record of working well with people from diverse cultural backgrounds, with a strong ability to support students working in an interdisciplinary manner from concept to fabrication. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems a plus.\u00a0 Knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployments; Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems a plus.\u00a0 WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee.#LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $64,350 - $69,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant---Book-Research---Development--and-Promotional-Assistant_JR109401": {"title": "Research Assistant - Book Research & Development, and Promotional Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\nPart time\n\nThe Research Assistant will work with Jessica Walker to support ongoing promotion of her recently released book as well as develop research and production for a new graphic novel project and support with event facilitation around the theme of children\u2019s book publishing. \u00a0 Duties and Responsibilities: Meet bi-weekly with faculty to develop research plan Identify primary and secondary resources that can inform the project\u2019s written and visual content Assemble research findings into a shared repository which may include Google Drive and Miro Document all works cited for written and visual resources Support with promotion and launch events for project being released in November 2025 Support with social media strategic plan Qualifications \u2013 Education, Experience, and Skills Experience conducting research for visual and/or narrative projects Ability to work independently Familiarity with novels and graphic novels for young adults Experience with creating social media content for creative projects WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will work with Jessica Walker to support ongoing promotion of her recently released book as well as develop research and production for a new graphic novel project and support with event facilitation around the theme of children\u2019s book publishing. Some tasks include developing a research plan, identifying sources, organizing findings and digital databases, among others. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Student-Marketing-Model---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109086": {"title": "Student Marketing Model - Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. We are seeking Student Models to participate in Marketing and Communications (M&C) photoshoots and videoshoots which will be used in advertising for recruitment for the Fall 2026 semester. The selected students will collaborate with The New School marketing staff and other selected students to gather useful photography and films to convey marketing stories. The photoshoots are expected to take approximately 5 hours (although some shoots may take longer), typically over the course of 1-2 days. CORE CAPABILITIES AND PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Pose for marketing photography / videography Advertise TNS products and services in marketing films. Participate in a photoshoot set up and provide input when requested. QUALIFICATIONS Current New School students in good standing and enrolled for the Spring 2026 semester Responsible and punctual, and able to meet deadlines and take direction WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. \u00a0 #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $20 per hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: We are seeking Student Models to participate in Marketing and Communications (M&C) photoshoots and videoshoots which will be used in advertising for recruitment for the Fall 2026 semester. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Wardrobe-Supervisor_JR109456": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Wardrobe Supervisor", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nPart time\n\nThe College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Wardrobe Supervisor to join its Performance Operations \u2013 Costume Department Staff. The Costume Department is responsible for the creation and delivery of garments as designated and discussed by the Costume Designer, Design Team, and Production Staff. The Wardrobe Supervisor coordinates with designers, shop management, and the production team to guarantee their assigned show meets the overarching theatrical vision while ensuring the safety and support of the students. The Wardrobe Supervisor is a well-organized, caring, and focused individual with experience in managing groups of people with varying experience in theater and opera production. The Wardrobe Supervisor reports to the Costume Shop Manager and works closely with Production Management and Stage Management for show-related tasks. This is an overhire position, with opportunities for work to be scheduled as needed by the Costume Department. The position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. RESPONSIBILITIES Collaborate with designers, creative teams, and production crew Delegate responsibilities to all overhire wardrobe crew members, and supervise overhire workers during shifts in the absence of the Costume Shop Manager Attend Load In and Strike, as well as all technical rehearsals and performances Launder rehearsal and production garments, and manage dry cleaning Cultivate safety and comfort in dressing rooms, quick-change areas, and fitting rooms Uphold professional demeanor in challenging situations, especially where students are present Follow all notes documents, paying close attention to shop management\u2019s instructions\u00a0 Additional responsibilities as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS 1 year previous Wardrobe Supervision experience 2 years of Theatrical Wardrobe experience Exceptional verbal and written communication skills appropriate in a professional, educational environment. Basic knowledge of stitching by hand, machine, and serger Team-oriented problem solver with strong collaborative skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience in an academic environment/working with students or young professionals. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours. #LI-Onsite Nights and weekends will be required. Costume Shop hours are 10:00am-5:00pm Monday through Friday. Schedule adjustments may be made due to external venue time constraints when offsite Hourly Rate: $30.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Assistant-Stage-Manager_JR109457": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Assistant Stage Manager", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Concerts & Events Production Department at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Overhire Assistant Stage Managers to join its team. Reporting to the Event Production Manager , the Overhire Assistant Stage Manager is directly responsible for supporting the production of concerts and special events presented by Performing Arts at the New School. The Assistant Stage Manager will support Concerts & Events by facilitating stage moves, backstage operations, and artist entrances during events. They will also coordinate logistics and paperwork with the Stage Manager or Production Manager and assist with soundchecks and technical rehearsals as needed, to remain on schedule and ensure that all events are run smoothly and efficiently.\u00a0 Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. \u00a0 This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Production department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Create, edit, manage and distribute show paperwork in collaboration with the Stage Manager/Production Manager Manage onstage and backstage activities including stage moves and artist wrangling Communicate with creative team, cast and crew Be familiar with all stage management responsibilities on a production Supervise stage crew including student workers (as needed) Manage backstage spaces including backstage wings, green room(s) and artist holding Spike furniture and other items Other duties related to the successful execution of the event as assigned Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS One year previous experience in assistant stage management or three years experience as a production assistant Strong analytical skills and ability to develop creative approaches to complex issues Excellent oral and written communication skills Interpersonal skills that include management, teamwork and collaboration, and conflict resolution Time and task management skills, including re-prioritization and an ability to work graciously under strict time constraints Must be able to remain standing for long periods, bend down and/or lift and move up to 50 pounds repeatedly Experienced or able to learn and develop fluency in The New School\u2019s suite of applications including Google Suite, Microsoft Office, WorkDay, Asimut and 25Live. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \u00a0 Three years previous experience as assistant stage manager, experience as stage manager Familiarity working on concerts and/or special events Ability to step in and call a show Experience reading stage plots Ability to read music WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite SALARY RANGE $30.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Costume-Shop-Assistant--Student-Assistant_JR109461": {"title": "FWS - Costume Shop Assistant, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nPart time\n\nThe Performance Operations \u2013 Costume Department at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as a Student Costume Shop Assistant . The Student Costume Shop Assistant assists the Costume Shop Manager with stock pulls, maintains the costume stock, and runs the stock pulls for faculty and classroom requests. We\u2019re looking for a well-organized, caring, focused individual with experience in group leadership, costume history, costume production, and general knowledge of the New York City Garment District, to support initiatives within the Costume Department . The Student Costume Shop Assistant reports to the Costume Shop Manager and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the fields of costume design and technology.\u00a0 This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards. \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Provide additional support during productions Attend all shifts in person as scheduled by shop management Assist fellow student workers as needed and overhires Act as a shopper for shop management Assist shop management with measurements Take notes in fittings Act on behalf of shop management in urgent or emergency situations Additional responsibilities as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of Federal Work Study award Basic stitching skills (hand and/or machine) Familiarity with fabrics and garment silhouettes Familiarity with costume production and costume design PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts or Parsons degree program Experience working on costumes for personal or production means Experience working as an Assistant Costume Designer or Production Assistant WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. Hourly Rate: $ 18/hour Hours per Week: up to 20 hours per week We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Provide in-person production support by taking notes during fittings, assisting shop management with measurements, and acting as a shopper for shop needs. Support fellow student workers and overhires, step in on behalf of shop management during urgent situations, and handle additional responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant---Indigenous-Practices---Land-Based-Technologies-in-Art---Design-Education_JR109402": {"title": "Research Assistant - Indigenous Practices & Land-Based Technologies in Art & Design Education", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nPart time\n\nPOSITION PURPOSE: This research assistant position will support the development of curricular-based educational opportunities that prioritize Indigenous material practices, relational making methods, and land-based technologies within art and design education at Parsons School of Design. The role includes assisting with research, curriculum development, archival investigation, material experimentation, and community engagement initiatives that center Indigenous making-based knowledge systems and pedagogies.\u00a0 \u00a0 This position will provide the student with valuable experience in collaborative research, pedagogical development, and community-centered design practices within a higher education setting. Through direct engagement with Indigenous methodologies, material practices, and interdisciplinary creative research, the student will strengthen their critical thinking, organizational, and professional skills while contributing to more inclusive and socially engaged approaches to art and design education Duties and Responsibilities Research Indigenous material practices, craft traditions, and land-based technologies. Assist with pedagogical development for workshops, educational programming and learning resources. Engage in archival and visual research related to Indigenous fashion, art, and design methodologies. Compile annotated bibliographies, teaching resources, and reference materials. Assist with material experimentation and documentation processes. Coordinate communication and scheduling with faculty, students, artists, and community collaborators. Support event planning, workshop preparation, and public programming initiatives. Maintain organized records of research findings, project timelines, and educational resources. \u00a0 Qualifications \u2013 Education, Experience, and Skills Current graduate or undergraduate student at Parsons School of Design. Interest in Indigenous studies, art and design education, material culture, fashion, or socially engaged practices. Strong research, writing, and organizational skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively. Experience with archival research, curriculum development, or community-based practices is an asset.\u00a0 WORK MODE: Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This research assistant position will support the development of curricular-based educational opportunities that prioritize Indigenous material practices, relational making methods, and land-based technologies within art and design education at Parsons School of Design. The role includes assisting with research, curriculum development, archival investigation, material experimentation, and community engagement initiatives that center Indigenous making-based knowledge systems and pedagogies. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant----Traces-of-Motherhood--Initiative-Support-Role_JR109404": {"title": "Research Assistant - 'Traces of Motherhood' Initiative Support Role", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nPart time\n\nThe Research Assistant will support Assistant Professor Anna Lerner-Zwick in multiple projects related to her research initiative, \u201cTraces of Motherhood.\u201d The assistant will play a key role in designing and working on a new exciting project in the field between design, medicine and function. This position is designed to be collaborative and immersive. The Research Assistant will be considered an integral part of the project, contributing meaningfully to its outcomes. Assistant Professor Lerner-Zwick is committed to providing a rich learning experience, ensuring the assistant gains substantial knowledge, skills, and insight while actively participating in the research process. Outcomes and Goals for this year: One major project for 2026/2027 focuses on a very exciting collaboration between a maternal fetal doctor in NYC and Anna Lerner-Zwick, redesigning and sampling garments for women in maternity care. In addition to that the work will also be to support the making and development of a collection to get ready for a future art exhibition around the theme of motherhood as well as supporting the next conference preparations. Here more information about the research topic : The research explores \u201ctraces of motherhood\u201d- the intersection of textile, birth, design, and motherhood. It investigates lived experiences of motherhood and artist-life, examining how art portrays women and birth, and how women reflect on motherhood in their practice. Building on scholarship around design systems that center the female body, birthing people, and women\u2019s rights, this work contributes to the underrepresented field of female experience in art and design, particularly suppressed voices tied to birth and care. Through craft, making, and experimental garments, the project uses textiles and material processes to reflect themes of motherhood, identity, and care. \u00a0 Duties & Responsibilities: Conduct independently patternmaking and sewing required for the project Conduct in-depth library and archival research, including collecting, organizing, and synthesizing visual and textual materials relevant to the project\u2019s themes. Assist in craft-based practice, including knitwear and other selected mediums, contributing to the development of creative outcomes. Support the curation, preparation, and documentation of physical creative outcomes, including exhibition preparation and installation. Provide organizational and logistical support for workshops, panel discussions, and other project-related events Contribute to the planning and development of publications (e.g., essays, zines, or books) emerging from the project. Document research processes, project activities, and outcomes for dissemination in both academic and creative contexts. Qualifications \u2013 Education, Experience, and Skills: The ideal candidate needs to be a Fashion Design Student and have good sewing and patternmaking skills: needs to be comfortable to sew on an industrial machine and be able to comfortably adjust and draft patterns and drape on a form. Needs to be committed to reliably meet at once a week for check ins, and to discuss ongoing research Should have a general interest in craft, and themes at the intersection of art, design and motherhood Strong organizational and time-management skills to support research and project development Proficiency in Adobe Creative Suite Experience in designing, drafting, and evaluating surveys to support qualitative and quantitative research Ability to conduct in-depth research, including gathering and curating relevant visuals, texts, and archival materials around a defined topic WORK MODE: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will support Assistant Professor Anna Lerner-Zwick in multiple projects related to her research initiative, \u201cTraces of Motherhood.\u201d The assistant will play a key role in designing and working on a new exciting project in the field between design, medicine and function. Some tasks include patternamking and sewing, library and archival research, organizational support for workshops and program events, among others. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Associate-Director--Audience-Engagement-and-Paid-Media_JR109393-1": {"title": "Associate Director, Audience Engagement and Paid Media", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School \u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW This person will work closely with Enrollment and college leadership to oversee and execute media planning, content distribution, and social media publishing that builds the brand and reputation for The New School and its individual colleges. The role is highly strategic\u2013 the ideal candidate can develop long-tail campaigns that work to support enrollment over time.\u00a0 This person should have a background working with both paid and organic content, and the ability to direct agency partners and set benchmarks. This person will work closely with producers, Creative staff, and college communications leads to generate and publish content that represents the diverse, polyphonic life of The New School. They will also act as a key point of contact for the communications and public affairs teams to manage rapid response and crisis scenarios.\u00a0 While the majority of this person\u2019s time will be dedicated to supporting the Parsons and Performing Arts college, some time will also be committed to supporting Lang, NSSR, centers and institutes, and university-wide initiatives. This position reports to the Assistant Vice President, Audience Development and Media Activation.\u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Planning & Campaign Execution Establish and execute strategic plan for paid and organic media, working collaboratively with leadership in BMC and the Parsons/PA college to set priorities, develop campaigns, and grow our audience of right-fit prospects. Coordinate campaigns with website, SEO, and AIO strategy so efforts cross-pollinate. Content Creation & Platform Management Collaborate with Content, Creative, and Parsons/PA teams to produce social media content that drives recruitment and supports retention, affiliation, and alumni engagement for our current community. Oversee distribution of paid and organic content to social media, YouTube, Google, and other platforms via direct publishing and agency partners. Cross-Functional & Audience Integration Integrate campaigns and organic content distribution with Enrollment, Recruitment, and Student Retention teams. Manage user communities and concerns, collaborating with Media Relations to ensure leadership messages are shared as appropriate on social media and colleges communications teams are prepared to respond. Analytics, Performance & Governance Set KPI benchmarks by platform and interpret and report analytics to inform strategy and tactics. Set policy, train, and advise colleagues on social media best practices. Present trend reports, insights, and research to senior leadership as needed. Keep abreast of current events as well as trends and advances in digital media. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Three+ years in marketing or advertising. Understanding of the marketing funnel and how it applies to student recruitment and retention.\u00a0 Strong grasp of analytics, marketing KPIS, benchmarks, and a strategic mindset to turn data into actionable insights.\u00a0 Strategic mindset and the ability to iterate based on data and insights. Familiarity with social media publishing software(s) such as Sprout, ContentStudio, etc. Experience managing paid media campaigns across platforms and modalities.\u00a0 Experience creating and executing strategic brand campaigns.\u00a0 Skilled team leader comfortable with dotted-line reporting structures. Working knowledge of AI and implications for search and inbound queries. High integrity and adherence to brand guidelines. Ability to engage a unique and diverse community and its concerns. Experience in community management and crisis communications. Excellent interpersonal skills; able to take feedback and work collaboratively.\u00a0 Sensitive to the speed required for social content, with a healthy respect for deadlines. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Higher education experience a plus. Experience managing high-profile brands. Experience establishing a brand presence on emerging platforms. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $83,500 - $90,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness \u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/SAS-Making-Center-Sound-Office-Tech-Assistant_JR109453": {"title": "SAS Making Center Sound Office Tech Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Media Studies Film Office within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 \u00a0 Student Sound Office Techs: Support the Sound Office with the maintenance, organization, and day-to-day operation of sound equipment and resources. Assist faculty and students with the setup, use, and proper care of sound equipment, including microphones, recorders, and accessories. Maintain and support Sound Suits and recording booths, ensuring spaces and equipment are clean, organized, and ready for use. Assist faculty and students with the proper use and basic troubleshooting of Sound Suits and recording booths. Assist with equipment check-ins, check-outs, setup, and inventory. Support the use of additional production equipment, including lighting and camera equipment for in-class use. Use online equipment management and scheduling platforms to support Sound Office operations. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be an active continuing matriculated student Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE On-Campus Position \u00a0-\u00a0Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate:\u00a0\u00a0 $17.00hr/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provided customer service and front-desk support in a diverse and inclusive learning environment, assisting students, faculty, and staff with equipment reservations, resource requests, and departmental services. Maintained shop, classroom, and equipment spaces through organization, upkeep, troubleshooting, and equipment management, while supporting daily operations, training sessions, and special projects. Assisted faculty and students with classroom and Film Office equipment, including check-in/check-out procedures, technical support, and maintaining equipment resources. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Research-Assistant---Scholarly-Fashion-Studies-Book-Proposal-Assistant_JR109399": {"title": "Research Assistant - Scholarly Fashion Studies Book Proposal Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nThe Research Assistant will support the research development and preparation of a scholarly fashion studies book proposal focused on swimwear. Building on an established theoretical framework examining swimwear through the intersections of diversity, social justice, and environmental impact, the position will assist the Lead Faculty Researcher in advancing the project from existing research and annotated bibliographies toward thematic synthesis, structural development, and manuscript planning. The Research Assistant will contribute to organizing and synthesizing existing scholarship, developing the project\u2019s thematic architecture, researching appropriate publishing formats and requirements, and supporting preparation of materials for submission to prospective academic publishers. Duties & Responsibilities Review existing annotated bibliographies and research notes to identify, organize, and synthesize key themes relevant to the book project. Support refinement of the book\u2019s focus and thematic structure through literature mapping and comparative analysis of existing scholarship. Assist with evaluating potential publication formats, including a single-author monograph and edited volume, and support development of corresponding chapter structures. Support preparation of the book proposal by organizing theoretical frameworks, research findings, chapter concepts, and related project materials. Research publisher requirements and proposal guidelines, including those of Intellect Publishing and other appropriate academic presses. Format and organize proposal materials in accordance with publisher submission requirements. Depending on the final publication strategy, assist with research and organizational support for a potential special issue or edited-volume pathway. Identify and catalogue potential multidisciplinary scholars and contributors whose work addresses relevant gaps in swimwear scholarship. Maintain bibliographies, research files, project documentation, and milestone tracking to support the timely development of the project. Qualifications \u2013 Education, Experience, and Skills Currently enrolled in a graduate program (Master\u2019s or Ph.D.) in Fashion Studies, Art History, Cultural Studies, Sociology, the Humanities, or a closely related field. Strong written communication, organizational, analytical, and research skills. Familiarity with literature reviews, annotated bibliographies, and scholarly research practices. Ability to work independently, manage multiple tasks, and meet agreed-upon project timelines. Strong attention to detail and ability to organize research materials and project documentation. Preferred Qualifications Research interest or prior experience in critical fashion studies, sustainability, body politics, social justice, or related interdisciplinary areas. Familiarity with academic publishing processes, book proposals, or editorial workflows. WORK MODE: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will support the research development and preparation of a scholarly fashion studies book proposal focused on swimwear. Building on an established theoretical framework examining swimwear through the intersections of diversity, social justice, and environmental impact, the position will assist the Lead Faculty Researcher in advancing the project from existing research and annotated bibliographies toward thematic synthesis, structural development, and manuscript planning. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Research-Assistant----TBD-Design-Development---Research-Assistant_JR109400": {"title": "Research Assistant - [TBD]Design Development & Research Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nPosition Purpose: This position supports the development of [TBD] Design, an emerging paradigm that treats structural conditions - legal, regulatory, institutional, economic, historical, and cultural - as active design material rather than fixed constraints. The Research Assistant will support both the theoretical development of this framework (contributing to an academic book chapter) and the design of practical tools that allow business strategists and innovators to apply it. This is an opportunity for a graduate student with interdisciplinary interests in design, policy, and business strategy to contribute directly to original scholarship and co-develop a new practitioner-facing framework from the ground up.\u00a0 Duties & Responsibilities Conduct literature review and source verification across design thinking, institutional theory, regulatory studies, and related fields\u00a0 Research and help document real-world case studies illustrating the interaction between policy/regulatory conditions and innovation outcomes Assist in designing a practical framework or tool (e.g., a \"Conditions Audit\" or \"Conditions Map\") that operationalizes Conditions Design principles for business practitioners Support prototyping and iteration of the tool (e.g., in Figma, Miro, or similar), including structuring content, visual layout, and usability considerations Compile and organize research materials, citations, and supporting documentation Participate in regular check-ins to discuss findings, direction, and revisions Qualifications \u2013 Education, Experience, and Skills Coursework or project experience in institutional analysis, regulatory studies, administrative law, or public policy Experience designing frameworks, canvases, workshop tools, or other structured decision-support artifacts Familiarity with design thinking methodology and tools (e.g., customer journey mapping, service design, systems thinking) Proficiency with visual/prototyping tools such as Figma or Miro Interest or background in sustainability, ESG, circular economy, or social innovation WORK MODE: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions .\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $31.37/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: This position supports the development of [TBD] Design, an emerging paradigm that treats structural conditions - legal, regulatory, institutional, economic, historical, and cultural - as active design material rather than fixed constraints. The Research Assistant will support both the theoretical development of this framework (contributing to an academic book chapter) and the design of practical tools that allow business strategists and innovators to apply it. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Parsons-Scholars---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109355-1": {"title": "Parsons Scholars - Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nParsons Scholars was founded in 1997 and provides NYC, financially eligible high school students with a 2.5 year art and design scholarship. We are seeking student workers to support Parsons Scholars\u2019 programming, focusing on mentorship for high school participants, as well as administrative and design support. Programming primarily runs on Saturdays from 8:40 AM - 5:40 PM, as well as offering weekday support for scholars. The fall semester will run from August 22, 2026 to December 12, 2026. The spring semester will run from January 16, 2026 to May 29, 2026. Students applying for this role should also apply to the Parsons Scholars Course Assistant role for a total estimated 10 hours per week.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated undergraduate student Federal Work Study eligible Organizational and communication skills Design skills (InDesign, Illustrator, Photoshop) Interest in art and design education Interest in equity and access in art and design PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with high school students Bilingual in English and either Spanish or Mandarin WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $21.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Parsons Scholars Mentors will support Youth Advocates for Saturday cohort sessions with material organization, preparation, and facilitation, provide one-on-one mentorship with high school students, document programming through photographs and written blog posts. They will also be expected to help organize and maintain inventory room, including restocking supplies as well as designing digital and print material for recruitment, public events, and the annual lookbook. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Social-Media-Student-Assistant_JR109366": {"title": "Social Media Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nThe School of Fashion seeks Social Media Student Workers for Fall \u201826/Spring '27 to help shape and implement a content strategy aimed at reaching external audiences through social media such as Instagram. This candidate will help shape engagement through building a strong internal community that supports students, alums & Program activities. This candidate may work across programs for full school-wide coverage. As such, they will work with the Program Administrators. Hiring Programs: AAS: FD - Fashion Design and FM - Fashion Marketing & Communications MFA: Fashion Design MFA: FDS - Fashion Design & Society and TEX - Textiles MPS: FBM - Fashion Business Management Responsibilities will include Understanding and contributing to the departmental content strategy Demonstrate skill in graphic design, photography and video content creation Designing social media strategies following the academic calendar Managing, creating and publishing original, high quality content on social platforms Schedule posts pre-approved by the Program Directors and Program Administrators Gather content\u00a0 Administering programs social media accounts to ensure up-to-date content Establish social media guidelines and policies Planning, producing, and publishing around 3 posts per week for the @parsonsaasfashion social media account Documents (via photo + writing / tagging) and promotes events, curricular activities, and student work. Responding to replies and direct messages on Instagram, et al. Collecting and analyzing data generated by these efforts, and suggesting changes in strategy based on that data A weekly check-in meeting Hourly Rate : $18 per hour Number of hours per week: 5-10 hours per week WORK MODE On-Campus Position - expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Off-Campus Position - expected to work off-campus (fashion show or event site) during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Name of supervisor: Catherine Cacho-Leary, Associate Director of Operations, School of Fashion Contact information for supervisor: acholec@newschool.edu Responsibilities: This position supports the department\u2019s social media and content strategy by creating and managing high-quality digital content. Responsibilities include developing social media plans aligned with the academic calendar, producing approximately three posts per week, and maintaining program social media accounts. The role also documents and promotes events, student work, and curricular activities through photography, video, writing, and other visual content. Student workers will engage with followers, monitor social media activity, and analyze performance data to recommend improvements to content strategy. The position also includes regular collaboration with Program Directors and Administrators through pre-approved content and weekly check-in meetings. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Event-Support-Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109371": {"title": "Event Support Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. EVENT SUPPORT STUDENT WORKER, SOF The Event Support Student Worker will support the Program Administrator working with the School of Fashion and the program faculty as assigned with general event needs such as the planning, setup, and breakdown of events during the year. This may include the SOF fashion shows, open studios, program recognition ceremonies, and material donations. This role includes both regularly scheduled and variable hours, depending on event needs. Evening and occasional weekend availability is required. RESPONSIBILITIES Support the School of Fashion programs and the Program Administrators with event planning, preparation, and execution. Support Staff and Faculty with projects for SOF events, materials donations, fashion shows and other productions. Welcome guests at events, manage check-in, provide information, and help direct foot traffic. Pick up and deliver items for event set up; assist with breakdown of events, including cleanup, room reset, and return of supplies to SOF operations hub. Coordinate with external vendors (catering, rental services, etc) and internal operations teams (security, facilities, events, IT) as related to work tasks. Support faculty and staff with materials donations, get materials unpacked and stored properly. Communicate proactively with SOF staff regarding scheduling, availability, and any potential conflicts for event support shifts. Assist event photographer with setup and capturing the event. Provide DJing services, play music playlist, confirm clean song versions. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ability to perform physical tasks consistent with a Medium exertion level, including regularly lifting and carrying up to 25 pounds and occasionally lifting up to 40 pounds; must be able to bend, reach, and lift fabric rolls to shoulder height. Strong communication skills with both internal and external stakeholders Attention to detail and ability to follow through on assigned tasks. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. Off-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work off-campus (fashion show or event site) during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $21.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The position supports the School of Fashion with event planning, preparation, and execution, including fashion shows, productions, and other programs. Responsibilities include welcoming guests, managing check-in, assisting with event setup and breakdown, and coordinating with vendors and internal teams. The role also provides support with materials donations, event photography, and organizing supplies. Student workers are expected to communicate with staff regarding scheduling and event support needs. Additionally, the position may provide DJ services and music support, including managing playlists and ensuring appropriate song versions are used. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Parsons-Scholars---Course-Assistant_JR109356": {"title": "Parsons Scholars - Course Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nParsons Scholars was founded in 1997 and provides NYC, financially eligible high school students with a 2.5 year art and design scholarship. We are seeking course assistants to support Parsons Scholars\u2019 Unfathomable Futures and Space and Materiality courses. Courses will take place on Saturdays, beginning August 29, 2026 to December 12, 2026. These course assistants will carry on to support Parsons Scholars\u2019 Drawing and Imaging, Integrative Studio I, and Integrative Seminar I courses in spring 2026 from January 23, 2027 to May 8, 2027. Students applying for this role should also apply to the Parsons Scholars Student Assistant Specialist role for a total estimated 10 hours per week.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated undergraduate student Federal Work Study eligible Organizational and communication skills Interest in art and design education Interest in equity and access in art and design PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with high school students Bilingual fluency in Spanish and/or Mandarin WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-Onsite Hourly Rate: $25.13 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Parsons Scholars Course Assistants support teaching and learning outcomes through non-instructional activities for Parsons Scholars FY courses: Space & Materiality, Unfathomable Futures, Integrative Studio I, Integrative Studio II, and Drawing & Imaging. Duties may include: attending class, making photocopies, demonstrating use of equipment, proctoring exams, collecting assignments, assisting with classroom set-up, uploading course content online, tracking students\u2019 participation online, assisting students having difficulty with online environment, and grading of student work. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Student-Lab-Assistant_JR109428": {"title": "Student Lab Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nThe New School\u2019s Innovation Center is seeking enthusiastic, curious, and motivated undergraduate students to join our team as Student Lab Assistants .\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 The Innovation Center explores the intersection of artificial intelligence (AI), extended reality (XR), emerging media, creative technology, design, and future-facing practices . Through the Center\u2019s AI, XR, and emerging technology initiatives, students and faculty engage with new tools and modes of creative practice through workshops, courses, research, experimentation, public programs, hackathons, and collaborative projects. \u00a0 Student Lab Assistants work closely with the Senior Director, Innovation Center team, faculty, and fellow students to support the Center\u2019s programs, spaces, technologies, and projects. The position offers an opportunity to develop hands-on experience with emerging technologies while contributing to a multidisciplinary community of students, faculty, researchers, artists, designers, and external partners. \u00a0 We are particularly interested in students who are curious about AI, immersive and spatial media, interactive experiences, creative computing, digital storytelling, and emerging forms of human-computer interaction . \u00a0 Candidates are not expected to have expertise in every technology or area listed below . We are looking for students who bring relevant skills, intellectual curiosity, strong communication abilities, and a willingness to learn new tools and approaches. Student roles and responsibilities will be shaped around each individual\u2019s background, experience, skills, and interests. \u00a0 Responsibilities: \u00a0 AI, XR, and Emerging Technology Support Explore, test, evaluate, and document emerging technologies, platforms, applications, and creative tools used by the Innovation Center. Support experimentation and projects involving generative AI, immersive media, virtual and augmented reality, spatial computing, interactive media, creative coding, 3D environments, and other emerging technologies . Assist students and faculty in learning and using Innovation Center technologies and platforms. Facilitate access to equipment and resources during open lab hours, workshops, classroom visits, demonstrations, and special programs. Provide basic technical troubleshooting and user support. Assist with prototyping, experimentation, user testing, research, and documentation of Innovation Center projects. Research new tools, platforms, creative practices, and developments in AI, XR, emerging media, and related fields. Participate in testing and evaluating technologies for potential use in teaching, learning, creative practice, and public programming. Help maintain equipment, software, digital resources, and Innovation Center workspaces. \u00a0 Workshops, Learning, and Community Support Assist with the preparation and facilitation of workshops related to AI, XR, immersive media, creative technology, and emerging tools. Support students during open lab hours and hands-on learning sessions. Help develop tutorials, demonstrations, resource guides, and other learning materials. Assist faculty and classes visiting the Innovation Center. Help create a welcoming and accessible environment for students with different levels of technical experience. Explain technical concepts and tools clearly to students, faculty, and visitors from a wide range of disciplines. \u00a0 Projects and Events Support Innovation Center projects, research initiatives, exhibitions, demonstrations, hackathons, design jams, talks, conferences, and public events. Assist with event preparation, technology setup, participant support, documentation, and event logistics. Collaborate with Innovation Center staff, student teams, faculty, and external partners on interdisciplinary projects. Support project organization, scheduling, research, documentation, and other administrative needs. Assist with tracking project progress and maintaining shared project resources. \u00a0 Media, Communications, and Documentation Help document Innovation Center projects, workshops, events, student work, and research through photography, video, and other media. Assist with the creation of visual and multimedia materials for the Innovation Center\u2019s website, social media, presentations, events, and public communications. Produce or edit content including graphics, short-form video, photography, web content, and social media posts. Help organize and maintain digital archives of Innovation Center projects and activities. Contribute ideas for communicating emerging technology, student projects, research, and Innovation Center initiatives to broader audiences. \u00a0 Minimum Qualifications: Currently enrolled as an undergraduate student at The New School. Demonstrated interest in one or more of the following areas: artificial intelligence, emerging technology, immersive media, XR/AR/VR, interaction and visual design, digital media, creative technology, creative coding, game design, storytelling, video, communications, or multimedia production . Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to communicate effectively with students, faculty, staff, and visitors from different disciplines and levels of technical experience. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work independently as well as collaboratively within a team. Willingness to learn new technologies, platforms, and workflows. Ability to work responsibly within Innovation Center policies, procedures, and equipment guidelines. Ability to manage multiple priorities in a dynamic and fast-paced environment. \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications : Photography, video production, motion graphics, audio, or multimedia editing. Generative AI tools and creative AI workflows. Creative and development tools such as Unity, Unreal Engine, Blender, Adobe Creative Cloud, TouchDesigner, Figan or related platforms. Interactive media, creative coding, web development, game development, or physical computing. AI-assisted image, video, audio, design, research, or prototyping tools. XR technologies, including virtual reality, augmented reality, mixed reality, or spatial computing. VR headsets and immersive hardware such as Meta Quest or similar platforms. UX, interaction design, prototyping, or user testing. Web publishing and digital communication platforms. Workshop facilitation, peer tutoring, technical support, or student mentoring. Event production, exhibitions, hackathons, or public programming. Research into emerging technologies, creative practices, or technology trends. \u00a0 A strong candidate will be curious, collaborative, proactive, creative, dependable, and comfortable learning through experimentation . Excellent candidates will also demonstrate patience and enthusiasm when helping others explore unfamiliar technologies. \u00a0 WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17\u2013$19 per hour \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The position involves exploring, testing, evaluating, and documenting emerging technologies such as AI, XR, immersive media, creative coding, and 3D environments. Student Lab Assistants provide technical support to students, faculty, and visitors by assisting with equipment, software, troubleshooting, and open lab activities. They support workshops, classes, tutorials, demonstrations, and hands-on learning while helping create an inclusive and welcoming environment for users with varying levels of technical experience. Assistants also contribute to Innovation Center projects, research, events, exhibitions, hackathons, and other programs through technology setup, project coordination, documentation, and participant support. Additionally, they help create and organize media and communications materials, including photography, video, graphics, web content, social media, and digital archives. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Red-Dog-Productions_JR109430": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Red Dog Productions", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks student staff for the Red Dog Productions videography team. The assignments include University-wide live events, covering symposia and panel discussions hosted in the auditoriums as well as documenting conferences, exhibitions, social receptions, concerts and lectures. Red Dog Productions will train individuals and they will work alongside producers and faculty advisors to create professional quality, original content that can be added to career portfolio and resumes, while gaining professional experience. Red Dog Productions is currently seeking great candidates to perform the duties of the Production Crew. This is a non-academic worker student staff position that reports to the Manager of Red Dog Productions. RESPONSIBILITIES Shoot HD video and set up equipment including tripod and mics Using editing software, transfer and compress files; edit footage Audio mixing Lighting setup and design for interviews, streaming and still photography Web and social media for video and photography Training in different areas as needed MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current FT active student status Knowledge of film and video equipment Technical knowledge of film and video cameras, lenses, lighting, audio, and support equipment\u00a0\u00a0 Experience with film and video production procedures and protocols, set ups, studio and equipment maintenance Film editing and software experience Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Production experience\u00a0 WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE\u00a0: $17/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * For full consideration, please include a Resume and Cover Letter in your application. Responsibilities: Shoot HD video and set up equipment including tripod and mics. Using editing software, transfer and compress files and edit footage. Create audio mixing, lighting setup/design for interviews, streaming and still photography. Create web and social media for video and photography. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Mannes-Dean-s-Office--Student-Assistant_JR109434": {"title": "FWS - Mannes Dean\u2019s Office, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Mannes School of Music Dean's Office at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as the Mannes Dean\u2019s Office Student Assistant . We\u2019re looking for a responsible and motivated individual to support initiatives within the Mannes Academics area . This position reports to the Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music, and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in music performance or music education. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards. RESPONSIBILITIES Set up classroom technology, assisting faculty with logging into computers and accessing files. Coordinate with the Technical Services Office to ensure classroom technology needs are met, including retrieving, setting up, and returning loaned gear.\u00a0 Provide the Mannes Dean\u2019s Office with any general office assistance as needed, including delivering/receiving equipment and scores\u00a0 Additional duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current student in Mannes (BM, MM, or PDPL). Previous administrative experience. Recipient of Federal Work Study award. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17.00 Hours per Week : 1-3 hours per week on average SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume and cover letter outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, and their Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Set up classroom technology and assist faculty with computer login and file access while coordinating with the TSO office to retrieve, configure, and return loaned gear. Provide general administrative support to the Mannes Dean\u2019s Office through the delivery and receipt of scores and equipment alongside additional duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Drama-Bank-Street-Programs--Student-Assistant_JR109446": {"title": "Drama/Bank Street Programs, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nThe School of Drama at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Drama/Bank Street Programs, Student Assistant. We\u2019re looking for an organized and enthusiastic individual with a self-starter disposition interested in all aspects of drama production & operations to support the School of Drama\u2019s programs at 151 Bank Street . This is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in non-profit, performing arts, or higher education administration.. RESPONSIBILITIES Support daily operations within the School of Drama and Production programs with scheduling, paperwork, database upkeep, and general organizational/operational tasks Station Bank Street\u2019s Welcome Hub in Room 102 to answer student, faculty and visitor questions Assist with faculty copy/IT needs, mail distribution, stocking supplies, program printing, special events, and mainstage marketing support.\u00a0 Generate information and update College of Performing Arts Calendar; Post monthly on Call Board Support needs for classes / small projects Manage supply orders and restocking (tape, first aid, etc.) Create and circulate materials for the Arts Letters Maintain photo archives, scripts and production books Maintain physical supplies in Bank Street as requested Additional responsibilities as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ability to commit to a consistent weekly schedule. Excellent written and verbal communication, attention to detail, and group and independent problem solving skills Working knowledge of Google Suite: Drive, Calendar, etc General knowledge of theater practices and protocols Commitment to diversity and inclusion PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program At least one semester at The New School/other university or equivalent professional experience. Interest in operations and production, administration, and/or creative technologies. Current student enrolled in a College of Performing Arts program Recipient of a Federal Work Study award. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: Hourly Rate: $17.00 Hours per Week: up to 10-20 on average SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 Applicants must submit a cover letter, resume, and copy of their Federal Work Study award through their application. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.\u200b Responsibilities: Station at the Bank Street Welcome Hub to manage daily administrative operations, scheduling, database upkeep, and front-desk reception for the School of Drama and Production programs. Assist with faculty IT needs, supply inventory, calendar management, event marketing, production archiving, and the creation of Arts Letters materials. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/FWS-Student-Assistant---Admission-Operations_JR109431": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant - Admission Operations", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nThe Admission Operations office seeks motivated students to provide general office support. Responsibilities include but are not limited to the following: Open, scan, and process incoming mail Provide administrative support to the Operations team Consolidate applicant and prospect records Perform duplicate checks for applicant records in our CRM Respond to prospective applicant inquiries via email Maintain current knowledge of admission and student services areas Assist in admission-related mailings Participate in special projects as needed Additional duties may be assigned Minimum Qualifications: Professional demeanor and ability to maintain confidentiality Excellent written and verbal communication skills Superior organizational skills Energetic, personable and team-oriented Strong work ethic; Ability to take direction and follow through on jobs until completion Experience working independently with limited hands-on supervision Ability to take the initiative and remain flexible Working knowledge of Google Suite and Microsoft Office Suite (Excel, Word). Preferred Qualifications : Preference will be given to students with Federal Work-study awards Hourly Rate : $17\u00a0 Hourly Rate 10-20 hours a week \u00a0 Work Mode : Hybrid/Remote \u00a0\u2013\u00a0Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely, with the occasional on-campus presence required with\u00a0advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their\u00a0best judgment\u00a0to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce Responsibilities: The Admission Operations Student Assistant will provide administrative support to the Operations team. They will be expected to perform duplicate checks for applicant records in our CRM, answer student emails in a professional manor and other duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Affairs-Assistant---Dean-of-Students-Office_JR109445": {"title": "Student Affairs Assistant \u2013 Dean of Students Office", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director of Student Conduct and Community Standards, the Student Affairs Assistant will work as part of a high-impact student cohort supporting key leadership initiatives, event logistics, research projects, and administrative operations for the Dean of Students Office. The Student Affairs Assistant will have opportunities to collaborate with professional staff and fellow student employees, engage directly with the New School community, and contribute to programs and resources that promote a welcoming, inclusive, and connected campus experience. This role is ideal for a student interested in higher education, student affairs, and community engagement and is eager to develop professional skills while making a meaningful contribution to the student experience. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist in the planning, promotion, and execution of monthly Dean of Students Office Hours and divisional student engagement events. Provide logistical and administrative support for the Faculty in Residence (FIR) program and related initiatives. Assist with collecting data on various\u00a0 student affairs topics. Collaborate within a four-person student cohort on short- and long-term special projects assigned by the Dean of Students Office. Provide occasional reception coverage, front-desk customer service, and visitor greeting during staff lunch breaks, illness, or high-volume periods. Staff events, team meetings, and office hours in person as required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This Position requires a Federal Work-Study Award Proficiency in Canva, Google Suite, and Microsoft Office applications. Can work with The New School\u2019s branding and style guides to create a suite of coherent collateral across Student Success PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience in event coordination, student leadership, customer service, or administrative office support. Familiarity with research methods and basic project management toolsets. Demonstrated commitment to community building, inclusivity, and student engagement. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $18-$19/ hr Diversity Statement: \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Questions can be emailed to baholdie@newschool.edu but applications must be submitted through the New School WorkDay Jobs Portal for an interview to be scheduled. Responsibilities: The Student Affairs Assistant will work as part of a high-impact student cohort supporting key leadership initiatives, event logistics, research projects, and administrative operations for the Dean of Students Office. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Success-Hub---Student-Assistant--FWS-_JR109405": {"title": "Student Success Hub - Student Assistant (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nStudent Success is dedicated to providing students with a dynamic student life experience that complements their curricular experiences and cultivates their intellectual curiosity. We work collaboratively with parts of the university to equip students with resources and opportunities that allow them to thrive both academically and personally. Our goal is to foster a sense of belonging and help students succeed in an ever-changing world.\u00a0 The Student Success hub is our collaborative work space that houses several units under the Student Success umbrella. Reporting to the Manager of Operations, the Student Office Assistant will be the first point of contact for visitors to the Student Success Hub, providing customer service to our internal and external clients. The Student Office Assistant will also be responsible for assisting in general office tasks and ad hoc projects.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Greet visitors who enter the office space and assist them with the check in process Notify appropriate staff member of guest arrival\u00a0 Provide general information to students and other visitors of Student Success areas Assist with picking up and dropping off mail and packages from mailroom Support with administrative tasks like printing, photocopying, and data entry Assist with inventory of office supplies Ad hoc projects as assigned by Director of Operations and Manager of Operations \u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled as a New School Student who qualifies for Federal Work Study Ability to provide a high level of customer service to our diverse community Ability to work a minimum of 7 hours per week during the hours of 9-5, Monday through Friday PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Enjoys problem solving Familiarity with navigating The New School\u2019s resources Ability to handle multiple tasks at once in busy environments Ability to demonstrate strong organizational skills \u00a0 WORK MODE \u00a0 \u25cf\u00a0 On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. \u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17-19 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Please include your hours of availability with your resume and cover letter .\u00a0 Responsibilities: The Student Office Assistant will be the first point of contact for visitors to the Student Success Hub, providing customer service to our internal and external clients. The Student Office Assistant will also be responsible for assisting in general office tasks and ad hoc projects. \u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Mannes-Techniques-of-Music--Tutor-Generalist_JR109444": {"title": "Mannes Techniques of Music, Tutor Generalist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nThe Mannes School of Music at the College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Tutor Generalists. We\u2019re looking for patient and responsible individuals to support instruction. This position reports to Part-time Associate Teaching Professor Christopher Park, and is an ideal position for students who are interested in careers in Music and education. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. RESPONSIBILITIES Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator Respond to email inquiries regarding tutoring assignments in a timely manner Submit timesheets and tutoring session confirmation forms in a timely manner Schedule tutoring sessions with assigned tutees Additional responsibilities as assigned to assist other students in out-of-class by tutoring and answering questions about course content taught in class. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS For each class you wish to tutor, minimum qualifications are as follows: Completed or tested out of class Excellent content knowledge in subject matter Excellent verbal and written communication skills Ability to patiently and efficiently provide instructional support to other students Ability to self-schedule tutoring sessions, once assigned PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of a Federal Work Study Award Current student in a Mannes School of Music or Performer-Composer degree program WORK MODE On-Campus Position - E mployees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $29.20 Hours per Week: Up to 10, as needed This is a SENS-UAW unit position as per the collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator, providing out-of-class support and answering questions regarding course content taught in class. Schedule tutoring sessions, respond promptly to email inquiries, and submit required timesheets and confirmation forms in a timely manner alongside additional responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Jazz---Contemporary-Music--Tutor-Generalist_JR109429": {"title": "Jazz & Contemporary Music, Tutor Generalist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nINTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH The School of Jazz & Contemporary Music at the College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Tutor Generalists. We\u2019re looking for patient and responsible individuals to support instruction. This position reports to Part-time Associate Teaching Professor Ed MacEachen, and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in Jazz & Contemporary Music and education. This is a SENS-UAW unit position as per the collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. RESPONSIBILITIES Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator Respond to email inquiries regarding tutoring assignments in a timely manner Submit timesheets and tutoring session confirmation forms in a timely manner Schedule tutoring sessions with assigned tutees Additional responsibilities as assigned to assist other students in out-of-class tutoring, and answering questions about course content taught in class. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS For each class you wish to tutor, minimum qualifications are as follows: Completed or tested out of class\u00a0 Obtain faculty recommendation Excellent content knowledge in subject matter Excellent verbal and written communication skills Ability to patiently and efficiently provide instructional support to other students Ability to self-schedule tutoring sessions, once assigned PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of a Federal Work Study Award Current student in a School of Jazz & Contemporary Music or Performer-Composer degree program WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $29.20 Hours per Week: Up to 10, or as needed We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume and cover letter outlining their skills and experience concerning this position; and their Federal Work Study award letter if applicable Submit SJCM Tutoring Application Form: https://forms.gle/jnyoJvc4rsejVQ6u8 For each subject you wish to tutor, obtain a faculty recommendation to be sent to Ed MacEachen at Maceache@newschool.edu Responsibilities: Tutor students in approved subjects as assigned by the Tutoring Coordinator, assisting with out-of-class coursework and answering questions regarding course content. Manage session scheduling, maintain prompt email communication, and submit required timesheets and confirmation forms in a timely manner alongside additional assigned duties. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Theater---Opera-Production--Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109424": {"title": "FWS - Theater & Opera Production, Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Performance Operations area at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Theater & Opera Production Student Assistant Specialist. We\u2019re looking for self-sufficient, adaptable, hard-working individuals to support initiatives within the Production area. This position reports to the Producer of Theater and Opera and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in Non-Profit Arts Management or Entrepreneurship or Technical/Theatrical Production. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist Production Management with multiple fall/spring projects, including but not limited to Mannes Opera and School of Drama BFA and MFA productions.\u00a0 Maintain campus access records to confirm that temporary employees have building access for all work-related calls. Schedule design, production, or various other meetings for assigned projects - taking notes and distributing meeting minutes. Attend and support required load-ins, strikes, run-throughs, technical rehearsals, and dress rehearsals as assigned. Schedule rehearsal and performance space usage in coordination with production, academic & university teams. Independently lead small-scale projects. Additional responsibilities as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Exceptional written and verbal communication skills. Interpersonal skills that allow the individual to function in a variety of settings among widely different groups of people. A unique combination of creative and analytical talents. The ability to work productively and graciously under strict time constraints. Flexibility and a \u201cteam player\u201d sensibility. Enthusiasm to learn the operations of carpentry/rigging, lighting, sound, carpentry/props, costumes, ground plans, and light plots. Recipient of Federal Work Study award. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS BFA Student in Dramatic Arts. MFA Student in Contemporary Theater Performance. MA Student in Arts Management and Entrepreneurship degree program. MM Student in Voice. \u00a0 WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $18.00 per hour Hours per Week: up to 15-20, not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek while classes are in session SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, as well as a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Assist Production Management across fall and spring Mannes Opera and School of Drama productions by coordinating venue scheduling, meeting logistics, and temporary employee access records. Attend and support key production milestones including load-ins, technical rehearsals, and strikes while independently leading small-scale projects and handling additional responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/InsideSchools-FWS-Student-Assistant_JR109411-1": {"title": "InsideSchools FWS Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. InsideSchools, a project of the Center for New York City Affairs, is seeking a skilled researcher and writer to support the education team. Serving the largest school district in the country, our education team is committed to helping improve the quality of New York City schools for all families, especially communities in poverty and of color. For two decades, our InsideSchools website has been a trusted resource for caregivers and students seeking information about NYC\u2019s 1,800 public schools, with more than 1 million users visiting our site every year. The education team offers policy analysis, reports, and public commentary to inform system change and school improvement. InsideSchools also supports immigrant families to navigate the NYC public school system. We facilitate workshops and provide one-on-one support at shelters and in partnership with community-based organizations.\u00a0 This work-study position is for 10 to 20 hours per week and will report to the Director of Education Policy and InsideSchools and the InsideSchools Program Manager. RESPONSIBILITIES The Student Assistant will contribute to building the capacity of InsideSchools to more effectively and efficiently serve NYC parents and students. The position will support the education team\u2019s major projects. Responsibilities include: Write descriptions of over 1,800 NYC early childhood centers, elementary schools, middle schools, and high schools. Elevate the voices of middle and high school students through youth-centered services.\u00a0 Support newly arrived migrant families to navigate the NYC public school system. Engage and empower parents. Collect input from school communities to inform content and programming. Develop comprehensive resources to support public school families throughout the K-12 experience. Collect and conduct data analyses of NYC education data, research individual schools, and draft narrative content for school profiles. Support the design, collection, and analysis of data, including surveys, analytics, interviews, and focus groups, etc. Answer questions from public school families, including working with the team to monitor comments and inquiries. Research educational topics and draft content for InsideSchools website and policy reports. Conduct and present literature reviews and data analyses related to NYC education that guide the education policy agenda and program implementation. Assist in planning and implementing workshops for migrant families, events, and focus groups. Attend education team meetings. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current full-time New School student with a Federal Work Study (FWS) award as part of their financial aid package for the 2026-27 academic year.\u00a0 Strong interest in the public education system. An understanding of or interest in learning more about the most pressing issues in urban education, particularly the relationship between families and schools. Strong research and analytical skills.\u00a0 Excellent writing and communication skills. Capacity to work independently and as part of a team. Spanish, French, or Chinese language skills are a plus (not required). PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS We are looking for individuals who have experience in one or more of the following areas: Data analysis Marketing and outreach Qualitative and quantitative research\u00a0 Social media\u00a0 Writing Project management Teaching, training, or curriculum development Youth development WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI HOURLY RATE\u00a0 $17 - $19 per hour\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Student Assistant will support the InsideSchools team through research, writing, data analysis, and community engagement to help improve educational access for NYC public school families. Responsibilities include researching and writing school profiles and educational resources, analyzing education data, assisting with workshops and outreach for newly arrived migrant families, collecting feedback from students and families, and contributing to policy research and content that informs programs and advocacy. This position offers hands-on experience at the intersection of education, research, and community engagement.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/InsideSchools-FWS-Design-Student-Assistant_JR109412": {"title": "InsideSchools FWS Design Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. InsideSchools, a project of the Center for New York City Affairs, is seeking a skilled design assistant to support the education team. Serving the largest school district in the country, our education team is committed to helping improve the quality of New York City schools for all families, especially communities in poverty and of color. For two decades, our InsideSchools website has been a trusted resource for caregivers and students seeking information about NYC\u2019s 1,800 public schools, with more than 1 million users visiting our site every year. The education team offers policy analysis, reports, and public commentary to inform system change and school improvement. Since last summer, InsideSchools has supported newly arrived migrant families living in shelters to navigate the NYC public school system. We have facilitated workshops and provided one-on-one support at shelters and in partnership with community-based organizations.\u00a0 This work-study position is for 10 to 20 hours per week and will report to the Director of Education Policy and InsideSchools and the InsideSchools Program Manager. RESPONSIBILITIES The design assistant will support the education team\u2019s major projects including: Providing descriptions of over 1,800 NYC early childhood centers, elementary schools, middle schools, and high schools. Elevating the voices of middle and high school students through youth-centered services.\u00a0 Engaging and empowering parents. Collecting input from school communities to inform content and programming. Developing comprehensive resources to support public school families throughout the K-12 experience. Developing and supporting partnerships and training programs with schools and community-based organizations.\u00a0 Take photos of individual schools, edit images, draft captions for images, manage necessary release forms, and draft narrative content for school profiles.\u00a0 Update and maintain the InsideSchools website and customized search tools, provide design suggestions for engagement.\u00a0 Produce and edit content for promotion, communication, and marketing of InsideSchools through email copy, social media content, graphics, and flyers. Support the design, collection, and analysis of data, including surveys, analytics, interviews and focus groups, etc. Answer questions from public school families, including working with the team to monitor comments and inquiries. Assist in planning and implementing events and focus groups. Attend education team meetings. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current full-time New School student with a Federal Work Study (FWS) award as part of their financial aid package for the 2026-27 academic year.\u00a0 An understanding of or interest in learning more about the most pressing issues in urban education, particularly the relationship between families and schools. Design capabilities to create graphics for flyers, social media, and website. Experience using content management systems (CMS) (i.e. Contentful, Wordpress), productivity software (i.e. Trello, Google Workplace, Google Analytics), design software (Canva, Illustrator, Photoshop), and social media (Twitter, Facebook) a plus. Strong writing and communication skills. Capacity to work independently and as part of a team. Spanish, French and Chinese language skills are a plus (not required). PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS We are looking for individuals who have experience in one or more of the following areas: Graphic design Data visualization Marketing and outreach Photography Social media\u00a0 Writing Web design WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17 - $19/hour\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Design Assistant will help build InsideSchools' capacity to better serve NYC public school families by creating engaging digital and print content, maintaining website resources, and supporting communications, outreach, and community engagement initiatives. Responsibilities include graphic design, photography, website updates, content creation, data visualization, and assisting with research, events, and school profiles to ensure families have access to clear, accessible, and high-quality educational information. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/Equity-for-Children-FWS-Social-Media-and-Outreach-Assistant_JR109413": {"title": "Equity for Children FWS Social Media and Outreach Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Position/Role Overview Equity for Children/Equidad para la Infancia (EFC), a center within the Studley Graduate Program in International Affairs (SGPIA), is a pioneer and leader in the movement for equity through direct action and support of initiatives and programs that advance the agenda of children\u2019s rights and wellbeing. We work to advance equity for children by creating and disseminating research about policies affecting the most underserved populations and programs that raise innovative solutions. We engage with children and youth to help shape responsible future leaders who realize the importance of equity-focused policy for themselves and for society in general. Founded in 2006 by SGPIA Professor Alberto Minujin at The New School, the program provides a global knowledge acquisition tool for policymakers, academics, students, practitioners and all those working to better the lives of children in need. With a presence in New York, Buenos Aires and Mexico City, Equity for Children sponsors independent, critical thinking and debate, original research, teaching and events in person and through its websites in English, Spanish and Portuguese, encouraging shared conversation, vision and knowledge for all those involved in children\u2019s rights. Reporting to EFC\u2019s Associate Director, the Social Media and Outreach Assistant will focus on developing the social media and online presence of EFC. Additional tasks may be requested to assist with the general communications strategy of the organization. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Tasks may include the following, and specific deliverables will be further identified: I. Social Media & Websites Manage and post given articles and links on EFC Social Media (Facebook, Twitter and Linked In) at least once per week on each account. Track number of hits, users, likes, followers, friends, and other metrics on a regular basis (once a week or month). Propose strategies to improve social media presence and track metrics. Post EFC website content on our WordPress sites to drive followers. Help with specific dissemination strategies on social media (i.e., for new research or an event). Collect and compile monthly strategic website and mass mailing metrics. Provide metrics analysis to pull out recommendations and best practices to increase outreach. II. Assist with documenting events for the EFC website As needed: Live post via social media. Take photos and notes. Prepare write-ups for the website. Conduct short video interviews. QUALIFICATIONS Interest in children\u2019s rights. Verbal and written fluency in English and Spanish, preferred. Technologically capable and experienced with WordPress and HTML; social media platforms expertise with Facebook, Twitter, Instagram and LinkedIn. Some knowledge of graphic design platforms (Photoshop, Canva, etc.) preferred. Some knowledge of video production preferred. The ability and commitment to work for the agreed hours and days, and to be responsive with communication, and responsible with their duties. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $18/hr WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Reporting to EFC\u2019s Associate Director, the Social Media and Outreach Assistant will focus on developing the social media and online presence of EFC. Additional tasks may be requested to assist with the general communications strategy of the organization. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Residential-Education-Community-Assistant---FWS-_JR109395": {"title": "Residential Education Community Assistant ((FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. We are seeking competent Community Assistants to support the daily operation of the residence hall offices during the Fall 2026 - Spring 2027 academic year. Based on needs and talents, this position will assist with graphic design, social media influence, office coverage, answering questions from students and parents, and other duties as assigned. The ideal candidate will be a hard-working student worker able to undertake a variety of tasks, be detail-oriented and creative, use discretion, exude confidence in making decisions, and work well under pressure. The qualified candidate will be able to prioritize their work and will rise to the challenge of incorporating new and effective strategies to achieve the best results.\u00a0 The Residential Education Community Assistant (CA) position at The New School is a position for the duration of the academic year beginning Monday, September 7, 2026 and ending Friday, May 14, 2027. Community Assistants may be asked to work over University Breaks, including but not limited to Winter Break and Spring Break. Community Assistants incoming for the academic year will need to take part in CA Training as scheduled by your supervisor. CAs are required to work a minimum of 10 hours and cannot exceed a maximum of 15 office hours per week. Hours may include weekends if coverage is needed of the office and a CA is available to work. Additionally, hours must be worked between 9 am-5 pm, Monday through Friday.\u00a0 \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Office Responsibilities\u00a0 Staff the residential office during your scheduled shift and maintain a visually appealing, clean, and sanitized work area.\u00a0 Administrative tasks including completing paperwork (log, reports, etc), responding to building emails and voicemails, assisting with filing, and other office coverage tasks as needed. Contact Hall Staff regarding emergencies as necessary.\u00a0 Provide excellent customer service in all interactions with residence hall occupants.\u00a0 Conduct tours of the residence hall during your designated shift.\u00a0 Other duties as assigned.\u00a0 Maintain confidentiality in all aspects of the position General ResHall duties as assigned including but not limited to: Lockouts. Keycard encoding. Assisting professional staff with dorm initiatives including creating marketing events, posting signs, etc.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ability to lift packages that weigh at least 20 pounds.\u00a0 Excellent customer service skills.\u00a0 Clear communication skills: listening, verbal & written skills etc.\u00a0 Dexterity, efficiency, and self-motivation to sort large volumes of mail.\u00a0 Good organizational skills, reliable, dependable, and attentive to detail.\u00a0 Ability to complete administrative tasks neatly and legibly. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Graphic design experience or courses.\u00a0 Ability to use computer software including Google and Microsoft Suite.\u00a0 Experience with Google Suite. \u00a0 WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17.00/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Community Assistants will support the daily operation of the residence hall offices during the Fall 2026 - Spring 2027 academic year. Based on needs and talents, this position will assist with graphic design, social media influence, office coverage, answering of questions from students and parents, and other duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/FWS-Student-Assistant-Specialist--Web-UX-and-Content-Creation_JR109384": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant Specialist, Web UX and Content Creation", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Higher Education Affordability Project at the Center for New York City Affairs is hiring a team of Federal Work Study students to assist our work in web design, social media development, and content creation. You will be part of a student team working for our award-winning website at Understandingfafsa.org . The website and email newsletter are focused on helping first-generation students and college access counselors understand and navigate our nation\u2019s complicated and confusing financial aid process. We work closely with students and counselors to design, craft, and test materials with the goal of making college financial aid easy to understand. We are seeing FWS students with interest and experience in web and UX design, social media marketing and/or web content creation. This is an ideal position for a student with a passion for web design, publishing, and social media who is interested in helping families navigate the financial aid system and avoid too much college debt. Students will work as a team and report directly to the Director of CNYCA\u2019s Higher Education Affordability Project. RESPONSIBILITIES Work with our professional design team on developing V6 of the website including a new section focused on our popular classroom materials for counselors and families.\u00a0 Assist with the administration and publication of our weekly newsletter and updates on LinkedIn and Instagram.\u00a0 Assist the team in developing content and curriculum for the website, including research and writing of short \u201chow-to\u201d blogs and tools for students, families, and educators. Work with these audiences to develop new publications or products to make financial aid easier to understand and more accessible in high school and college.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Experience in graphic design, web design, social media or content production. Passion for learning new skills and working with a team to develop materials and games that help students to learn about college financing and financial planning. Attention to detail and excellent organizational skills with the ability to meet deadlines. Full-time New School student with a Federal Work-Study award (If you don\u2019t see this on your award letter, ask the financial aid office if you have a work-study award. Many students are unaware of this financial aid benefit.)\u00a0 \u00a0 WORK MODE Hybrid - This FWS position is for 10-15 hours per week with flexibility around the semester schedule. All work and meetings can be remote, though we hope to organize in-person team meetings occasionally. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $21/hr This is a Federal Work-Study position with the possibility of evolving into a New School Student Employment position if grant funding allows.\u00a0 SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS Please include the following with your application: New School Financial Aid Award Letter with FWS Allocation\u00a0 Cover Letter detailing your interest and what role(s) you would like to play\u00a0 Resume Portfolio or work samples\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Students will assist the project director and the editorial and design team in developing and promoting new content for our website at Understandingfafsa.org , assisting with a major buildout, promoting new content on social media and helping with administrative tasks. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Technician--Audio-Production---Media-Resources_JR109346": {"title": "Technician, Audio Production & Media Resources", "description": "Posted: 2026-09-01\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician in Audio Production and Media Resources for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces including sound production studios and equipment resources, traditional black and white darkroom, film processing room, experimental darkroom, Digital-C printing lab, film scanning and inkjet printing labs, photography and motion capture studios, and the Equipment Resource Center.\u00a0 This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters Professional union contract, that reports to the Assistant Director, Sound and Filmmaking Operations.\u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned and maintain a clean and safe working environment.\u00a0 Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, and continuous operation of a variety of hardware/software technologies and stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Support faculty and students. Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment. Make hardware/software purchases and process recommendations. Create and update web page content. Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours. Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record keeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. Become proficient in Web Check Out or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; meet deadlines and anticipate facility and checkout center needs, adjusting approach and prioritizing competing demands.\u00a0 Ability to be proactive in addressing facility needs, including but not limited to maintaining organized workspaces, tracking inventory locations, and monitoring Technician communication networks.\u00a0 Properly handle, mix, and dispose of photo-chemistry and safely use ladders and other hardware to support facility needs. Acquire working knowledge of Digital C printing and a variety of Motion Capture and Augmented/Virtual Reality practices (including but not limited to: full body tracking, facial tracking, projection mapping, mixed reality, Motive (or other equivalent Motion Capture software), relevant CG and SFX software and SDKs) within the first 6 months of employment.\u00a0 Maintain audio equipment and materials. Maintain audio interface and software in labs. Perform audio inventory, prepare checkouts, and perform logistics and deliver to classrooms as needed. Completion of university-required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that include nights and weekends, in addition to scheduling shifts with semester changes. Knowledge of a variety of analog and digital photographic and audio processes, be familiar with practices related to current design and art practice - including: analog/experimental/digital photography, digital input/output, studio and on-location lighting and audio recording equipment.\u00a0 Experience and knowledge of working with traditional darkroom chemistry. Technical knowledge of analog and digital technologies, including but not limited to still cameras, video equipment, audio equipment, studio strobe lighting and grip equipment, continuous lighting, virtual reality, projectors, steadicams, film scanners, large format printers, media players, Mac mini(s), tablets and other smart devices. Full comprehensive experience in maintaining and troubleshooting equipment/tools. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment, with a working knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployment, network systems, color calibration/profiling. Direct experience with Adobe Creative Cloud, Capture One, Flexcolor Scan Software, various printer server software, Opti-Track Motive, Google Docs, Audio production processes and equipment & software including Pro Tools.\u00a0 Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems.\u00a0 Knowledge of Motion Capture and Augmented/Virtual Reality practices. Knowledge of Digital C Printing. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum\u00a0 SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS *\u00a0 For full consideration, please submit a resume and cover letter in your application. WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness \u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/UC-Sewing-Student-Assistant_JR109318": {"title": "UC Sewing Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-31\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in various shops within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open work spaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties for each space vary slightly, but entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 Responsibilities Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor.\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available\u00a0 to assist them.\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls in a professional and courteous manner.\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests.\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance.\u00a0 Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery.\u00a0 Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness.\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned.\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 Minimum Qualifications:\u00a0 Excellent customer service skills\u00a0 Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Physical ability to lift items\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work mode: On-campus position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work that is not feasible, or not in the organization's best interest, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Salary: $17.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Manager-of-Academic-Planning_JR109314-1": {"title": "Manager of Academic Planning", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Administrative work at Lang/NSSR is collaborative, based on a premise of teamwork, and is student-centered.\u00a0We are committed in our administrative staff to the same values that undergird Lang and The New School: civic engagement, social justice, and learning. Together we work towards Lang\u2019s success and the success of The New School. In this spirit, there is a tradition of contributing creatively and generously both within the focus areas of one\u2019s position and as needed and available in other areas. The Manager of Academic Planning supports curriculum building for the Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts (Lang) and The New School for Social Research (NSSR) within the Academic Planning and Curriculum Hub. The Manager works with the colleges\u2019 academic leadership, faculty, and administrative teams and reports to the Associate Director of Academic Planning. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES \u00a0 Support the course planning process to ensure the successful implementation of curriculum: Manage and monitor course enrollments throughout the registration process.\u00a0 Provide strategic recommendations for course additions and cancellations when necessary.\u00a0 Oversee, track, and efficiently resolve student requests related to course registration; collaborate with Advising with respect to new and continuing student enrollment.\u00a0 Facilitate communication within programs to inform students, faculty, and staff about curricular developments, policy changes, and registration matters.\u00a0 Actively engage with the Director of Academic Planning to assure instructional spaces are designed to adequately accommodate courses, including attributes (i.e., software, hardware, furniture, and adjacencies, etc.; serve as point-person on room scheduling matters for academic programs.\u00a0 Work in concert with Associate Dean of Part-Time Faculty Affairs and Associate Dean of Administrative Faculty Affairs to assure part-time faculty obligations are realized for schools; support processes to identify need for and proper placement of Teaching Assistants and Teaching Fellows; assure placement for Full-Time Faculty.\u00a0 Supervise and provide ongoing oversight in the production, completion, submission, proofing and revising of key documentation for classes in the fall, spring and summer semesters.\u00a0 Create and maintain shared schedule documentation containing information including, but not limited to, overall curricular program offerings, faculty assignments, course meeting days and times, and room assignments.\u00a0 Create and maintain shared curricular documentation containing information including, but not limited to, course pedagogy, course descriptions, co- and prerequisite information, room-type preferences, adjacencies, seat reservations, and course restrictions.\u00a0 Provide regular college-wide curricular data reports that highlight key factors such as enrollment trends, cross registration, faculty and space utilization, and other reports as requested. Support part-time faculty (PTF) affairs to facilitate compliance with UAW-ACT Local 7902 collective bargaining agreement and academic planning processes: Manage and update PTF data needed for overall academic planning cycle.\u00a0 Develop and complete PTF reports necessary for both college-wide curriculum planning purposes and those requested by central University offices. Perform additional tasks as required in support of the Academic Planning and Curriculum Hub and the overall operations of the College.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor\u2019s Degree with related experience. Minimum of 5 years of experience in higher education or a related field. Demonstrated ability to analyze, summarize, and draw conclusions from large datasets. High level of computer proficiency in Google Drive, MS Office, and online database systems; strong knowledge of word-processing, spreadsheet, and database software is essential Ability to work well under pressure, take initiative, and follow through on multiple tasks while maintaining a high degree of accuracy, speed and poise. Ability to communicate well with different populations at the University including faculty, students, and staff. Excellent task-management, organizational, and inter-personal/service skills. Ability to problem solve and take initiative.\u00a0 Commitment to discretion when working with private and protected data.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master\u2019s degree. Knowledge of and proficiency in Banner, Tableau, Degreeworks, and WorkDay and the ability to pull and prepare reports out of these systems. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $70,000 - $73,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness \u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Assistant-Director-for-Research-and-Instruction_JR109291-1": {"title": "Assistant Director for Research and Instruction", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design and the College of Performing Arts (Mannes, Jazz, and Drama), Lang College and the New School for Social Research, and Parsons Paris . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. Reporting to the Director of Research & Instructional Services (RIS), the Assistant Director provides leadership in the planning, management, assessment, and strategic development of research and instructional services. Position duties are concentrated in supporting departmental assessment and analytics, developing and advancing strategic initiatives, and serving as liaison in subject areas related to management and entrepreneurship. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Assistant Director of Research & Instructional Services is a key member of the department's leadership team, balancing operational leadership with opportunities to strengthen and evolve library services in response to the changing needs of the university community. Working collaboratively with library colleagues, faculty, and academic programs, the Assistant Director supervises the Research Unit, leads assessment efforts that inform decision-making, and helps identify and implement new initiatives, including emerging areas such as scholarly communication. The successful candidate will bring strong leadership, analytical thinking, and a collaborative approach to enhancing research, teaching, and learning while contributing to the Libraries' long-term goals. This is a full-time position. \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Supervisory Duties Supervise Research & Instructional Services in the absence of the Director. Supervise assigned Subject Librarians.\u00a0 Lead \u201cResearch Unit\u201d and oversee areas of focus: Assessment and analytics Data visualization Scholarly communication and open publishing Assessment Duties Make budget recommendations for collection management. Ensure processes are effective, regularly assessing services and recommending revisions.\u00a0 Develop assessment strategies and actively participate in the implementation of strategic planning and goals.\u00a0 Generate and analyze departmental statistics, incorporating data visualization tools. Operational Duties Jointly oversee and carry out research and instructional services (in-person, by phone and virtually) for library locations.\u00a0 Serve as liaison for programs within management and entrepreneurship (e.g., Fashion Communication, Fashion Management, and Media Management) by providing: outreach, instruction, research assistance, reserves and new program support, collection development, online guides and tutorials.\u00a0 Develop and coordinate services that support scholarly communication, including open publishing, and provide guidance to faculty and researchers on evolving publishing practices. Support the development and assessment of new services, resources, and initiatives that enhance research, teaching, and library services. Collaboration Duties Partner with other library units to ensure excellent internal/external customer service standards. Collaborate with faculty, academic programs, and library colleagues to advance teaching, learning, and equitable access to library-supported course materials. Cultivate partnerships across the Libraries and the New School community to identify and advance scholarly communication initiatives and other emerging services. Participate in local and national forums in order to stay current with trends regarding research and instruction services in academic libraries. Participate in working groups, e.g., Stats and Analytics. Other duties as required.\u00a0 QUALIFICATIONS Relevant Bachelor\u2019s degree from a four-year program. ALA-\u00adaccredited MLS. Strong understanding of research data management, statistical analysis, scholarly publishing and open access. Supervisory and/or project management experience. Excellent interpersonal, written, and oral communication skills.\u00a0 Ability to thrive in constantly evolving environment and eager to facilitate innovative change.\u00a0 Ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines. 3+ years experience in an academic library environment. Master\u2019s in relevant subject area. Exemplary teaching skills or training experience. Demonstrated knowledge of current technologies related to research and instruction. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $77,000 - 80,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness \u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Discovery-Systems-and-Electronic-Resources-Specialist_JR109312-1": {"title": "Discovery Systems and Electronic Resources Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School \u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 8,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW \u00a0 Reporting to the Assistant Director for Library Systems, the Discovery Systems and Electronic Resources Specialist supports the discovery and access of the library\u2019s resources. The position provides support for electronic resource troubleshooting and discovery services. This role works closely with other Digital Library and Technical Services teams (Acquisitions, Metadata, and Collection Services) and consults with public services teams to enhance how users find, access, evaluate, and use library, archives, and special collections resources within the primary library catalog and related applications. The position supports the technology that connects faculty and students with library materials. The position requires strong analytical judgment, systems thinking, documentation skills, and the ability to translate access problems, metadata issues, usage data, and user behavior into practical service improvements. Work will directly contribute to resolving access and discovery challenges, making a meaningful difference for library users on all global New School campuses. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Primary Responsibilities Discovery & Electronic Resource Support Monitor and analyze catalog and electronic resource usage Troubleshoot electronic resource access (OpenAthens) and metadata issues\u00a0 Recommend and implement discovery and supporting system improvements Coordinate with university, consortial, and vendor support departments Create and review documentation for library staff Facilitate import of new digital collections and secure electronic lending within the discovery interface.\u00a0 Collaborate with colleagues in the Libraries, Archives, and other areas of the university Enrich and load metadata for electronic/digital library materials in consultation with the Metadata and Collection Services team. Collect, analyze, and present data from various sources Other Duties As Assigned QUALIFICATIONS Required Bachelor\u2019s degree and relevant professional experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience supporting electronic resources, discovery systems, metadata workflows, library systems, scholarly communications, or academic information services. Experience with data integrations, APIs, or automated workflows Strong analytical and problem-solving skills \u00a0 Preferred Master\u2019s degree in Library and Information Science, information management, data analytics, educational technology, publishing, or a related field. Demonstrated experience with Ex Libris Alma/Primo, or comparable library service platforms ( Alma / Primo certification as an example). Experience with Digital Asset Management systems such as ContentDM or Alma-D/Specto Familiarity with specific standards: COUNTER, SUSHI, KBART, and OpenURL Ability to read, adapt, or troubleshoot basic scripts (Python and/or JavaScript), structured data, or configuration files in support of library systems and electronic resource workflows. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement with on-campus library patrons and staff.\u00a0#LI-Onsite Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE $66,300 - $70,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Narwhal-Fitness-Instructor---OCSE-_JR109324": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Narwhal Fitness Instructor  (OCSE)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nPart time\n\nThe Office of Student Engagement oversees community building programs, and seeks a student worker to support programming for commuter students. This position reports to the Associate Director for Recreation & Commuter Engagement. The New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education.\u00a0 Reporting to the Associate Director of Recreation, & Commuter Engagement, the Fitness Instructor will be responsible for leading fitness classes, creating content for social media accounts, and acting as the point-person for inquiries and class sign ups. RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a student fitness instructor during a TNS fitness class.\u00a0 Create content for the Commuter Group\u2019s NarwhalNation and Instagram pages.\u00a0 Reply to student inquiries related to the fitness class in question. Lead events in question with a fitness course/lesson plan.\u00a0 Support students in signing up for the class. Take class attendance per session. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled New School student Demonstrated commitment to community building and teamwork Reliable, organized, and communicative Past fitness experience Ability to lead fitness sessions Able to manage a hybrid schedule with majority in-person hours PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience/certification working as a fitness instructor is preferred.\u00a0 WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI HOURLY RATE : $19 - $21 / hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u200b SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Questions can be emailed to recreation@newschool.edu but applications must be submitted through the New School WorkDay Jobs Portal for an interview to be scheduled.\u00a0 Responsibilities: The Fitness Instructor will be responsible for leading fitness classes, creating content for social media accounts, and acting as the point-person for inquiries and class sign ups. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Assistant-Manager-of-Mechanics--Sewing-and-Soft-Construction-Fixed-Term_JR109216-1": {"title": "Assistant Manager of Mechanics, Sewing and Soft Construction Fixed-Term", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Parsons School of Design, a division of The New School, invites applications for the position of Assistant Manager of Mechanics, Sewing & Soft Construction within the Making Center. The Parsons Making Center encompasses over 70,000 square feet of laboratories, shops, and studios that foster experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. This role is responsible for the operational management of the Sewing facilities and equipment, ensuring a safe, healthy environment for undergrad, graduate, and continuing education students and faculty while overseeing essential administrative duties. We seek a personable, proactive, and detail-oriented professional to support a wide range of processes in sewing and soft construction, with a core focus on repair and maintenance of machinery.\u00a0 This is an exempt administrative position reporting to the Facilities Manager of Sewing and Soft Construction, Making Center, based in a fast-paced environment that operates year-round.\u00a0The hire is for a 3-year fixed term subject to end unless otherwise approved for renewal. \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain a clean and safe working environment and uphold a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Lead the process to troubleshoot, repair, maintain, install, and ensure continuous operation and support for a variety of industrial and domestic sewing equipment. Lead the efforts and train other staff on routine maintenance of equipment, in addition to developing Standard Operating Procedures and preventative maintenance schedules. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Assist faculty and students. Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment. Make hardware/software purchase and process recommendations. Create web page content. Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours. Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record-keeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. Oversee tool and equipment checkout for both in-shop/lab use and off-site special requests. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; adjust approach and prioritize competing demands as necessary. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment with productive and positive workplace relationships. Stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and/or 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to scheduling shifts with semester changes. Completion of university required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Become proficient in WebCheckout or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Knowledge of industrial sewing and soft construction techniques, finishing, and troubleshooting for traditional garment and accessory construction, as well as experimental projects. Technical knowledge of a variety of industrial sewing techniques and machinery, as well as experience with repair and maintenance of equipment Expertise in threading, safe operation, and routine maintenance of sewing machines. Ability to identify machine issues, troubleshoot, and conduct repairs to keep 400+ machines in working order.\u00a0 Facilitate repairs with external vendors as needed, including procurement and contract management.\u00a0 Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment. Familiarity with Adobe Creative Cloud and Google Suite. Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Trained sewing mechanic. Expansive technical experience servicing specialized industrial sewing machinery such as lockstitch, overlock, coverstitch, flatlock, post, walking foot, blind hem, chainstitch, embroidery, sonobond, and skiver machines. Experience with shoe-making processes, leatherwork, and fiber art practices. Experience with building and hand tools to maintain all studio equipment and furniture.\u00a0 Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems.\u00a0 Experience with large-scale hardware and software deployments. Experience with Juki, Consew, Brother, and Singer sewing machinery. \u00a0 WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement.\u00a0#LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $66,300 - $69,000 per year WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 Interview finalists will be asked to come in for an in-person interview in which their technical skills will be assessed.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Front-of-House-Usher--Student-Assistant_JR109365": {"title": "FWS - Front of House Usher, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nPart time\n\nPOSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Front of House area at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as an Usher. We\u2019re looking for responsible, personable, communicative, and punctual individuals to support initiatives within the Front of House area. This position reports to the Manager of Event Operations and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the performing arts, events, and production. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Greet patrons and answer questions Distribute printed programs to patrons Assist patrons in finding their seats Check in patrons using our app-based check-in system Execute stage changes for classical recitals when assigned Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Background and education in the performing arts PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS\u00a0 2 years usher experience 2 years experience in customer service Recipient of Federal Work Study award WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17.00 Hours per Week: 3-20, or as needed. Not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek while classes are in session. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit both a resume and cover letter outlining their interest, skills, and experience pertaining to this position; and a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Provide front-of-house support by greeting patrons, processing app-based check-ins, distributing programs, and assisting guests with seating and inquiries. Execute stage changes for classical recitals and perform additional operational responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Front-of-House-Usher--Student-Assistant_JR109364": {"title": "Front of House Usher, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nPart time\n\nPOSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Front of House area at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as an Usher. We\u2019re looking for responsible, personable, communicative, and punctual individuals to support initiatives within the Front of House area. This position reports to the Manager of Event Operations and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in the performing arts, events, and production. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Greet patrons and answer questions Distribute printed programs to patrons Assist patrons in finding their seats Check in patrons using our app-based check-in system Execute stage changes for classical recitals when assigned Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Background and education in the performing arts PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS\u00a0 2 years usher experience 2 years experience in customer service WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17.00 Hours per Week: 3-20, or as needed. Not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek while classes are in session. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit both a resume and cover letter outlining their interest, skills, and experience pertaining to this position; and a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Responsibilities: Provide front-of-house support by greeting patrons, processing app-based check-ins, distributing programs, and assisting guests with seating and inquiries. Execute stage changes for classical recitals and perform additional operational responsibilities as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-Metal-Shop-Student-Assistant_JR109135": {"title": "E4 Metal Shop Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.\u00a0 Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work Mode: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0 and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-Wood-Student-Assistant_JR109138": {"title": "E4 Wood Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.\u00a0 Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work Mode : On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI \u00a0 Hourly Rate : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0 and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---79-Fifth-Avenue/Event-Planning-Support--Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109332": {"title": "Event Planning Support, Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-21\nPart time\n\nThe India China Institute (ICI) at The New School seeks a highly motivated student to provide part-time support for the Institute's public programs, events, communications, and day-to-day activities. \u00a0 The ideal candidate is organized, detail-oriented, dependable, and able to work independently while collaborating effectively with faculty, staff, students, invited speakers, fellows, and other members of the university community. The position offers an opportunity to gain hands-on experience in event planning, communications, digital media, and program administration in an international academic environment. \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES The student worker will assist with the planning and implementation of the Institute's public programs and activities. Responsibilities may include, but are not limited to: Assist in coordinating public programs, including creating event pages, marketing campaigns, booking spaces, arranging catering and honoraria Submit expense reports and navigate platforms for reservations, receipts and reconciliations (MyDay, Concur); Manage correspondence with fellows, invited guests, event audience, students and faculty, administrators and colleagues, newsletter subscribers, etc.; Design monthly newsletter using Mailchimp Support other Institute programs and administrative activities as needed. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS For Federal Work Study award letter Strong written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple tasks and deadlines effectively. Ability to work independently while also collaborating with a diverse group of colleagues and stakeholders. Familiarity with Microsoft Office, Google Workspace, and other common digital tools. Comfort learning and using web-based applications and new software. Interest in event planning, communications, international affairs, higher education, or related areas. \u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with Mailchimp, WordPress, Adobe Creative Suite, or similar communications and design platforms. Experience with event planning, communications, social media, or digital content creation. Working knowledge of MyDay and/or Concur. Experience with maintaining databases or managing online content. Other web, digital media, or programming skills.\u00a0 WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $19 to $21 per hour. The rate for this position is commensurate with experience.\u00a0\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student will provide part-time support for the Institute's public programs, events, communications, and day-to-day activities. The student will collaborate with staff, faculty and students across the university. The position is open to both graduate and undergraduate students.\u00a0 Federal Work Students are encouraged to apply.\u00a0 This position may have high and low weeks of work. At times, it may demand up to 20 hours per week.\u00a0 At other times, it may demand fewer than 10 hours. The candidate must be available to deliver on demand.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Part-time-Student-Health-Counselor_JR109251-1": {"title": "Part-time Student Health Counselor", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 8,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. \u00a0 The New School is seeking two part-time, 10 months per year, Student Health Counselors who are licensed clinical psychologists to provide clinical supervision of doctoral-level psychology students, crisis intervention, short-term therapy, and case management to both graduate and undergraduate degree seeking students. We are seeking candidates who are highly skilled clinicians who have experience working with individuals with diverse identities and have experience with clinical supervision. This position reports directly to the Director of Counseling. These positions are part of the Student Health Employees at the New School (SHENS-UAW) Union.\u00a0 The terms and conditions of employment for this position are covered by a collective bargaining agreement between the New School and SHENS-UAW. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide crisis intervention, assessment,\u00a0 short-term individual and group therapy, and case management to university students Work with students in crisis and those with trauma histories, including racialized and other trauma related to identity. Supervise PhD psychology externs and MSW interns Connect with collateral contacts including hospitals, community providers, faculty and staff, as appropriate Assist with wellness outreach to university students, staff, and faculty Collaborate closely with interdisciplinary team within Student Health Services including: psychiatry, medical, and public health Participate actively in staff meetings and in weekly supervision. Maintain timely, appropriate and accurate records and correspondence related to students served Attend workshops, training seminars and university meetings as appropriate \u00a0 QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D or Psy.D in psychology Current licensure to practice in New York State Clinical supervision experience Some evening hours required\u00a0 \u00a0 WORK MODE This position is 100% in-person on campus during fall and spring semesters #LI-Onsite 10-month per year position Winter break will afford opportunities for some remote work hours.\u00a0 HOURLY RATE $55.44/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. \u00a0 BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) \u00a0 Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan \u00a0 Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) \u00a0 Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents \u00a0 Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Technician--Sound---Film-Resources_JR109309": {"title": "Technician, Sound & Film Resources", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician for Sound Film Resources for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and support experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces, including sound & video production studios and equipment resources, filmmaking equipment center, and production facilities and studios. This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters Professional union contract, that reports to the Assistant Director, Sound and Filmmaking Operations.\u00a0 \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned and maintain a clean and safe working environment. Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff. Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, and continuous operation of a variety of hardware/software technologies and stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Support faculty and students Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment Make hardware/software purchase and process recommendations Create and update web page content Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record keeping, inventories, and metrics tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts Become proficient in Web Check Out or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment Assist with facility improvement projects, shows and installations, and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; meet deadlines and anticipate facility and checkout center needs, adjusting approach and prioritizing competing demands. Ability to be proactive in addressing facility needs, including but not limited to maintaining organized workspaces, tracking inventory locations, and monitoring Technician communication networks. Properly use ladders and other hardware to support facility needs. Maintain audio and filmmaking equipment, studios, and materials. Maintain audio and filmmaking interfaces and software in labs. Perform audio and filmmaking inventory, prepare checkouts, and perform logistics and deliver to classrooms as needed. Completion of university-required staff training and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by the supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. Knowledge of a variety of analog and digital filmmaking processes, be familiar with practices related to current media practices - including: analog/experimental/digital audio and filmmaking processes, digital input/output, studio and on-location lighting, experience and knowledge of working with sound recording. Technical knowledge of analog and digital technologies, including but not limited to 16mm film cameras, digital video/cinema equipment, audio recording equipment, studio and on-location lighting and grip equipment, projectors, steadicams,, media players, mac mini(s), tablets and other smart devices. Full comprehensive experience in maintaining and troubleshooting equipment/tools. Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment, with a working knowledge of large-scale hardware and software deployment, network systems, color calibration/profiling. Direct experience with Adobe Creative Cloud,, Google Docs, Audio production processes and equipment & software including Pro Tools.\u00a0 Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems. Knowledge of Motion Capture and Augmented/Virtual Reality practices. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS For full consideration, please submit a resume and cover letter in your application. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/Technician--Sewing-and-Soft-Construction--fixed-term-_JR109308": {"title": "Technician, Sewing and Soft Construction (fixed term)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks a Full-time Technician in Sewing and Soft Construction for the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0The Technician should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0This is a fast-paced, dynamic environment that runs year-round. The Technician will interface with students and faculty daily and work with Making Center staff to support multiple processes and resources for all users in a range of making spaces including the sewing, shoemaking, tool checkout/ store, and soft construction facilities.\u00a0 This is an administrative position, governed by a Local 1205 Teamsters union contract, that reports to the Assistant Manager of Sewing and Soft Construction. The hire is for a 3-year fixed term subject to end unless otherwise approved for renewal. \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain a clean and safe working environment and uphold a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Work with other Making Center staff on the troubleshooting, repair, maintenance, installation, continuous operation, and support for a variety of hardware/software and technologies. Assist supervisor and Making Center Leadership with the following: Assist faculty and students. Lead demo sessions about various pieces of equipment. Make hardware/software purchases and process recommendations. Create web page content. Create and conduct orientations, trainings, and tours. Participate in the onboarding process for new technician staff, through peer-to-peer trainings and skills sharing. Maintain administrative systems for scheduling, record-keeping, inventory, and metric tracking. Provide support for store and tool checkout functions as related to assigned areas Oversee daily workings of the Making Center facilities as assigned. Supervise student workers, including training, tracking accountability, and assigning tasks during shifts. Oversee tool and equipment checkout for both in-shop/lab use and off-site special requests. Create clear, accurate, user-friendly technical documentation, signage, and instructions for labs and equipment. Assist with facility improvement projects and special Making Center, program, school or Parsons-wide initiatives as needed. Adapt to changes in the work environment; adjust approach and prioritize competing demands as necessary. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment with productive and positive workplace relationships. Stay current with relevant technology and developments within the field. Attend regularly scheduled Making Center staff meetings and perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s Degree and/or 3 years of relevant or professional experience within the industry. Flexible schedule - ability to work non-traditional schedules that may include nights and weekends, in addition to schedule shifts with semester changes. Completion of university required staff trainings and necessary COF's for all Making Center Facilities within the first 6 months of employment. Become proficient in WebCheckout or current equipment management system within the first 6 months of employment. Knowledge of industrial sewing and soft construction techniques, finishing, and troubleshooting for traditional garment and accessory construction, as well as experimental projects. Technical knowledge of a variety of industrial sewing techniques and machinery.\u00a0 Confidence in threading, safe operation and maintenance of sewing machines. Mechanical understanding, with a strong desire to learn how to troubleshoot and maintain several different types of industrial machines for repair and maintenance purposes.\u00a0 Understanding of working in a cross-platform (Windows and macOS) environment. Familiarity with Adobe Creative Cloud and Google Suite. Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Strong ability to assist students working in an interdisciplinary and/or experimental manner from concept to fabrication. A desire to learn new skills and processes related to Parsons Making Center initiatives. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with specialized industrial sewing machinery such as lockstitch, overlock, coverstitch, flatlock, post, walking foot, blind hem, chainstitch, embroidery. Experience with shoe making processes, leatherwork, and fiber art practices. Experience with fashion production processes such as patternmaking and textile manipulation. Experience with basic building and hand tools. Experience assessing and maintaining industrial sewing machines.\u00a0 Knowledge of Webcheckout or comparable equipment management systems.\u00a0 Experience with large-scale hardware and software deployments. Experience with Juki, Consew, Brother, and Singer sewing machinery. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-ONSITE SALARY $28.52 per hour/$51,906.40 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS \u00a0 Interview finalists will be asked to come in for an in-person interview in which their technical skills will be assessed. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/N---66-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Assistant--Equipment-Resource-Center---Photography-and-Media-Resources--FWS-_JR109305": {"title": "Student Assistant, Equipment Resource Center - Photography and Media Resources (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Photography Labs and Equipment Resource Center within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES: Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Darkroom and Alternative Process Labs - Students are responsible for setting up and maintaining equipment, mixing and preparing photographic chemicals, and keeping the space clean and organized. Assist other students with darkroom procedures, manage supplies, enforce safety protocols, troubleshoot issues, and document processes. Additionally, you might train new users and help with routine maintenance and repairs to ensure everything runs efficiently. Print and Scanning Labs - In the scanning and print lab, student workers will be responsible for setting up and operating scanning and printing equipment, managing and organizing digital files, and ensuring high-quality output. They will assist users with file preparation, troubleshoot technical issues, and maintain and calibrate equipment. Additionally, the role includes managing inventory of paper, ink, and other supplies, keeping the workspace clean and organized, and providing support and guidance to fellow students on lab procedures and equipment use. Equipment Resource Center - As a student worker assisting technicians in the daily operations of an equipment resource center, you will support the setup, maintenance, and operation of various equipment and tools. Responsibilities include effectively using Webcheckout for managing checkouts and returns, helping with routine inspections, troubleshooting technical issues, and ensuring equipment is clean and in good working order. You\u2019ll assist in managing inventory, including tracking and restocking supplies, and provide support to users by explaining equipment functionality and usage procedures. Additionally, you\u2019ll contribute to organizing and maintaining a safe and efficient work environment, and collaborate with technicians to streamline daily operations and address any operational challenges. Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness. Complete special projects as assigned Participate in training sessions and staff meetings MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from the supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0 and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/AY26-27-SCE-Tutor_JR109265": {"title": "AY26/27 SCE Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nPart time\n\nRESPONSIBILITIES Student tutor will assist other students in out-of-class by tutoring and answering questions about course content taught in class. Creating a weekly schedule where students can sign up and reserve time to work one on one with tutor.\u00a0\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Interest and Knowledge : Strong interest in systems and ID, AD, PD software. Completed first year of SCE course work.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Interest and Knowledge : Strong interest in systems and ID, AD, PD software. Completed first year of SCE course work.\u00a0 WORK MODE: In person #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE : $29.20/hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Working with students outside the classroom that have specific needs around key SCE software. Tutors will be responsible for c reating a weekly schedule where students can sign up and reserve time to work one on one with tutor.\u00a0\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/AY26-27-SCE-Course-Assistant_JR109266-1": {"title": "AY26/27 SCE Course Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nPart time\n\nRESPONSIBILITIES: Support teaching and learning outcomes through non-instructional activities.\u00a0 Duties may include: attending class, making photocopies, demonstrating use of equipment, proctoring exams, collecting assignments, assisting with classroom set-up, uploading course content online, tracking students\u2019 participation online, assisting students having difficulty with online environment, and grading of student work. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Interest and Knowledge : Strong interest in organization and software. Completed first year in SCE. WORK MODE: \u00a0 Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. This position will require 2 days of on campus work. There will be times that this position can be remote however, this will be discussed as projects come in. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE: $25.13 Diversity Statement: We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: This position will s upport teaching and learning outcomes through non-instructional activities.\u00a0Duties may include, however not limited to: attending class, making photocopies and demonstrating use of equipment for labs as needed. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/G---80-Fifth-Avenue/Health-Promotion-Specialist_JR109232-1": {"title": "Health Promotion Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nFull time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 8,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The New School\u2019s Student Health Services (SHS) seeks a full-time Health Promotion Specialist (HPS) to support student wellbeing through equity-centered health promotion, community engagement, and inclusive student programming. Reporting to the Director of Public Health Services, this position advances the mission of Wellness and Health Promotion (WHP) by fostering a campus culture grounded in belonging, equity, social justice, and collective wellbeing. WHP, the public health branch of SHS, promotes health as a critical component of academic success and student development. The Health Promotion Specialist will lead initiatives that support historically marginalized and underrepresented student communities while helping to embed anti-oppression, inclusion, and culturally responsive practices across student-facing programs and services. This role combines strategic organizing, student advocacy, educational programming, assessment, and cross-campus collaboration to advance equity and belonging throughout the student experience. Working under the supervision of the Director of Public Health Services, the Health Promotion Specialist will support university-wide prevention and health promotion efforts aligned with The New School\u2019s strategic priorities and commitment to student success and social justice. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES \u00a0 Student Engagement, Belonging & Advocacy Co-develop and implement student-centered initiatives that foster belonging, inclusion, and wellbeing for undergraduate and graduate students, particularly students from historically marginalized communities. Create community-building programs in response to campus, national, or global incidents impacting student wellbeing. Provide mentorship, support, and referral services to students navigating complex personal, social, or identity-related concerns. Serve as a liaison and advocate for student voices in campus planning and decision-making processes. Support retention and success initiatives for underrepresented student populations. \u00a0 Health Promotion & Educational Programming Co-design and facilitate workshops, retreats, trainings, and educational campaigns focused on: Cultural competency Anti-bias education Social justice and equity Intersectionality and addressing microaggressions Self-care and collective care Community wellbeing Two Spirit, Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, Trans, Queer, Intersex, Asexual+\u00a0(2SLGBTQIA+) health and well-being (e.g. Safe Zone) Collaborate with academic divisions and student affairs partners to integrate health and wellbeing themes into curricular and co-curricular experiences. Assist in activating The New School\u2019s Healthy Campus Communities Framework, including: Learning and working environments Personal growth and development Services and supports Policies and procedures Physical spaces Social connection and community engagement\u00a0 \u00a0 Assessment, Evaluation & Data Analysis Support administration and analysis of student wellness assessments, including the National College Health Assessment (NCHA). Assist in preparation of presentations and recommendations to inform strategic planning and continuous improvement efforts. \u00a0 Peer Leadership & Program Coordination Help supervise and coordinate the university\u2019s Peer Health Advocacy Program and other student leadership initiatives. Support training and professional development for peer educators, student interns, and student leaders. Assist with program operations, communications, and event logistics. \u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's\u00a0degree in Public Health, Health Promotion,\u00a0Social Work, Psychology, Student Affairs, or a related field. Minimum of 2 years of professional experience in higher education, student affairs, public health, or community-based social justice, equity, and belonging\u00a0programming. Demonstrated experience developing and facilitating educational workshops, trainings, or student engagement initiatives. Knowledge of the social determinants of health, health promotion theory and practice with intersectional frameworks. Strong understanding of diversity, equity, inclusion, anti-oppression, and student belonging practices. Excellent written, verbal, presentation, and interpersonal communication skills. Strong organizational, project management, and collaboration skills. Ability to work effectively in diverse, multidisciplinary, and fast-paced environments. Ability to manage confidential information with professionalism and discretion. \u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience supervising peer educators, student staff, or interns. Experience with campus climate assessment, data analysis, or program evaluation. Familiarity with restorative/healing-centered practices. Knowledge of culturally rooted practices and trauma-informed approaches. Experience working in a large, decentralized university setting. Bilingual or multilingual proficiency. \u00a0 WORK MODE During the fall and spring semesters, this position is 100% in-person and requires some evening hours. During winter break and summer periods, a hybrid work schedule may be considered. #LI-ONSITE SALARY RANGE $66,300 - $70,000 per annum \u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: \u00a0 Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) \u00a0 Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan \u00a0 Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) \u00a0 Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents \u00a0 Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * (if applicable) Please submit two statements as part of your application package: Philosophy statement on equity and student belonging Statement on activating the social determinants of health at The New School and how this framework would inform your work within a Health Promotion specialty focused on equity and belonging. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/NSSR-Student-Advisor_JR109200": {"title": "NSSR Student Advisor", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The department is seeking a dedicated Student Advisor for the forthcoming 2026-2027 academic cycle. In this role, advisors support both incoming and current NSSR graduate scholars by facilitating enrollment procedures, degree requirement fulfillment, and various institutional administrative processes. This appointment carries a projected commitment of 10 to 25 hours per week for the duration of the academic year. This position is not covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Below is a list of responsibilities. The primary supervisor for the duty is noted (C=Chair; AA=Academic Affairs; AA/C=joint involvement of Academic Affairs and Chair in aspects of the project): Coordinate faculty advising for new and continuing students (C). Provide program information to continuing students (AA/C). Develop and/or update student handbooks to be available during fall registration each year (AA/C). Organize, advertise, and conduct orientation activities for new students (AA/C). Assist in gathering information about enrollment and placement (AA/C). Gather, organize, and review data for biannual academic review of students by department/committee faculty (AA/C). Maintain department records for students, including faculty advising assignments (AA/C). Report on departmental advising and dissertation committees to the Office of Academic Affairs. (AA) Participate in departmental/committee faculty meetings (C). Participate in monthly meetings with other Student Advisors and the Coordinator of Academic Affairs (AA). Post and maintain regular office hours throughout the fiscal year (July 1- June 30), as well as increased office hours during registration and add/drop periods (AA). Consult and assist students during all periods of registration(AA). Work with the Registrar\u2019s Office to complete paperwork regarding non-course requirements, as well as complete the graduation-related tasks (AA/C). Assist with the administration of departmental/committee MA and PhD exams (C). Evaluate placement and removal of certain department registration holds (AA/C). Carry out the administrative and clerical responsibilities (typing, correspondence, telephoning, filing, etc.) associated with the above responsibilities (AA/C). QUALIFICATIONS An advanced scholar in excellent standing, possessing a cumulative GPA of at least 3.4, who has successfully fulfilled 66% of their attempted credits prior to the start of the term. Candidates must not have requested or received any timeframe extensions regarding the fulfillment of their NSSR degree requirements. Demonstrates a comprehensive understanding of the institutional policies and milestones necessary for scholarly advancement within the NSSR and their specific academic department. WORK MODE This role utilizes a hybrid model. To foster robust on-site partnership and community participation, students must maintain a physical presence on campus as required. Alternative remote or blended schedules may be reviewed individually, contingent upon departmental requirements and functional demands. Such setups require prior consultation and formal authorization from the relevant divisional leadership or an authorized representative. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $27 per hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: NSSR academic affairs seeks dedicated Student Advisors for each department and degree program for the forthcoming academic year. In this role, advisors support both incoming and current NSSR graduate scholars by facilitating enrollment procedures, degree requirement fulfillment, and various institutional administrative processes. This appointment carries a projected commitment of 10 to 25 hours per week for the duration of the academic year. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Electrician_JR109313": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Electrician", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW \u00a0 The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking to add highly qualified Overhire Electricians to join its Lighting department. Reporting to the Master Electrician, the Overhire Electrician is directly responsible for the installation of lighting equipment and systems under the direction of the Master Electrician in venues controlled by the College of Performing Arts, assigned university space, and outside venues as needed. This includes but is not limited to Drama productions, Operas, Concerts, experimental productions, external performance partnerships, and musicals.\u00a0 The Overhire Electrician is an integral and vital collaborator who thrives in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly team-oriented environment. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Lighting department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Implement the lighting designs for CoPA productions under the Direction of the Master Electrician or their designee\u00a0 Perform repair and maintenance of lighting equipment and systems in CoPA venues as assigned by the Master Electrician.\u00a0 Perform lighting hang, focus & strikes for CoPA Productions under the direction of the Master Electrician Perform work in compliance and under permits for all Fire, Health, and Safety matters for CoPA venues and productions in coordination with University partners as it relates to lighting or effects Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Previous experience as an entertainment electrician is required Exceptional ability to navigate challenging situations diplomatically. Interpersonal skills that allow the individual to function in a variety of settings among widely different groups of people The ability to work productively, graciously, and with an appropriate sense of humor under strict time constraints Understanding of the principles of entertainment lighting, lighting technology, low voltage LEDs, and electrical theory\u00a0 Ability to read and execute light plots Flexibility, and a \u201cteam player\u201d sensibility Knowledge of safety procedures for theatrical lighting, rigging, and safety when working at heights Flexible hours as needed, based on project assignment to include nights and weekends PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor\u2019s degree in Theatre Design and Technology\u00a0 Experience in an educational or teaching environment Experience working with ETC, EOS, and Paradigm control systems\u00a0 Experience working with multi-parameter lighting equipment\u00a0 WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite HOURLY RATE: $27.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/FWS---Welcome-Desk--Student-Assistant_JR109307": {"title": "FWS - Welcome Desk, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-14\nPart time\n\nThe College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Welcome Desk, Student Assistant. We\u2019re looking for friendly, organized, and responsible individuals to support initiatives within the College of Performing Arts. This position reports to the Coordinator of Administration and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in non-profit, performing arts, or higher education administration. Responsibilities: Serve as a primary service point for the College of Performing Arts Reception duties and answering general phone and email inquiries Clerical duties such as filing, copying, scanning and shredding Handling assignments from department heads, staff, and administration Maintaining organization of office and supplies Running errands on campus as requested by Performing Arts staff Minimum Qualifications: Recipient of Federal Work Study award Must be a current New School student Strong organization skills with the ability to work independently and manage multiple priorities. Self-motivated, proactive, detail-oriented, and responsible. Punctual and reliable. Excellent customer service skills. Technical proficiency with computer applications including the Google Suite and MS Office Ability to commit to a regular schedule for the duration of the semester Preferred Qualifications: Experience in customer service Experience in an administrative/office environment Work Mode : On-Campus Position #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17.00/hour Hours per Week: 10 This position is open to student applicants with a Federal Work Study award.\u200b We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Serves as the primary service point for the College of Performing Arts by managing reception, answering phone and email inquiries, and maintaining office organization. Executes daily clerical tasks, campus errands, and administrative assignments from department staff. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist---Academic-Support_JR109302": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist - Academic Support", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13\nPart time\n\nThe New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education. The Dean\u2019s Office at Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, the Adult Bachelor\u2019s Program, and The New School for Social Research seeks a Student Assistant Specialist to manage academic content at our college websites. RESPONSIBILITIES Research and update course samplings Manage departmental faculty pages Audit posted edits Cross-check that curricular requirements for academic offerings are consistent in all channels Check in weekly with a supervisor MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Matriculated New School student Applicants must have a Federal Work-Study (FWA) award since this position is paid for with Federal Work-Study funds. Organized and able to manage projects on one\u2019s own\u00a0 Patient and able to focus; Attentive to details Ability to communicate in a professional manner with faculty and staff Able to work 5 hours per week PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with a content management system is a plus but not necessary. The person in this position will be trained on our cms platform. WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate:\u00a0$18/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: This Student Assistant Specialist manages academic content at our websites (Eugene Lang College, the Adult Bachelor\u2019s Program, and The New School for Social Research). This work entails researching and updating course samplings, editing departmental faculty pages, and cross-checking curricular requirements for accuracy. If they complete their work in less than five hours in any given week and wish to work more to make up the difference, they will have the option to provide additional administrative support. The person in this position works independently for the most part although a supervisor will review much of their work before it is posted. They report to the Associate Director of Academic Communication. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Cooper-Hewitt-6th-Floor-Office-Student-Assistant_JR109292": {"title": "Cooper Hewitt 6th Floor Office Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-13\nPart time\n\nResponsibilities : The student assistants will be responsible for supporting the operations of the 6th-floor classroom and office in Cooper Hewitt (CH). Additional responsibilities include: Serve as the student liaisons between Cooper Hewitt and Parsons Contributing to the HDCS newsletter Collaborating with Cooper Hewitt security on escorting faculty, students, and external visitors without CH IDs inside the facility. Supporting HDCS-related events Managing the 6th-floor workspaces: classroom, admin office, computer room, and lounge. Maintaining supplies/inventory Supporting faculty with technology Qualification : Must have a Cooper Hewitt ID and current HDCS student. No previous experience is needed. Work Mode : On-Campus (Cooper Hewitt) #LI-DNI Availability: Office Hours are subject to change, mostly Wednesdays & Thursdays. Additional hours for work performed remotely relating to HDCS operations and for events when applicable. Office hours will be split and arranged between the Student Assistant in collaboration with the Associate Director of Operations, Luke Alan Davis. Rate: $18 per hour, 10 hours max per week. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student assistants will be responsible for supporting the operations of the 6th-floor classroom and office in Cooper Hewitt (CH). Additional responsibilities include serving as the student liaisons between Cooper Hewitt and Parsons Contributing to the HDCS newsletter. Collaborating with Cooper Hewitt security on escorting faculty, students, and external visitors without CH IDs inside the facility. Supporting HDCS-related events. Managing the 6th-floor workspaces: classroom, admin office, computer room, and lounge. Maintaining supplies/inventory. Supporting faculty with technology We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Research-Assistant---Eugene-Lang-College_JR109288": {"title": "Research Assistant - Eugene Lang College", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-12\nPart time\n\nLang seeks graduate students to serve as Research Assistants for departments, labs, and affiliated centers with good attention to detail, independent project management skills, and strong writing and organizational skills. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Conceive new research questions and assist faculty in developing continued research questions Support faculty in literature reviews Analyze data Prepare work for presentation and publication Please be sure to discuss with your supervisor the specific expectations for your assignment QUALIFICATIONS Prior office or administrative experience\u00a0 Strong writing skills Proficient in Google Suite Proficient / Advanced in Excel and Google Sheet with the ability to organize and manage large amounts of information Comfortable with all aspects of Zoom Ability to maintain to student privacy (FERPA) WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $31.37/hr This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The Research Assistant will support an Lang faculty member with research activities, including developing research questions, conducting literature reviews, analyzing data, and assisting with presentations and publications. The role requires strong writing, organizational, and project management skills, proficiency with Google Workspace, Excel, and Zoom, and the ability to work independently while maintaining confidentiality. This is a hybrid graduate student position that provides hands-on experience in academic research and scholarly collaboration. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/HEOP-Academic-Year-Mentor--FWS-_JR109257": {"title": "HEOP Academic Year Mentor (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10\nPart time\n\nThe New School Arthur O. Eve Higher Education Opportunity Program is a New York State Education Department\u2013funded program that offers financial, academic, and socio-emotional support for five years of undergraduate study and related educational activities. It is designed specifically for economically and educationally disadvantaged students who might not meet traditional college admissions criteria but demonstrate promise and potential for academic achievement and success.\u00a0 At The New School, the HEOP office is housed under the Student Affairs\u00a0 in which they will coordinate, evaluate, and administer academic, and operational activities of the program to foster student growth and success. HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.\u00a0 This is a one-academic-year position beginning Fall 26 through Spring 27,\u00a0 9 hours per week. RESPONSIBILITIES Support the HEOP program with event preparation including but not limited to transporting materials, decorating & arranging spaces, and event breakdown/clean-up Perform administrative duties related to the functional operation of the HEOP Space Communicate important information regularly to first-year HEOP students via email\u00a0 Contribute content on the official @NewSchoolStudentLife Instagram page in accordance with Student Success and university guidelines and policies. Assist with creating visual materials for college fairs, school visits, HEOP and New School Firsts specific events\u00a0 Assist HEOP students with challenges they may be experiencing by providing support and guidance Participate in the assessment efforts of HEOP by assisting in the administration of surveys to students, as well as filling out assessment tools aimed at understanding learning outcomes MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Passionate about access/opportunity programs Be open-minded, and culturally competent Takes initiative, works independently and collaboratively, strong work ethic\u00a0\u00a0 Meets deadlines promptly, communicates clearly, and asks for help when needed Uses good judgment, thinks critically and thoughtfully Must have a cumulative GPA of 3.0 or higher Assist students with the development of their study skills\u00a0 Knowledge of social media platforms such as but not limited to IG, Facebook, etc. Experience with researching, brainstorming, ideating, and creating programming and marketing materials Must be able to lift boxes weighing up to 35lbs\u00a0 Must be available to attend in person training on September 1st WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.\u00a0 \u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/FWS----Drama-Bank-Street-Programs--Student-Assistant_JR109276-1": {"title": "FWS -- Drama/Bank Street Programs, Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10\nPart time\n\nINTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH The School of Drama at the College of Performing Arts seeks a highly motivated student to serve as Drama/Bank Street Programs, Student Assistant. We\u2019re looking for an organized and enthusiastic individual with a self-starter disposition interested in all aspects of drama production & operations to support the School of Drama\u2019s programs at 151 Bank Street . This is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in non-profit, performing arts, or higher education administration.. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards. RESPONSIBILITIES Support daily operations within the School of Drama and Production programs with scheduling, paperwork, database upkeep, and general organizational/operational tasks Station Bank Street\u2019s Welcome Hub in Room 102 to answer student, faculty and visitor questions Assist with faculty copy/IT needs, mail distribution, stocking supplies, program printing, special events, and mainstage marketing support.\u00a0 Generate information and update College of Performing Arts Calendar; Post monthly on Call Board Support needs for classes / small projects Manage supply orders and restocking (tape, first aid, etc.) Create and circulate materials for the Arts Letters Maintain photo archives, scripts and production books Maintain physical supplies in Bank Street as requested Additional responsibilities as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Recipient of a Federal Work Study award. Ability to commit to a consistent weekly schedule. Excellent written and verbal communication, attention to detail, and group and independent problem solving skills Working knowledge of Google Suite: Drive, Calendar, etc General knowledge of theater practices and protocols Commitment to diversity and inclusion PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Current student in a College of Performing Arts degree program At least one semester at The New School/other university or equivalent professional experience. Interest in operations and production, administration, and/or creative technologies. Current student enrolled in a College of Performing Arts program WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $17.00 Hours per Week: up to 10-20 on average SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 Applicants must submit a cover letter, resume, and copy of their Federal Work Study award through their application. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.\u200b Responsibilities: Station at the Bank Street Welcome Hub to manage daily administrative operations, scheduling, database upkeep, and front-desk reception for the School of Drama and Production programs. Assist with faculty IT needs, supply inventory, calendar management, event marketing, production archiving, and the creation of Arts Letters materials. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Delivery-Services-Student-Assistant_JR109171": {"title": "Delivery Services Student Assistant _ FWS", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10\nPart time\n\nLocated in the heart of New York City, The New School has been a pioneering force in education, creativity, and innovation since its founding in 1919. With a progressive, globally recognized interdisciplinary model, the university is home to a diverse community of thousands of students from more than 100 countries. We offer over 135 degree and diploma programs across our esteemed colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The New School Libraries: Delivery Services Department is seeking 6+ Student Worker Library Assistants who will support the university's mission of preparing students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thereby making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Safely transport library books between various library locations and delivery points Properly package library books for shipment using appropriate materials to protect books from damage during transit Responsible for physically loading and unloading packages by hand, including placing, pushing, lifting, carrying, and pulling the items in an efficient and safe manner Inspect incoming and outgoing library books for damage, missing pages, or other issues, and report any discrepancies to the appropriate personnel Prepare shipping labels and necessary documentation for accurate tracking and delivery confirmation Coordinate with the supervisor to prioritize book delivery based on departmental demand and urgency Perform other duties as assigned by the department supervisor QUALIFICATIONS Ability to lift, push, or carry up to 50 lbs routinely Must have strong interpersonal and communication skills Attention to detail Must be punctual and capable of following written and verbal instructions as to how the packages must be moved Proficient in the safe handling and operation of sharp objects, including box cutters, utility knives, and other cutting tools Preference to individuals with experience operating heavy machinery such as motorized hand trucks Federal Work Study Award Letter WORK MODE: This position is in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17.00-$18.00 per hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? The New School Library plays a central role in supporting the university's mission to prepare students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thereby making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School.\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment based on race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce Responsibilities: Safely transport library materials between library locations, ensuring books are properly packaged, loaded, unloaded, and handled to prevent damage during transit. Inspect incoming and outgoing materials for damage or discrepancies, prepare shipping labels and documentation, and maintain accurate tracking of deliveries. Coordinate delivery priorities with the supervisor based on departmental needs and perform other duties as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/List-Center-Library-Student-Assistant_JR109170": {"title": "List Center Library Student Assistant - FWS", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10\nPart time\n\nLocated in the heart of New York City, The New School has been a pioneering force in education, creativity, and innovation since its founding in 1919. With a progressive, globally recognized interdisciplinary model, the university is home to a diverse community of thousands of students from more than 100 countries. We offer over 135 degree and diploma programs across our esteemed colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The New School Libraries are seeking 6+ Student Worker Library Assistants who will support the university's mission of preparing students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thereby making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with supervisor Monitor patrons entering and exiting the library and be ready to assist them at the circulation desk Check in and check out library materials to patrons at the desk using library software Stacks maintenance including shelving, in-housing books, shelf reading, etc. Answer the telephone Help patrons find items in the stacks and in the library catalog Assist patrons with library printing Other library-related tasks such as scanning books, paging books, preparing reserve books, and other duties assigned by staff Assist with the opening or closing procedures in the library depending on shift time Federal Word Study Award Letter QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills\u00a0 Ability to understand and follow verbal and written instructions and communicate clearly with library users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Physical ability to lift items and push loaded book carts. Accommodations can be made if needed. WORK MODE : This position is in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17.00-$18.00 per hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? The New School Library plays a central role in supporting the university's mission of preparing students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thus making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School.\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment based on race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce Responsibilities: Students provide excellent customer service by assisting patrons with circulation services, locating library resources, and answering general inquiries. Support daily library operations through collection maintenance, opening and closing procedures, and other assigned tasks to ensure a safe, organized, and welcoming environment. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/SAS-Media-Studies-Film-Office-Tech-Assistant_JR109205": {"title": "SAS Media Studies Film Office Tech Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Media Studies Film Office within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Student Film Office Techs: Support Film Office managers with Film Office duties by maintaining film equipment and resources Assist\u00a0 faculty and students with equipment.\u00a0 Work with additional online resources including (websites, web-based platforms\u00a0 Assist with on-site check ins and checkouts of camera and grip equipment Student Classroom Student Assistants: Attend all class or assigned workshops sessions- Classroom Technicians will have to arrive early to checkout equipment, and stay to the end of class to pack and return equipment. support faculty and students in use of Film Office equipment.\u00a0 Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Must be an active continuing matriculated student Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE On-Campus Position \u00a0-\u00a0 Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $17.00hr/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provided customer service and front-desk support in a diverse and inclusive learning environment, assisting students, faculty, and staff with equipment reservations, resource requests, and departmental services. Maintained shop, classroom, and equipment spaces through organization, upkeep, troubleshooting, and equipment management, while supporting daily operations, training sessions, and special projects. Assisted faculty and students with classroom and Film Office equipment, including check-in/check-out procedures, technical support, and maintaining equipment resources. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---Parson-Admission_JR109259": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant - Parson Admission", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nPart time\n\nParsons\u2019 student assistants work collaboratively in the Parsons admission department responding to, cataloging as necessary, and fielding general inquiries, maintaining digital records, preparing recruitment materials for and participating in various virtual and in-person events as needed, and performing various administrative tasks including responding to email, and representing Parsons School of Design and its admission office from the current student-perspective. The incumbent will assist with inquiries directed to Parsons from internal and external stakeholders, including prospective students and families, applicants, and admitted and deposited students. Parsons\u2019 student assistants report directly to the assistant and associate directors of Parsons\u2019 admission offices. \u00a0 We are looking for a dynamic, motivated individual who can work independently but is also a team player. The ideal candidates for this position are undergraduate and graduate students interested in leveraging their positive experiences at Parsons, and working on a small team, to field inquiries from prospective students, families, and other constituents.\u00a0\u00a0 Experience in an office setting or customer service is a plus. Applicants must be enrolled in an undergraduate or graduate program at Parsons School of Design at The New School. Students from all of Parsons\u2019 undergraduate and graduate academic disciplines are encouraged to apply. Strong writing and speaking skills are essential. Applicants will be asked to complete a brief writing assignment during the application process. They will be assigned to undergraduate admission if enrolled in an undergraduate program and graduate admission if enrolled in a graduate program. Priority will be given to applicants who hold federal work-study awards. Responsibilities include: Monitoring Parsons admission and recruitment inbox inquiries and fielding replies Draft email response templates for commonly asked questions Provide administrative support to Parsons\u2019 admission team Assist with admission and recruitment events as needed, including speaking about your experiences as a Parsons student, and representing both Parsons and its admissions offices Additional project-based tasks as needed including virtual and in-person meetings with prospective students and their families Desired Qualifications: Customer service experience or administrative office experience Excellent verbal and written communication skills Strong understanding of the Parsons student experience and programming Experience working independently and expediently with limited hands-on supervision Proficiency with Google Drive Ability to demonstrate discretion in handling confidential applicant information Ability to perform multiple tasks with effective time management and prioritization Availability to work 15-20 hours weekly\u00a0 Hourly Rate: $17\u00a0 Work Mode: Hybrid/Remote - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice . Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus, when necessary, to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Special Instructions to Applicants : Please upload all Required Applicant Documents to be considered:\u00a0 Resume/CV Cover Letter Federal Work Study Award We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Work collaboratively in the Parsons admission department responding to, cataloging as necessary, and fielding general inquiries, maintaining digital records, preparing recruitment materials for and participating in various virtual and in-person events as needed, and performing various administrative tasks including responding to email, and representing Parsons School of Design and its admission office from the current student-perspective We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/HEOP-Academic-Year-Mentor--OCSE-_JR109256": {"title": "HEOP Academic Year Mentor (OCSE)", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nPart time\n\nThe New School Arthur O. Eve Higher Education Opportunity Program is a New York State Education Department\u2013funded program that offers financial, academic, and socio-emotional support for five years of undergraduate study and related educational activities. It is designed specifically for economically and educationally disadvantaged students who might not meet traditional college admissions criteria but demonstrate promise and potential for academic achievement and success.\u00a0 At The New School, the HEOP office is housed under the Student Affairs\u00a0 in which they will coordinate, evaluate, and administer academic, and operational activities of the program to foster student growth and success. HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.\u00a0 This is a one-academic-year position beginning Fall 26 through Spring 27,\u00a0 9 hours per week. RESPONSIBILITIES Support the HEOP program with event preparation including but not limited to transporting materials, decorating & arranging spaces, and event breakdown/clean-up Perform administrative duties related to the functional operation of the HEOP Space Communicate important information regularly to first-year HEOP students via email\u00a0 Contribute content on the official @NewSchoolStudentLife Instagram page in accordance with Student Success and university guidelines and policies. Assist with creating visual materials for college fairs, school visits, HEOP and New School Firsts specific events\u00a0 Assist HEOP students with challenges they may be experiencing by providing support and guidance Participate in the assessment efforts of HEOP by assisting in the administration of surveys to students, as well as filling out assessment tools aimed at understanding learning outcomes MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Passionate about access/opportunity programs Be open-minded, and culturally competent Takes initiative, works independently and collaboratively, strong work ethic\u00a0\u00a0 Meets deadlines promptly, communicates clearly, and asks for help when needed Uses good judgment, thinks critically and thoughtfully Must have a cumulative GPA of 3.0 or higher Assist students with the development of their study skills\u00a0 Knowledge of social media platforms such as but not limited to IG, Facebook, etc. Experience with researching, brainstorming, ideating, and creating programming and marketing materials Must be able to lift boxes weighing up to 35lbs\u00a0 Must be available to attend in person training on September 1st WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE $18 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: HEOP Mentors will report to the HEOP Manager. HEOP mentors will support the HEOP program including supporting events from prep to breakdown, assist with administrative tasks, assist HEOP students through email and in person. HEOP Mentors will also contribute to content on social media and assist with visuals for promotional material.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---Lang-Admission_JR109248": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant - Lang Admission", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant - Eugene Lang College Admission plays an important role in supporting the Undergraduate Admission team by serving as a knowledgeable and welcoming resource for prospective students and families. Working collaboratively with the admission staff, this position assists with responding to inquiries, maintaining digital records, supporting recruitment initiatives, and representing the student experience through one-on-one conversations and admission events. The Student Assistant helps create a personalized and informative experience for prospective students while contributing to the daily operations of the admission office. The successful candidate is an organized, dependable, and enthusiastic communicator who enjoys working both independently and collaboratively. The ideal candidate demonstrates strong writing and interpersonal skills, exercises sound judgment when handling confidential information, and is eager to share their experience as a current Eugene Lang student. This position reports to the Assistant Director of Admission. Priority consideration will be given to current Eugene Lang College students. Students from all academic disciplines are encouraged to apply, provided they have a strong understanding of the Lang student experience and academic programs. Responsibilities: Respond to prospective student and family inquiries through the Lang Admission email inbox and other communication channels. Meet individually with prospective students and families to share the Eugene Lang student experience and answer questions about campus life and academics. Represent Eugene Lang College at undergraduate admission events, including information sessions, open houses, admitted student programs, and virtual events. Draft and maintain email templates and other communication resources for frequently asked questions. Assist with administrative projects, data entry, and digital record maintenance using university systems. Support the creation of social media and other digital content that promotes Eugene Lang College and student life. Complete additional project-based assignments and departmental duties as needed. Minimum Qualifications:\u00a0 Current undergraduate student in good academic standing. Strong understanding of the Eugene Lang College student experience and academic programs. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Customer service, peer leadership, or student-facing experience. Ability to work independently while managing multiple priorities and meeting deadlines. Proficiency with Microsoft Office and Google Workspace. Ability to exercise discretion and maintain confidentiality when handling applicant information. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Preferred Qualifications:\u00a0 Previous experience working in admissions, higher education, student leadership, orientation, or another customer service environment. Experience creating content for social media or digital communications. Familiarity with Slate or other CRM/database systems. Public speaking or presentation experience. Hourly Rate :\u00a0 $17.00 per hour Work Mode:\u00a0 Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.\u00a0#LI-DNI SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Please upload all Required Applicant Documents to be considered:\u00a0 Resume/CV Cover Letter Federal Work Study Award We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Assist the Lang Admission Office with prospective student outreach, inquiry management, event support, and administrative operations. Serve as a student ambassador by sharing firsthand experiences, creating communication materials, and supporting initiatives that strengthen recruitment and engagement. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/No-Campus-Location/Director-of-Payroll_JR109261": {"title": "Director of Payroll Services", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to approximately\u00a0 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The Human Resources\u2019 office promotes activities which enhance the quality of work life and heighten the sense of community. The office provides guidance, administers a myriad of services to employees with continual efficiency and effectiveness, and handles employee problems with care and professionalism.\u00a0 Our open-door policy welcomes all employees encouraging them to fully utilize the office\u2019s services. Employees' matters are treated with respect, care, and confidentiality. Professionalism, capable management, collaboration, and progressive engagement are the cornerstones of office operations.\u00a0 Reporting to the Assistant Vice President for HR Operations, the Director of Payroll Services leads a team of experienced payroll professionals and directs the payroll function for faculty, administrative staff, and student workers. In addition to ensuring that the university meets its financial commitment to employees by providing accurate and timely salary and wage payments, this position collaborates with Information Technology/Workday Solutions Group and other Human Resources functional areas to implement strategic projects aimed to enhance the employee experience. The successful candidate will be dedicated to providing accessible and transparent payroll processes for employees, supporting a culture of care, and fostering an equitable and inclusive workplace culture.\u00a0 The Director of Payroll Services needs to be a collaborative partner that demonstrates both personal and professional integrity. They are transformative, collaborative, flexible and creative but can also be a hands-on problem solver with a track record of designing and delivering customer-focused services modeled on best practices.\u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES AND PRIMARY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Payroll Operations & Systems Strategy Direct end-to-end weekly and biweekly payroll processing across faculty, staff, and student workers, ensuring accuracy, timeliness, and total compliance within the Workday Payroll ecosystem.\u00a0 Serve as the primary technical and functional Workday Payroll subject matter expert (SME), & managing backend configurations.\u00a0 Manages processing in the university\u2019s Workday Payroll system, and plans and manages the implementation of Workday system changes and updates. Oversee strategic vendor relationships with ADP for tax filing, wage garnishments, and regulatory processing, ensuring seamless integration between Workday and ADP systems.\u00a0 Updates earning and deduction codes as needed, and coordinates with the university\u2019s Finance office to update GL posting rules. Architect and maintain inbound/outbound integration files between Workday Payroll, benefit providers, financial platforms, and third-party vendors to safeguard end-to-end data integrity.\u00a0 Manages payment requests for garnishments. Lead end-to-end Payroll testing strategies, including tenant updates, regression testing, semi-annual Workday feature releases, and custom integration validation.\u00a0 Initiates and controls W-2 processing and reconciling. Compliance and Regulatory Oversight Develops and maintains processes to ensure best practices, internal controls, and compliance with local, state, and federal regulations and the University\u2019s policies and procedures. Oversees Non-Resident Alien processing for faculty, staff, and student workers to ensure appropriate W-2/1042-S reporting and tax withholding. Remains current with United States and New York City and State payroll-related regulations and tax laws. Issue Resolution and Data Integrity Proactively identifies, researches, analyzes, and resolves payroll-related issues and discrepancies. Collaborates with University departments to ensure system and data integrity in the university\u2019s Workday system and all related systems. Leadership and Staff Development Lead, mentor, and empower the payroll team, cultivating a collaborative, continuous-learning environment that promotes professional growth, high performance, and operational excellence\u00a0 Drive large-scale strategic payroll projects, managing cross-functional initiatives from initial scoping and business process re-engineering through implementation and post-go-live optimization.\u00a0 Streamline end-to-end year-end processing, directing W-2, 1042-S, and multi-state/local tax reconciliation processes with high precision and error-free execution. \u200b Audit and Financial Collaboration Works collaboratively with the university\u2019s Finance office in leading preparation of payroll information for the university\u2019s annual audit and other audits as needed. Time and Attendance Management Maintains non-union and union time off and time tracking processes, including system process improvements. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications: Seven years of payroll management experience in a mid-sized or large organization Strong Excel and other Microsoft Office application skills Workday Payroll\u00a0 Demonstrated effective communication and reporting skills Bachelor\u2019s Degree required Preferred Qualifications: Familiarity with Sprintax Calculus software Five or more years of experience with Workday payroll processing Experience managing Payroll in a university environment Experience working with ADP for payroll and tax processing Certified Payroll Professional certification WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE: \u00a0 $120,000 - $130,000 per annum WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness\u00a0 Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Additional Perks Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/OCSE-Senior-Student-Assistant-Specialist---Welcome-Center_JR109244-1": {"title": "OCSE Senior Student Assistant Specialist - Welcome Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-06\nPart time\n\nThe New School is seeking enthusiastic, outgoing, and dependable graduate students to serve as Senior Student Assistant Specialists in the University Welcome Center . Senior Student Assistant Specialists play an important role in welcoming prospective students, families, and other visitors to The New School and helping them learn more about our academic programs, student experience, and admission process. They lead in-person campus tours and support virtual and on-campus recruitment programs and events, serving as knowledgeable and authentic representatives of the New School student experience. In addition to these visitor-facing responsibilities, Senior Student Assistant Specialists provide operational and peer leadership within the Welcome Center . They assist with student staff onboarding, training, and scheduling; visitor communications; recruitment logistics; and special projects. They may also help develop student-focused content for Welcome Center social media, online admitted student communities, and the New School Parent and Family Hub. This position is ideal for a graduate student who enjoys working with people, demonstrates strong organizational and communication skills, and is comfortable balancing public-facing responsibilities with administrative and operational work. Senior Student Assistant Specialists are responsible for: Serving as a welcoming, knowledgeable, and professional representative of The New School and creating a positive first impression for prospective students, families, and other campus visitors. Leading engaging in-person campus tours and supporting virtual and on-campus admission programs and events. Maintaining current knowledge of The New School's academic programs, student services and resources, campus facilities, and admission processes and requirements. Assisting with the scheduling and coordination of student tour guides, private group visits, and other Welcome Center activities. Supporting student staff onboarding, peer training, and ongoing professional development. Providing on-site operational and administrative leadership during select weekday and Saturday visit programs. Assisting with visitor communications and maintaining accurate campus visit and participation data. Coordinating recruitment material shipments and maintaining Welcome Center materials and inventory. Contributing content and student perspectives to Welcome Center social media, online admitted student communities, and parent and family engagement platforms. Supporting special projects and other Admission and Welcome Center initiatives as assigned. \u200b Qualifications: Must be a currently enrolled New School graduate student. Previous administrative or office experience required. Strong interpersonal, communication, and organizational skills. Comfortable speaking to and engaging with individuals and groups. Ability to provide accurate information while communicating in a warm, approachable, and professional manner. Demonstrated reliability, attention to detail, and ability to manage multiple responsibilities. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Interest in mentoring and supporting fellow student employees. Familiarity with The New School's academic programs, resources, and student experience preferred. Pay Rate: $21/hour Work Mode: Hybrid/Split: Employees hired for this position have a regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (e.g., 1\u20134 times per week) and also work some of the time remotely. Because of the visitor-facing nature of this position, the majority of scheduled hours will take place in person at the University Welcome Center, 2 West 13th Street. #LI-DNI The New School is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Senior Student Assistant Specialists play an important role in welcoming prospective students, families, and other visitors to The New School and helping them learn more about our academic programs, student experience, and admission process. They lead in-person campus tours and support virtual and on-campus recruitment programs and events, serving as knowledgeable and authentic representatives of the New School student experience. In addition to these visitor-facing responsibilities, Senior Student Assistant Specialists provide operational and peer leadership within the Welcome Center. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/Director-of-Alumni-and-Volunteer-Engagement_JR109230": {"title": "Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-06\nFull time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. \u00a0 POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Director of Alumni and Volunteer Engagement provides strategic and operational leadership for The New School\u2019s university-wide alumni engagement program. The Director develops and advances a comprehensive strategy that strengthens alumni connection, supports institutional priorities, and creates meaningful opportunities for alumni to engage with students, faculty, fellow alumni, and the university. \u00a0 Reporting to the Assistant Vice President for Alumni & Community Engagement, the Director sets goals and priorities and supervises staff responsible for school-based and university-wide engagement initiatives. The Director directly leads alumni engagement for Parsons and Performing Arts while ensuring that alumni across all New School schools and programs receive coordinated, relevant, and inclusive opportunities for lifelong connection. \u00a0 The Director oversees regional and affinity engagement, reunions, events, communications, digital communities, volunteer leadership, and data-informed outreach. The role establishes shared standards, manages resources, assesses performance, and collaborates closely with Development and Alumni Engagement colleagues, academic leadership, faculty, and other university partners. \u00a0 Alumni Engagement Develop and implement a comprehensive university-wide alumni engagement strategy aligned with institutional and Development and Alumni Engagement priorities while recognizing the distinct identities of The New School\u2019s schools and programs\u00a0 Establish annual goals, priorities, work plans, timelines, and measures of success for the alumni engagement team. Oversee the strategic development and execution of an annual slate of in-person and virtual alumni initiatives, including regional, industry, affinity, reunion, volunteer, and community-building opportunities. Expand alumni participation across career and life stages, geographic regions, industries, affinities, interests, and levels of prior engagement. Manage alumni engagement resources and align investments with strategic priorities. Direct Reunion strategy, planning, communications, and execution in partnership with Development and Alumni Engagement and school-based colleagues. Lead the development and management of the Executive Alumni Council of The New School and provide strategic direction for Alumni Council committees. Manage relationships with alumni leaders and volunteers, supporting their involvement and deepening their connection to The New School. Develop alumni leaders and volunteers who serve as informed ambassadors and advocates for The New School. Partner with student-facing offices to strengthen alumni-to-student engagement and create pathways that support the transition from student to active alum. Build relationships with industry leaders, employers, and external partners that expand engagement opportunities for alumni and students. Represent Alumni Engagement on university-wide committees and special initiatives and build partnerships that strengthen alumni and student engagement. \u00a0 Staff Leadership and University Partnerships Supervise alumni engagement staff, including setting team priorities, coordinating workflows, establishing expectations, and ensuring accountability for program execution and results. Promote collaboration and information sharing across the Development and Alumni Engagement division. Partner with deans, academic leaders, faculty, and school-based colleagues to identify priorities and advance alumni engagement plans. Build and maintain productive relationships across the university to support coordinated alumni engagement. \u00a0 Communications, Data, and Assessment Guide segmented outreach strategies based on alumni interests, industries, affiliations, geographic locations, career stages, and engagement preferences. Direct the strategy, messaging, and development of alumni communications in partnership with the Associate Director of Alumni and Community Engagement Communications Establish standards for the accurate and timely entry, review, and maintenance of alumni engagement information in Salesforce and lead efforts to improve data quality and completeness. Establish performance measures for volunteer engagement, mentoring, programming, digital activity, communications, data quality, and program outcomes. Use performance data and alumni feedback to assess effectiveness, identify engagement gaps, refine audience strategies, allocate resources, and guide future planning. \u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Five to seven years of progressively responsible professional experience in higher education, advancement, alumni relations, or constituent engagement. Strong presentation and public-speaking skills, with the ability to represent the university effectively in varied settings. \u00a0Demonstrated record of designing, growing, and evaluating programs, communications, and events, including large-scale initiatives. Exceptional organizational, verbal, written, interpersonal, and relationship-management skills, with the ability to engage effectively with varied audiences. Strong leadership and collaboration skills, with the ability to build trust, establish credibility, and work effectively across departments, academic units, and leadership teams. Sound judgment, discretion, diplomacy, and the ability to navigate complex organizational relationships. \u00a0Entrepreneurial and solutions-oriented approach, with the ability to anticipate needs, adapt to changing priorities, and creatively address challenges. \u00a0Experience using data and performance measures to evaluate engagement, improve programs, and guide strategic decisions. Demonstrated proficiency with CRM systems, online community platforms, event registration tools, email marketing systems, or comparable technology. Experience developing and managing segmented communications and digital engagement strategies. Strong commitment to The New School\u2019s mission and to accessible, inclusive, and audience-centered engagement. Availability to work some evenings and weekends and travel occasionally, as required. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-Onsite SALARY RANGE: $108,000- $112,000 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. \u00a0 BENEFITS AT THE NEW SCHOOL The New School is committed to supporting the well-being and success of our faculty and staff. We offer a comprehensive benefits package designed to meet the diverse needs of our employees and their eligible dependents. Highlights include: Health & Wellness Comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Financial Wellness Retirement plan administered by TIAA Qualified Transportation Expense Plan Work-Life Balance Paid vacation and sick leave, with accruals starting on your first day Paid holidays and floating holidays Workmode & Telework Policy (if applicable) Professional Growth Career advancement and professional development opportunities Access to university programs, lectures, and events featuring world-renowned speakers and organizations Tuition waiver benefit for eligible employees and dependents Employee discounts with local vendors and service providers \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant---Making-Center_JR108986": {"title": "Student Assistant - Making Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-06\nPart time\n\nParsons School of Design Parsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in various shops within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open work spaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0Duties for each space vary slightly, but entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills.\u00a0 Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users.\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail.\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Physical ability to lift items.\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 WORK MODE On-campus position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work that is not feasible, or not in the organization's best interest, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE \u00a0 $17.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant--FWS----Dean-s-Office-Eugene-Lang_JR109210": {"title": "Student Assistant (FWS) - Dean's Office Eugene Lang", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-03\nPart time\n\nThere are currently several short-term (1-year) Federal Work Study employment positions open in the Dean's Office at Eugene Lang College. Ideal applicants must possess a positive attitude; an ability to work well with staff, faculty, and students; attention to detail; schedule flexibility (especially early mornings); and punctuality. Student workers will be expected to maintain a professional attitude and treat all materials and information they encounter with the utmost discretion. Student workers will assist with department events, marketing, and logistics as needed. Candidates will be paid through the federal work study program. As such, only students actively enrolled in the work-study program are eligible for this role.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES General Administrative Position Duties and Responsibilities:\u00a0 Greet visitors and answer phones: direct callers and visitors to appropriate sources or information\u00a0 Assist Lang College staff in short-term and long-term projects\u00a0 Maintain Dean's office files and assist in office communication\u00a0 Sort mail, make copies/PDFs, file and shred documents\u00a0 Email staff/faculty and check ELC Dean e-mail address as needed\u00a0 Deliver mail and packages to other offices as needed (on and off campus)\u00a0 Set up and organize for office events\u00a0 Stock and maintain supply rooms, copy rooms, basement printers and faculty resource room Maintain general neatness and organization of Dean's office, including the kitchen pantry and conference room\u00a0 Ensure the Millimeter Reading Room is organized and tidy Design promotional materials (posters, flyers, PDFs, JPEG, graphics) geared to students for our department events as needed Distribute posters/flyers Greet and register event guests\u00a0 Proof copy and branding for marketing materials and digital event promo Transcribe audio or edit transcriptions as needed Assist Senior Secretaries with other general office duties as assigned MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Adaptable and enthusiastic attitude, strong work ethic, proactive stance\u00a0 Self-starter, able to work independently Able to take direction and responsibility for assignments, follows through on jobs until completion Professional demeanor and strong customer service skills Good communication skills Strong organization skills and interest in office administration\u00a0 Proficiency in Google Apps, Microsoft Office Suite, and internet research\u00a0 Basic understanding of design principles PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with data entry and working in Excel databases\u00a0 Understanding of University divisions and programs\u00a0 Strong written and verbal communication skills \u00a0 Experience with flyer design (Canva or comparable) a plus Experience with Adobe Suite (Photoshop/Illustrator/Acrobat/Publisher) a plus WORK MODE : On-Campus Position ,Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $18 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 Responsibilities: The student assistant greets visitors to the 3rd floor of Eugene Lang College and provides assistance as needed. The position also supports Dean\u2019s Office staff and faculty as needed and monitors the copy room/mailboxes/supply closet. Additional duties include posting flyers/notices around the 11th and 12th Street buildings as well as other TNS buildings. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/University-Center-Library-Student-Assistant_JR109169": {"title": "University Center Library Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-08-03\nPart time\n\nFWS Student Worker Library Assistant University Center Library\u00a0 Located in the heart of New York City, The New School has been a pioneering force in education, creativity, and innovation since its founding in 1919. With a progressive, globally recognized interdisciplinary model, the university is home to a diverse community of thousands of students from more than 100 countries. We offer over 135 degree and diploma programs across our esteemed colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The New School Libraries are seeking 60+ Student Worker Library Assistants who will support the university's mission of preparing students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thereby making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with supervisor Monitor patrons entering and exiting the library and be ready to assist them at the circulation desk Check-in and check-out library materials to patrons at the desk using library software Stacks maintenance including shelving, in-housing books, shelf reading, etc. Answer the telephone Help patrons find items in the stacks and in the library catalog Assist patrons with library printing Other library-related tasks such as scanning books, paging books, preparing reserve books, and other duties assigned by staff Assist with the opening or closing procedures in the library depending on shift time QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills\u00a0 Ability to understand and follow verbal and written instructions and communicate clearly with library users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Physical ability to lift items and push loaded book carts. Accommodations can be made if needed. WORK MODE: This position is in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE: $17.00-$18.00 per hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? The New School Library plays a central role in supporting the university's mission of preparing students to understand, contribute to, and succeed in a rapidly changing society, thus making the world a better and more just place. Our work contributes to this mission by offering consistent and reliable service to all students, faculty, and staff of The New School.\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Students provide excellent customer service by assisting patrons with circulation services, locating library resources, and answering general inquiries. Support daily library operations through collection maintenance, opening and closing procedures, and other assigned tasks to ensure a safe, organized, and welcoming environment. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Part-Time-Teaching-Staff_JR109164": {"title": "Part-Time Teaching Staff", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz & Contemporary Music at The New School is seeking highly qualified candidates to join its roster of Part-Time Teaching Staff. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide dedicated mentorship and instruction to students within the School of Jazz & Contemporary Music Serve as a substitute instructor within the School of Jazz & Contemporary Music Assess student learning outcomes and aid student success and individual artistic growth Submit grades in a timely manner MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated success as a performing artist, with significant professional accomplishments and accolades 2-3 years of teaching experience with a variety of teaching methods and curricular perspectives, preferably in a College, University, and/or Conservatory Ability to work with diverse students and colleagues, fostering inclusive classroom environments Proven track record with achieving learning outcomes as defined by curriculum and course syllabi PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS A Bachelor\u2019s, advanced, or terminal degree in the field of specialty or equivalent experience (BM, MM, DMA, PH.D or equivalent) WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Jazz Private Lessons - $156.00/hour Part-Time Teaching Staff Substitute - $120.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Part-Time-Faculty--Local-802_JR109165-1": {"title": "Part-Time Faculty, Local 802", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz & Contemporary Music at the College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking highly qualified candidates to join its roster of Part-Time Faculty. This is a Local 802 - American Federation of Musicians unit position as per the collective bargaining agreement between AFM Local 802 and the University. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching graduate and/or undergraduate students within a defined field of expertise Developing and managing course syllabi and ensuring that syllabi meet Program and School standards Planning and creating lectures, discussions, and course assignments Assessing student learning outcomes and aiding student success Submitting grades in a timely manner MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent experience\u00a0 Demonstrated success as a creative, teaching, and/or performing artist, with significant professional accomplishments and accolades 2-3 years of teaching experience with a variety of teaching methods and curricular perspectives, preferably in a College, University, and/or Conservatory Ability to work with diverse students and colleagues, fostering inclusive classroom settings Proven track record with achieving learning outcomes PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS An advanced, or terminal degree in the field of specialty (MM, MA, DMA, PH.D or equivalent) WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Local 802 Part-Time Faculty are compensated at the following minimum hourly rates for the duration of the 2026-27 Academic Year: Jazz Private Lessons - $156.00/hour Jazz Classroom - $156.00/hour Rates are subject to increase per contractual obligations as outlined in the Collective Bargaining Agreement between Local 802 and The New School. WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E3-Light-Lab-Student-Assistant_JR109161": {"title": "E3 Light Lab Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the E4 Wood, Metal, Tool Checkout, and Light lab within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 \u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls / emails professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Help students checkout appropriate tools for their projects. Inspect tools upon return and address issues such as damage, missing parts, or cleanliness. Maintain tool organization within the shop Using ladders and assisting in the installation of lighting equipment (specific to Light Lab Student Employees) Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 Opening and closing the shop as assigned (unlocking resources, logging into computers, etc.). Processing / recycling materials. Receiving and organizing deliveries. Tracking and moving inventory. Assist with store support, including: Procuring purchased items for students and faculty.\u00a0 Processing and preparing materials (including but not limited to wood, metal, plaster, clay and alginate). Counting and tracking inventory. Moving and organizing materials (up to 25 lbs, occasionally up to 50 lbs). Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Replace tool parts in kits and sets. Restock consumables. Change blades and other machine components as needed. Daily maintenance and cleaning of machinery Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness, including: Dust control including vacuuming and mopping Restocking PPE Clearing surfaces and resetting machines\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17.00 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Provide a high level of customer service, assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests , assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance, perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness,\u00a0 and complete special projects as assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Sign-Language--Tutor_JR109140": {"title": "Sign Language  Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student\u2019s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student\u2019s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator. \u00a0 Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training.\u00a0 Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. Set up their STARFISH office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application.\u00a0 Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus.\u00a0 WORK MODE: Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate :\u00a0U$29.20 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon). We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/FWS-Student-Assistant---Content-Creator--Admission-Communications-_JR109123": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant - Content Creator (Admission Communications)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nPart time\n\nThe New School is looking for an enthusiastic, responsible, creative individual with a Federal Work Study Award to join our Admission Communications team as a Content Creator. This position is hybrid, with required on-campus content creation, weekly in-person office time, mandatory staff meetings, and mandatory event coverage. This position is an important, integral part of Admission Communications and Welcome Center work, and essential to the success of Enrollment Management\u2019s mission. Our Content Team members connect with and create content for prospective students, applicants, parents, and families on Instagram, ZeeMee, CampusESP, and other communications platforms. They also engage with our audiences at in-person and online events. Importantly, individual Content Creators work independently and collaboratively, contributing to a positive, enjoyable work environment. Applications without work samples will not be considered. Click the \u201cAdd\u201d button on the \u201cWebsites\u201d part of the \u201cMy Experience\u201d section of the application to share a link to work samples in a portfolio of your relevant work. Your work sample portfolio can include website or email design, social media strategy and design, graphic design, media editing, and/or writing samples. Your portfolio can be a website, or a collection of work on a Google Doc or Google Slides. Candidates considered for this role will be asked to complete a creative assessment/video editing exercise. Finalists may be asked to provide references who can evaluate qualifications and overall fit for the position. Candidates will be contacted at their New School email addresses after a review of all applications submitted for the position. Successful candidates will: Meet all content deadlines, attend all content team meetings, and represent Admission Communications at in-person and online events Check in daily with Content Managers and Welcome Center professional administrators on our Slack workspace Be comfortable in front of the camera Create dynamic, authentic, approachable multi-media content for our communications platforms that provides an honest view and personal insight into student life Learn and understand our different audiences and platforms, including prospective students, applicants, parents/families, Instagram, ZeeMee, and CampusESP Enjoy connecting with and assisting our audiences, answering questions about student life and admission processes, and providing support Enjoy collaborating with professional staff and other student team members Contribute to a friendly, fun working environment Understand this is a position within a team where all team members are responsible to each other and rely on each other in different ways Be a positive, dependable, responsible resource for our team Complete other content creation and duties as assigned Manage their own time Commit to working 5-8 hours per week Understand this is job position is an important, integral part of Admission Communications and Welcome Center work, and essential to the success of Enrollment Management\u2019s mission Minimum Qualifications:\u00a0 Applicants must be currently enrolled in a degree program at The New School. Additional qualifications include: Federal Work Study Award Familiarity with and enthusiasm for social network sites, social media tools, and associated apps and technology Excellent writing and editing skills Strong video editing skills with desire to learn and troubleshoot challenges Impeccable attention to detail and aesthetics Sound judgment Positive attitude Excellent communicator Highly organized--enjoys organization Self-motivated\u00a0 Enjoys assisting others and providing support Able to focus without being distracted by cell phones, personal social media, and competing responsibilities Familiarity with Instagram, Zeemee, and CampusESP Familiarity with Google applications including Sheets, Docs, Forms, and Slides Extensive experience with Canva video, photo, and design capabilities Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $17 per hour\u00a0 Work Mode:\u00a0 Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Please upload all Required Applicant Documents to be considered:\u00a0 Resume/CV Cover Letter \u200bWork Sample Federal Work Study Award We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Please upload your Work Sample and Federal Work Study Award letter if you have one to the \u201cCover Letter and Resume/CV\u201d part of the \u201cMy Experience\u201d section of the application. Responsibilities: Our Content Team members connect with and create content for prospective students, applicants, parents, and families on Instagram, ZeeMee, CampusESP, and other communications platforms. They also engage with our audiences at in-person and online events. Importantly, individual Content Creators work independently and collaboratively, contributing to a positive, enjoyable work environment. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/A---66-West-12th-Street/FWS-Front-Desk-Student-Assistant--Creative-Writing_JR109131": {"title": "FWS Front Desk Student Assistant, Creative Writing", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-21\nPart time\n\nThe New School for Social Research (NSSR) is seeking a Front Office Student Assistant to work in the Creative Writing Program. The Creative Writing Program supports New School students, faculty, administration, and the New York City community. We are a busy office composed of staff members, faculty, graduate and undergraduate students.\u00a0 The ideal candidate should be reliable and motivated to help with day-to-day operations in a busy, fast-paced office. Selected candidates will be familiarized with Creative Writing\u2019s academic programs, course offerings, events, and curriculum. This position is expected to work on-site Mondays - Thursdays, up to 20 hours per week. Responsibilities include: Assist with in-person, telephone, and email inquiries\u00a0 Direct calls/emails/requests for faculty, administrators, or other departments/programs\u00a0 Assist with clerical support for Creative Writing staff and faculty as assigned, including data entry, filing, and other projects Other duties as assigned\u00a0 Minimum Qualifications: Professional with excellent interpersonal, customer service and communication (written and verbal) skills Self-motivated, proactive and able to work independently and use good judgement Punctual and reliable Tech savvy with command of Google Drive, Zoom Ability to proofread and a keen eye for details\u00a0\u00a0 Friendly demeanor and a positive attitude Federal work study funding Preferred Qualifications: Students whose concentration is writing and literature are particularly encouraged to apply Proficiency in Adobe Suite a plus Prior experience in an administrative support position Workmode : This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17 - $19/hr \u00a0 To apply, submit a cover letter and resume. Please include a scan of your FWS authorization. Be sure to include the days and times you\u2019re available to work. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The student employee will serve as the front desk office assistant and assist with in-person, telephone and emial inquiries. Additionally, they will also suppor the Creative Writing staff and faculty with clerical and administrative funcitons such as date entry, filing and other projects assigned. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Digital-Tools---Student-Assistant_JR109093": {"title": "Digital Tools - Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-20\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Digital Tools Shops (3D Lab, CNC Shops, Laser Lab, D12 Prototyping Shop) within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 3D Lab Checking in and assisting with 3D printing consultations Operating 3D Printers Managing print submission waitlist Laser Lab Check in students for laser cutting consultations and orientations Review files for laser cutting and engraving Troubleshoot problems with student cuts D12 Prototyping All of the above CNC Shops Assisting with CNC consultations and orientations Assisting staff with loading and unloading materials Operations room cleaning Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 \u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends Work Mode : On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17 / per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/German-Language-Tutor_JR109117": {"title": "German Language Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-20\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student\u2019s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student\u2019s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator. \u00a0 Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training.\u00a0 Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. Set up their STARFISH office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application.\u00a0 Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus.\u00a0 WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate :\u00a0U$29.20 Diversity Statement:\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University \u00a0 Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon). We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/ULC-Student-Assistant_JR109121": {"title": "ULC Student Assistant", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-20\nPart time\n\nThe University Learning Center (ULC) is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School\u2019s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Student assistants provide crucial customer service and admin support to ensure students can easily access our services. Responsibilities include , but are not limited to, assisting students, faculty and staff with requests (in-person, via email, online chat), including: Monitor the ULC inbox and respond to student, faculty and staff inquiries including: Scheduling assistance ULC policy clarification Troubleshoot minor tech issues, such as connectivity/audio issues, etc. Direct students to the appropriate New School office(s) Schedule appointments for students, including using Google calendar and ULC online scheduler (WCONLINE) Communicate with ULC staff and tutors via Slack and Gchat, including notifying tutors of timely student issues\u2013lateness, tech problems, etc. and vice-versa Update spreadsheets to track confidential information, including: Student attendance Scheduling data Session summaries 6-8 hours a week, depending on availability Minimum Qualifications: Strong communication, attention to detail, and problem-solving skills Comfortable working in a fast-paced, collaborative environment Friendly demeanor and a positive attitude Experience working with Google Drive, especially Google Sheets (or Excel) Prior office experience preferred Experience working with ESL students a plus Preferred Qualifications: Federal Work Study Preferred Work Mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $17/hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: ULC student assistants help students, faculty and staff to access tutoring services, including answering emails, scheduling appointments, communicating with staff and tutors via Gchat and Slack, and updating spreadsheets to track confidential information. \u200b This role is hybrid, with both online and in-person shifts. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/ULC-Math-Tutor_JR109125": {"title": "ULC Math Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-20\nPart time\n\nThe University Learning Center (ULC) is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School\u2019s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals.\u00a0 Job Responsibilities: Tutor undergraduate and graduate students in a variety of math and economics subjects, including Quantitative Reasoning, Statistics, R, Tableau, SPSS, Economics, Accounting, Excel, Math Merchandising, Math Tools, and Calculus.\u00a0 Work with students of varying skill levels from demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between.\u00a0 Create a professional and welcoming environment by modeling respect for students\u2019 diverse cultures, language skills, and experiences. Complete administrative tasks, such as writing brief descriptions of each session and emailing a brief summary of daily attendance. Other opportunities include leading in-class workshops and/or specialized ULC workshops. Minimum Qualifications: Tutors must be currently enrolled in a graduate program at the university and have at least one year of tutoring experience or teaching math/economics in a formal academic setting.\u00a0 Candidates must have taken Algebra, Pre-Calculus and Calculus, and Statistics. Potential tutors must have earned a grade of B+ or higher in the above mentioned mathematics courses relevant to the subject they will be tutoring.\u00a0 Prior tutoring or teaching experience is preferred. Please send at least one faculty recommendation indicating your proficiency with specific software programs to learningcenter@newschool.edu . Must have a 3.0 GPA or higher Excellent communication skills Demonstrated reliability and punctuality Able to commit to 6 (or more) tutoring hours per week Experience working with students with ADHD a plus Preferred Qualification : Experience working with ESL and graduate-level students strongly preferred Federal Work Study preferred \u200b Work Mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $29.20/hr through August 31 (new pay rate will be determined by next collective bargaining agreement) We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals.\u00a0Tutors work one-on-one with undergraduate and graduate students of varying skill levels on math and economics. Support during sessions can entail demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/U---63-Fifth-Avenue/ULC-Software-Computer-Programming-Tutor_JR109124": {"title": "ULC Software/Computer Programming Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nPart time\n\nThe University Learning Center (ULC) is dedicated to providing academic support to The New School\u2019s diverse student population through one-on-one tutoring sessions, workshops, and more. Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals.\u00a0 Job Responsibilities: Tutor undergraduate and graduate students in various software, including Photoshop, InDesign, Illustrator, Rhino, Figma, Blender, Premiere, After Effects, AutoCAD, Fusion, V-Ray, Revit, SketchUp, etc and/or in various coding languages, such as HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Python, Unity, Arduino and other languages you are fluent in.\u00a0 Work with students of varying skill levels from demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between.\u00a0 Create a professional and welcoming environment by modeling respect for students\u2019 diverse cultures, language skills, and experiences. Complete administrative tasks, such as writing brief descriptions of each session and emailing a brief summary of daily attendance. Other opportunities include leading in-class workshops and/or specialized ULC workshops. Minimum Qualifications: Must have a high level of comfort and expertise in the Adobe CS and other software programs and/or must have strong knowledge of computer programming and a high level of expertise in HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Knowledge of additional programming languages is highly preferred, especially Python.\u00a0 Must be currently enrolled in an undergraduate or graduate program at The New School.\u00a0 Prior tutoring or teaching experience is preferred. Please send at least one faculty recommendation indicating your proficiency with specific software programs to learningcenter@newschool.edu . Must have a 3.0 GPA or higher Excellent communication skills Demonstrated reliability and punctuality Able to commit to 6 (or more) tutoring hours per week Experience working with ESL and graduate-level students strongly preferred Experience working with students with ADHD a plus Federal Work Study preferred Work Mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Pay Rate : $29.20/hr through August 31 (new pay rate will be determined by next collective bargaining agreement) We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors help students become stronger and more independent learners, so that they succeed in achieving their academic goals.\u00a0Tutors work one-on-one with undergraduate and graduate students of varying skill levels on software or computer programming. Support during sessions can entail demonstrating the basics to assisting with complex problems and a range of issues in between. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Event-Assistant---Office-of-Student-Engagement--OSE-_JR109004": {"title": "JR109004 Student Event Assistant - Office of Student Engagement (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director in the Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Event Assistants will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School.\u00a0 This position primarily focuses on the logistical support for OSE related Events/Initiatives as well as other Student-Led Events/Programs with occasional collaboration from Student Activities Board (SAB). The Office of Student Engagement (OSE) at The New School supports the university's educational mission by fostering opportunities for meaningful engagement and connections beyond the classroom. SLI encourages students to actively participate in the university community by joining or starting a student organization, engaging in recreational activities, or attending events and programs organized by the Student Activities Board.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Provide onsite event management support during SLI and student-led events and programs. Participate in recurring student staff meetings and retreats. Assist in the marketing plan, communication,\u00a0 and promotion of Student-Led programs through the Narwhal Nation and across major social media and communications platforms.\u00a0 Maintain, organize, inventory, and catalog office supplies and Storage Spaces of the Office of Student Leadership and Involvement across Fanton Hall and the University Center. The\u00a0 Student Event Assistant will also cross-train and work collaboratively across SLI Student workers and volunteers where needed. Photography: If applicable, provide documentation and photography support at campus events and programs. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This Position requires a Federal Work-Study Award PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Ability to creatively problem solve, multi-task, and function in a collaborative work environment. Capacity to provide and receive constructive critique. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0 #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $17-$19/hr\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 To be considered: Through the career portal, please provide your financial aid award letter, and a resume. You may contact Jose Gomez, Assistant Director, via email at gomezj2@newschool.edu for more information. Responsibilities: The Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Event Assistants will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School. \u00a0This position primarily focuses on the logistical support for OSE related Events/Initiatives as well as other Student-Led Events/Programs with occasional collaboration from Student Activities Board (SAB). \u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Student-Activity-Group-Leader--Office-of-Student-Engagement--OSE-_JR109003": {"title": "JR109003 Student Activity Group Leader- Office of Student Engagement (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Assistant Director in the Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Activity Group Leaders will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School.\u00a0 This position primarily focuses on the Student-Led Events and Programs for the Student Activities Board (SAB) with collaborative support from Student Event Assistants. The Office of Student Engagement (OSE) at The New School supports the university's educational mission by fostering opportunities for meaningful engagement and connections beyond the classroom. OSE encourages students to actively participate in the university community by joining or starting a student organization, engaging in recreational activities, or attending events and programs organized by the Student Activities Board, which is the New School's programming board, responsible for developing and implementing small/large scale events such as open mic nights, silent disco, and mixers to bring the New School's community together.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a student officer on the Student Activities Board (SAB), which hosts student-led events throughout the Academic Year (Fall/Spring)for on and off campus. Reply to student inquiries related to student-led programming. Staff SLI-TE events (when needed), meetings, and office hours for SAB in person. Prepare for events and hold virtual office hours remotely. Photography: If applicable, provide documentation and photography support at campus events and programs. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled New School student who qualifies for Federal Work Study. Demonstrated commitment to community building and teamwork. Reliable, organized, and communicative. Able to manage a hybrid schedule and confirm weekly changes for event staffing. Able to commit to weekly meetings on Thursdays from 4-5 (in person) OR Fridays 4-5 (on zoom) PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience working in an office or community building environment is preferred. WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI Houry Rate: $17-$19/hr\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u200b SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS\u00a0 To be considered: Through the career portal, please provide your financial aid award letter, and a resume. You may contact Jose Gomez, Assistant Director, via email at gomezj2@newschool.edu for more information. Responsibilities: The Office of Student Engagement (OSE), Student Activity Group Leaders will support the goals of the office and the division of Student Success within The New School. \u00a0This position primarily focuses on the Student-Led Events and Programs for the Student Activities Board (SAB) with collaborative support from Student Event Assistants. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Digital-Tools---Student-Assistant-FWS_JR109097": {"title": "Digital Tools - Student Assistant FWS", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in the Digital Tools Shops (3D Lab, CNC Shops, Laser Lab, D12 Prototyping Shop) within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq. ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open workspaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration.\u00a0 The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail-oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0 Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Cultivate a diverse, inclusive, and welcoming learning environment and a respectful shop culture for students, faculty, and staff. Provide a high level of customer service, maintaining a positive and professional demeanor and attitude in all interactions with students, faculty, and staff.\u00a0 Report for shift on time and review priority tasks with the supervisor\u00a0 Monitor patrons entering and exiting the shop space and be ready and available to assist them\u00a0 Provide information to students, faculty, staff, and the general public regarding department services, policies, and procedures Perform basic reception duties including greeting visitors and answering phone calls professionally and courteously\u00a0 Assist students and faculty with reservations and equipment requests\u00a0 Assist Making Center Staff with shop upkeep, organization, and maintenance including:\u00a0 3D Lab Checking in and assisting with 3D printing consultations Operating 3D Printers Managing print submission waitlist Laser Lab Check in students for laser cutting consultations and orientations Review files for laser cutting and engraving Troubleshoot problems with student cuts D12 Prototyping All of the above CNC Shops Assisting with CNC consultations and orientations Assisting staff with loading and unloading materials Operations room cleaning Perform tasks to assist in the maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of various machinery\u00a0 Perform regular upkeep of shops and spaces to maintain organization and cleanliness\u00a0 Complete special projects as assigned\u00a0 Participate in training sessions and staff meetings\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent interpersonal, oral and written communication skills, close attention to detail, and strong organizational and time management abilities. Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes Attention to detail\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds Ability to work without close supervision or alone Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.) Ability to lift items up to 25lbs Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends HOURLY RATE : $17.00/hour WORK MODE: On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment, monitoring making spaces, assisting Managers and Technicians with daily processes and projects, and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Tutor_JR109092": {"title": "Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-14\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphthongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student\u2019s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student\u2019s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator. Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training. Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. Set up their STARFISH office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application. Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus. WORK MODE: Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate : $29.20 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: Tutors are responsible for setting up their office hours- calendar in STARFISH and perform class visits in the language class for no more than fifteen minutes. They need to attend the tutoring appointments and cancel them when a problem/emergency occurs. Cancelation needs to be done forty-eight hours ahead letting the student and department know about it. Tutors need to submit their timesheets for payment every week (Fridays at noon). We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Stitcher_JR109073": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Stitcher", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-10\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Stitcher to join the Performance Operations \u2013 Costume Department Staff. The Costume Department is responsible for the creation and delivery of garments as designated and discussed by the Costume Designer, Design Team, and Production Staff.\u00a0 The Stitcher is a well-organized, self-disciplined individual who takes direction well and is self-sufficient when immediate direction is unavailable. The Stitcher reports to the Costume Shop Manager. The Stitcher coordinates with the design team(s) and shop management to ensure the timely and safe production of any garments requiring construction and/or alterations. Currently, the department covers the productions for the School of Drama, the Mannes Operas, and Concerts and Events for the School of Jazz.\u00a0 This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Costumes department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide support during garment fittings for upcoming productions Attend all shifts in person as scheduled by shop management Hand stitch and machine stitch garments, utilizing various machinery as needed Follow all fitting notes documents, paying close attention to shop management\u2019s instructions\u00a0 Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS 1 or more years of experience in patternmaking 3 years of professional experience in costume construction, stitching, and alterations Knowledge of current techniques, materials, and repertoire relevant for theatrical costuming Ability to follow guidance from leaders throughout the production process PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor of Arts or higher, or equivalent professional experience. Professional experience in one or more of the following areas: tailoring, historical costuming, couture stitching, draping Experience in an academic environment/working with students or young professionals. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Some nights and weekends may be required. Costume Shop hours are 10:00am-5:00pm Monday through Friday. Schedule adjustments may be made due to external venue time constraints when offsite. SALARY RANGE $27.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Language-Tutor_JR109045": {"title": "Language Tutor", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-10\nPart time\n\nThe language tutoring position is responsible to assist other students in one-on-one sessions of thirty minutes out-of-class. Tutors will answer questions about course content taught in class including grammar explanations, pronunciation of vowels sounds, diphtongs and consonant combinations that change according to the language. Tutors can review student\u2019s homework and guide them through it assisting in a couple of sentences, help in student\u2019s presentations preparation and written and oral assignments. Tutors will promote language courses after they accomplish the tutoring administrative, pedagogical and technological training sessions, they need to participate in cultural events organize in class according to their time availability. Tutors report to the faculty tutoring coordinator for pedagogical issues. Pursuant to the CBA, Academic Student Workers (ASWs) shall not be required to perform a service outside of the listing of required duties as noted above. ASWs are encouraged to notify their supervisors and contact person as soon as practicable during the academic term if they anticipate their total work assignment will exceed the terms of the assignment. RESPONSIBILITIES \u25cf Attend the administrative, pedagogical and technological tutoring training sessions for this objective they need to review all digital material sent previously for the training. \u25cf Perform class visits of no more than fifteen minutes in every language class offered and introduce themselves. Explain students how to sign in STARFISH for a tutoring session and the services provided including the extra-credit benefit when performing four tutoring sessions (some language classes require only three sessions, it varies). \u25cf Review, explain and guide students about material taught in class such as grammar structure, pronunciation of vowels, diphthongs and consonant combination sounds. Guide students by reviewing a couple of homework exercises. Tutors can assist students in their written and oral assignments, mini-projects and exam material review. \u25cf Assist students in the oral and written language used for class presentations providing corrections if needed and constructive feedback. \u25cf Facilitate interactive speaking and listening exercises to build a real-world conversation skill. \u25cf Contact language professors to participate in cultural class events allowing students to integrate in foreign language culture customs and social conventions. \u25cf Set up their STARFISH office hours according to the university academic calendar and remove it during holidays. Tutoring is only offer at the university. \u25cf Submit their online timesheets for payment through My-Day application. \u25cf Communicate immediately when a tutoring session has been cancelled. Contact the student and the program coordinator to inform about it. \u25cf Inform the tutoring coordinator if students are not at tending sessions. Tutoring is a free service and if a student does not attend the session their tutoring privilege can be removed. \u25cf Register all sessions performed in the tutoring excel track sheet provided every semester, indicating the day and time the session was performed and the student name too. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS \u25cf The tutoring job position is opened to students that are currently enrolled at the New School University only. \u25cf Native or near native fluency in the target language is desired, alongside strong English communication skills to clearly explain complex language rules. \u25cf Experience as a prior tutor in high school or other institutions in the country or abroad is a plus. \u25cf Strong and interpersonal skills such as patience, empathy, respect and tolerance to students diverse opinions. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Applicants with advanced knowledge of literature, grammar, writing and speaking in the target language is a plus. WORK MODE Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on- campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus, when necessary, in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $29.20 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. This position is covered by a collective bargaining agreement between SENS-UAW, Local 7902 and the University. Responsibilities: The Language Tutor provides one-on-one tutoring to help students strengthen their language skills, including grammar, pronunciation, homework, presentations, and written and oral assignments. Tutors complete required training, promote tutoring services, maintain accurate records, and support cultural engagement activities. They report to the faculty tutoring coordinator and must be currently enrolled students with strong language proficiency and communication skills. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Staff-Pianist--Senior-Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR109070": {"title": "Staff Pianist, Senior Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-10\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Mannes School of Music at the College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Staff Pianist, Senior Student Assistant Specialist. We are looking for organized, responsible, and collaborative individuals to support instruction and performances at the Mannes School of Music. This position reports to the Performance and Ensemble Manager at the Mannes School of Music. This is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career as a pianist. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Accompany instrumental and/or vocal students in private lessons, masterclasses, juries, classes, and/or other performance experiences. Learn assigned music prior to collaborating with your fellow students (e.g., correct tempi, dynamics, etc.). Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with students, faculty, staff, and audience members. Respond to inquiries from students, faculty, and staff in a timely manner regarding private lessons, masterclasses, and/or other performance experiences. Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items. QUALIFICATIONS Current student in the Mannes BM, MM, or PDPL degree programs. Prior coursework in collaborative skills, and/or equivalent experience. Extensive knowledge of collaborative piano performance practice with art songs, lieder, sonatas, concerti, and/or other vocal and instrumental solo repertoire WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $25.00 Hours per Week: Approximately 10 hours per week, or as needed. Not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit both a resume and cover letter outlining their interest, skills, and experience pertaining to this position. The Performance and Ensemble Manager will be in touch in order to schedule an audition with you. Responsibilities: Provide collaborative piano services for instrumental and vocal students in lessons, masterclasses, juries, classes, and other performance activities while preparing assigned repertoire to support successful performances. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Production-A1_JR109048": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Production A1", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Production Department at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Production A1 to join its team. Reporting to the Audio & Video Supervisor, the Overhire Production A1 is directly responsible for supporting the audio and video needs of academic related performances and partner events across the College of Performing Arts. The Overhire Production A1 is responsible for the overall quality of the live sound of concerts, panel discussions, festivals, and other special events at the College of Performing Arts. The A1 may also be responsible for the integrity of the livestream mix of a streamed performance. The Overhire Production A1 is also responsible for leading the audio crew when the Audio & Video Supervisor or Assistant Audio & Video Supervisor are deployed elsewhere. The Overhire Production A1 reports to the Audio & Video Supervisor. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Audio & Video department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Mix FOH, Monitors or Streaming Audio Feeds, occasionally at the same time on the same console Provide professional, courteous service to all parties Responsible for setup, operation, and strike of equipment before, during, and after events, including operating all audio consoles, playback support systems, and any audio related gear as required Direct any audio team members under their supervision in the completion of any required tasks, providing guidance when necessary to deliver the University\u2019s standard. Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS At least 4 years of experience as a live sound engineer for concerts, corporate events or theatrical productions Live-mixing skills and working knowledge of digital consoles, specifically, Yamaha, Allen & Heath, Midas, and Behringer Excellent knowledge of proper gain structure and signal flow Working knowledge of audio related software, such as Qlab 4&5, Wireless Workbench 7, Pro Tools, Logic. Dante Virtual Soundcard, Dante Controller, etc. Ability to interpret event technical documents including technical riders, signal flow diagrams, input lists, stage plots, run-of-show, and cue lists. Strong verbal and written communication, organization, and time-management skills Ability to communicate with other departments and event coordinator to ensure a smooth show. Punctuality, professionalism, and a positive collaborative attitude when working with Students, Faculty, Staff, and Team Members.\u00a0 Experienced or able to learn and develop fluency in The New School\u2019s suite of applications including Google Suite, Microsoft Office, and WorkDay. Ability to adhere to university COVID-19 policy and requirements Demonstrated commitment to Equity, Inclusion, Social Justice, and the transformative power of the arts and education PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Corporate, Special Events, Festival Sound, Line by Line Musical Mixing, and Broadcast experience are all plusses\u00a0 Self-motivated with ability to work with minimal supervision Ability to safely climb ladders and scaffolding, operate Genies, and comfortable working at heights Able to lift up to 50lbs WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE $35.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Production-A2_JR109049": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Production A2", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Production Department at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Production A2 to join its team. Reporting to the Audio & Video Supervisor, the Overhire Production A2 is directly responsible for supporting the audio and video needs of academic related performances and partner events across the College of Performing Arts. The Overhire Production A2 is responsible for running audio related crew on the stage and facilitating stage moves. The Overhire Production A2 is also responsible for coordination of RF mics. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Audio & Video department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Understand and execute the technical aspects of each design, including interpreting audio needs and all signal flow plans, I/O lists, and stage plots Setup, place, patch, and test all microphones with the A1 and any additional audio labor. Mix Monitors for events that require it Coordinate RF Mics for events that require it.\u00a0 Execute any stage moves required by the show Provide professional, courteous service to all parties Fulfills the role of System Tech or Console Operator, or both when required, depending on the size of the event Responsible for setup, operation, and strike of equipment before, during, and after events, including audio consoles, playback support systems, and any audio related gear as required Direct any technicians under their supervision in the completion of any required tasks, providing guidance when necessary to deliver the University\u2019s standard Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS At least 2 years of experience as a live sound engineer for concerts, corporate events or theater Excellent knowledge of proper gain structure and signal flow Working knowledge of audio related software, such as\u00a0 Wireless Workbench 6, Pro Tools, Logic. Dante Virtual Soundcard, Dante Controller, etc. Ability to interpret event technical documents including technical riders, signal flow diagrams, input lists, stage plots, run-of-show, and cue lists. Strong verbal and written communication, organization, and time-management skills Ability to communicate with Audio & Video Supervisor, Projections Designer, other departments, and Production Management to ensure a smooth show. Punctuality, professionalism, and a positive collaborative attitude when working with Students, Faculty, Staff, and Team Members.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Corporate, Special Events, Festival Sound, and Broadcast experience are all plusses\u00a0 Self-motivated with ability to work with minimal supervision Able to lift up to 50lbs Ability to safely climb ladders and scaffolding, operate Genies, and comfortable working at heights Monitor mixing skills and working knowledge of digital consoles, specifically, Yamaha, Allen & Heath, Midas, and Behringer WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE $30.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Video-Operator_JR109054": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Video Operator", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW \u00a0 The Production Department at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Video Operator to join its team. Reporting to the Audio & Video Supervisor, the Overhire Video Operator is directly responsible for supporting the audio and video needs of academic related performances and partner events across the College of Performing Arts. \u00a0 The Overhire Video Operator is responsible for the overall quality and operation of the College of Performing Arts\u2019 livestreams and video recordings. The Overhire Video Operator is also responsible for all associated infrastructure such as livestreaming computers, video switchers, and wireless video systems, as well as the operation and placement of cameras for livestreamed productions. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Audio & Video department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Understand and execute the technical aspects of each design, including interpreting video needs and all signal flow plans, I/O lists, and stage plots Determine best locations for cameras depending on stage layout to create an engaging and dynamic experience for our audience members Design Direct, and switch livestream presentations using Vmix and Blackmagic Atem Video Switchers Set Exposure and match color of each camera to create a unified look for multicam shoots Manage recorded media for pre recorded events Provide professional, courteous service to all parties Fulfills the role of Livestream System Tech Setup, operate, and strike equipment before, during, and after events. Direct any technicians under their supervision in the completion of any required tasks, providing guidance when necessary to deliver the University\u2019s standard. Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS At least 3 years of experience as a livestream engineer and/or 2 years of experience as a camera operator Excellent knowledge of Sony and Blackmagic Cameras as well as PTZ cameras and their operation.\u00a0 An artistic eye, Live-switching, skills and working knowledge of livestream hardware and software such as Vmix and ATEM Switchers. Understanding of setting exposure and white balance for stage lighting Familiar with CineEyes family of wireless video solutions.\u00a0 Excellent knowledge and troubleshooting of video signal flow from the camera to the stream destination. Ability to interpret event technical documents including signal flow diagrams. stage plots, run-of-show, and cue lists. Strong verbal and written communication, organization, and time-management skills Ability to communicate with other departments and event coordinators to ensure a smooth show. Punctuality, professionalism, and a positive collaborative attitude when working with Students, Faculty, Staff, and Team Members.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Television or special events experience a plus An eye for creating dynamic shots Self-motivated with ability to work with minimal supervision Able to lift up to 50lbs WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE $35.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Production-A-V-Stagehand_JR109053": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Production A/V Stagehand", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Production Department at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Production A/V Stagehand to join its team. Reporting to the Audio & Video Supervisor, the Overhire Production A/V Stagehand is directly responsible for supporting the audio and video needs of academic related performances and partner events across the College of Performing Arts. The Overhire Production A/V Stagehand is responsible for any audio or video related tasks assigned by the Audio & Video Supervisor or their delegates including but not limited to stage moves, install of audio and video equipment in theaters, and maintenance of gear and spaces. On certain events or calls, they may report to the Assistant Audio & Video Supervisor or individual departmental leads. This is an overhire position, with opportunities for work to be scheduled as needed by the Audio/Video Department. The position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Understand and execute the technical aspects of each design, including interpreting audio & video needs and all signal flow plans, I/O lists, and stage plots, or ground plans\u00a0 Safely and securely run signal and power cables, implement sound designer ground plans in theatrical load ins.\u00a0 Setup, place, patch, and test all microphones and speakers in conjunction with the A1 and A2. Assist in executing any stage moves required by the show Assist in maintenance of spaces and equipment as directed by the Audio & Video Supervisor Provide professional, courteous service to all parties Set up, operate, and strike equipment before, during, and after events, including audio consoles, playback support systems, and any audio related gear as required Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS At least 1 year of experience as a stage technician, for concerts, corporate events, or theater. Excellent knowledge of proper gain structure and signal flow Working knowledge of audio related software, such as Wireless Workbench 6, Pro Tools, Logic, Dante Virtual Soundcard, Dante Controller, etc. Ability to interpret event technical documents including technical riders, signal flow diagrams, input lists, stage plots, run-of-show, and cue lists. Strong verbal and written communication, organization, and time-management skills Ability to communicate with other departments and event coordinator, ensuring the event\u2019s success. Punctuality, professionalism, and a positive collaborative attitude when working with students, faculty, staff, and team members.\u00a0 Experienced or able to learn and develop fluency in The New School\u2019s suite of applications including Google Suite and WorkDay. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \u00a0 Corporate, Festival Sound, Broadcast and Special Events experience are all plusses\u00a0 Working knowledge of digital consoles, specifically, Yamaha, Allen & Heath, Midas, and Behringer Self-motivated with ability to work with minimal supervision Ability to safely climb ladders and scaffolding, operate Genies, and comfortable working at heights Able to lift up to 50lbs WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE $27.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Board-Operator_JR109052": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Board Operator", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\nPart time\n\nINTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Production Department at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Board Operator to join its team. Reporting to the Audio & Video Supervisor, the Overhire Board Operator is directly responsible for supporting the audio and video needs of academic related performances and partner events across the College of Performing Arts. The Overhire Board Operator is responsible for projection, video, and audio systems during theatrical productions where live mixing is not required. The Overhire Board Operator takes cues from stage management and implements the designs of the Sound Designer and/or the Projection Designer when required. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Audio & Video department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Take cues from stage management during tech and performances of drama shows, including through Qlab Assist in programming of video content when necessary using Qlab, Watchout, Millumin, Isadora, or other software to be disclosed ahead once confirmed with the designer Understand and execute the technical aspects of each design, including interpreting audio and video needs and all signal flow plans, I/O lists, and ground plans Setup, place, patch, and test all microphones and speakers according to the sound designer\u2019s specifications Provide professional, courteous service to all parties Fulfill the role of System Tech and Console Operator Setup, operate, and strike equipment before, during, and after events; including projectors, cameras, audio consoles, playback support systems, and any other audio or video related gear as required Direct any technicians, Assistant Stage Managers, or other stagecrew members under their supervision in the completion of any required tasks, providing guidance when necessary to deliver the University\u2019s standard Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Working knowledge of Qlab 5, Isadora, Millumin, Watchout, or other projection software. At least 2 years of experience as a live sound engineer for concerts, corporate events or theater Excellent knowledge of proper gain structure and video signal flow in theatrical systems Working knowledge of audio related software, such as Wireless Workbench 6, Dante Virtual Soundcard, Dante Controller, etc. Knowledge of projection mapping Ability to interpret event technical documents including technical riders, signal flow diagrams, input lists, ground plans, run-of-show, and cue lists. Strong verbal and written communication, organization, and time-management skills Ability to communicate with departmental leadership and designers to ensure successful productions Punctuality, professionalism, and a positive collaborative attitude when working with students, faculty, and staff PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \u00a0 Corporate, Special Events, Festival Sound, and Broadcast experience are all plusses\u00a0 Self-motivated with ability to work with minimal supervision Able to lift up to 50lbs Ability to safely climb ladders and scaffolding, operate Genies, and comfortable working at heights Working knowledge of digital audio consoles, specifically, Yamaha, Allen & Heath, Midas, and Behringer WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE $30.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Hair-and-Makeup-Supervisor_JR109056": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Hair and Makeup Supervisor", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\nPart time\n\nJoin The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Hair and Makeup Supervisor to join its Performance Operations department. Reporting to the Costume Shop Manager, the Overhire Hair and Makeup Supervisor is directly responsible for working with designers, actors, and crew members to provide exceptional hair and makeup services for the current production.\u00a0 \u00a0 The Overhire Hair and Makeup Supervisor has a passion for beauty, a creative eye, and the ability to work efficiently under pressure. This position supports the production process ensuring clients have accurate and consistent hair and makeup as created by the appropriate designers.\u00a0 The Overhire Hair and Makeup Supervisor is a creative artisan who thrives in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly team-oriented environment. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Costume department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. \u00a0 CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide hair and makeup services for actors as needed, including but not limited to applying makeup, styling hair, and installing hair extensions and/or wigs Consult with actors to determine their hair and makeup needs, potential allergies, and their knowledge of hair and makeup practices. Communicate effectively with actors, designers, and Costume Shop Manager to ensure their satisfaction with the services provided. Assist in maintaining the Costume Shop\u2019s inventory of Hair and Makeup products and tools. Participate in production strike as outlined by the Wardrobe Supervisor and Costume Shop management, including cleaning makeup brushes, washing wigs, disposing of unhygienic products, and restoring inventory. Pay close attention to actors\u2019 needs, especially any reported discomfort or adverse reactions to their hair or makeup, and indicate any incidents to stage management and the Costume Shop Manager.\u00a0 Follow all notes documents, paying close attention to shop management\u2019s instructions. Additional responsibilities as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience as an Hair and Makeup Artisan and/or Supervisor Attention to detail Ability to work in a fast-paced environment Knowledge of hair, makeup, and beauty products, trends, and techniques Excellent communication and customer service skills PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor of Arts or equivalent professional experience 2+ years prior experience as an Hair and Makeup Artist Experience in an academic environment/working with students or young professionals WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE $30.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/N---66-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Assistant---Making-Center_JR108985": {"title": "Student Assistant - Making Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-07\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in various shops within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open work spaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0Duties for each space vary slightly, but entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITES Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills.\u00a0 Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users.\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail.\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Physical ability to lift items.\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 WORK MODE On-campus position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work that is not feasible, or not in the organization's best interest, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Duties for each space entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment. The student assistant is also responsible for monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/N---66-Fifth-Avenue/Student-Assistant----Making--Center_JR108984": {"title": "Student Assistant -  Making  Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-06\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in various shops within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open work spaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed.\u00a0Duties for each space vary slightly, but entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills.\u00a0 Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users.\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail.\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Physical ability to lift items.\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 WORK MODE On-campus position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work that is not feasible, or not in the organization's best interest, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17.00 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. https://makingcenter.parsons.edu/labs/general-shop/ Responsibilities: Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant---Making-Center_JR109033": {"title": "Student Assistant - Making Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-06\nPart time\n\nParsons School for Design, a division of The New School, seeks Student Assistants in various shops within the Making Center. Parsons Making Center is a collection of more than 70,000 sq.ft. of labs, shops, studios, and open work spaces that provide curricular support for making across a broad range of mediums and disciplines, and supports experimentation and interdisciplinary collaboration. The student assistant should be a personable, proactive, detail oriented individual who will become integral to the daily workings of the facilities within the Making Center, with the ability to learn new skills and technologies as needed. Duties for each space vary slightly, but entail assisting students and faculty with the checkout of tools/equipment and/or monitoring making spaces and keeping the facilities clean and orderly.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Excellent customer service skills.\u00a0 Able to understand and follow verbal and written instructions from supervisor and communicate clearly with Making Center users.\u00a0 Adaptable and consistent when notifying the supervisor of schedule changes. Attention to detail.\u00a0 Ability to interact with students, faculty, and staff of diverse backgrounds. Ability to work without close supervision or alone. Familiarity with Google Suite (Calendar, Gmail, Docs, Sheets, etc.). Physical ability to lift items.\u00a0 Ability to work non-traditional hours such as evenings and weekends. Ability to handle objects weighing up to 25 lbs and on occasion may be expected to lift objects weighing up to 50 lbs.\u00a0 WORK MODE On-campus position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work that is not feasible, or not in the organization's best interest, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE \u00a0: $17.00 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Report for shift on time and primarily assist Making Center Staff with reviewing priority of tasks in the shop including: upkeep, organization, and maintenance. Work in WebCheckout providing students appropriate tools for their projects as well as process materials for the store. Proper opening and closing of the shops, as assigned by Making Center Staff, including opening the space and unlocking resources and turning on / logging into computers. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Commuter-Engagement-Group-Leader--FWS-_JR109002": {"title": "Commuter Engagement Group Leader (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-06\nPart time\n\nThe Office of Student Engagement oversees community building programs, and seeks a student worker to support programming for commuter students. This position reports to the Associate Director for Recreation & Commuter Engagement. The New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education.\u00a0 Reporting to the Associate Director of Recreation & Commuter Engagement, the Commuter Engagement Group Leader will serve as the student officer on the Commuter Engagement Board which hosts weekly meetings and monthly events both on and off campus. RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a student officer on the Commuter Engagement Board, which hosts weekly meetings and monthly events both on and off campus.\u00a0 Create content for the Commuter Group\u2019s NarwhalNation and Instagram pages.\u00a0 Reply to student inquiries related to commuter discounts, events, and services. Staff events, meetings, and office hours in person.\u00a0 Prepare for events and hold virtual office hours remotely MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled New School student who qualifies for Federal Work Study Demonstrated commitment to community building and teamwork Reliable, organized, and communicative Able to manage a hybrid schedule and confirm weekly changes for event staffing\u00a0 Able to commit to weekly meetings on Thursdays from 4-5 (in person) OR Fridays 4-5 (on zoom) Must be able to work at least one weekend or evening event per month\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience working in an office or community building environment is preferred.\u00a0 WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0 #LI-DNI SALARY RANGE $18-20 / hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u200b SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Questions can be emailed to recreation@newschool.edu . Responsibilities: The Commuter Engagement Group Leader will serve as the student officer on the Commuter Engagement Board which hosts weekly meetings and monthly events both on and off campus. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Athletics-Coordinator--FWS-_JR109032": {"title": "Athletics Coordinator (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-06\nPart time\n\nThe Office of Student Engagement oversees community building programs, and seeks a student worker to support programming for commuter students. This position reports to the Associate Director for Recreation & Commuter Engagement. The New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education.\u00a0 Reporting to the Associate Director of Recreation & Commuter Engagement, the Athletics Coordinator will assist in keeping the athletic space and equipment usable, reservations, communications, and provide game day support for teams. RESPONSIBILITIES Support athletic spaces and equipment management to ensure space is tidy and equipment is usable.\u00a0 Answer and monitor the recreation@newschool.edu email account Maintain space reservation policies in the fitness room. Assist in scheduling athletic games, including club sports and intramurals Provide game day support for teams. Provide social media and email newsletter support for athletics events Coordinate appearances with the Gnarls Squad. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled New School student who qualifies for Federal Work Study Demonstrated commitment to community building and teamwork Demonstrated interest in Sports Reliable, organized, and communicative Able to manage a hybrid schedule and confirm weekly changes for event staffin g\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience as a sports team Captain or in an athletics department is preferred CPR and First Aid certification preferred WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $20 / hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Questions can be emailed to recreation@newschool.edu . Responsibilities: The Athletics Coordinator will assist in keeping the athletic space and equipment usable, reservations, communications, and provide game day support for teams. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Gnarls-Communications-Coordinator--FWS-_JR109001": {"title": "Gnarls Communications Coordinator (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-06\nPart time\n\nThe Office of Student Engagement oversees community building programs, and seeks a student worker to support programming for commuter students. This position reports to the Associate Director for Recreation & Commuter Engagement. The New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education.\u00a0 Reporting to the Associate Director of Recreation & Commuter Engagement, the Gnarls Communication Coordinator will provide a key role in documenting, promoting, and creating content on social media platforms for events hosted by Recreation and Student Affairs as well as events throughout the University that Gnarls attends. RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a student officer on the Gnarls Squad Team, attending various on and off campus events hosted through Recreation and Student Affairs Create content for events Gnarls attends on NarwhalNation and Instagram.\u00a0 Serve as Gnarls photographer for Gnarls attending events.\u00a0 Reply to student inquiries related to Gnarls appearances/booking. Staff events, meetings, and office hours in person.\u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled New School student who qualifies for Federal Work Study Demonstrated commitment to community building and teamwork Reliable, organized, and communicative Able to manage a hybrid schedule and confirm weekly changes for event staffing\u00a0 Must be able to work at least one weekend or evening event per month\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience with Photography is preferred. Prior experience with Social Media is preferred. Prior experience working in an office or community building environment is preferred.\u00a0 WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI SALARY RANGE $18 - $20 / hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Questions can be emailed to recreation@newschool.edu Responsibilities: The Gnarls Communication Coordinator will provide a key role in documenting, promoting, and creating content on social media platforms for events hosted by Recreation and Student Affairs as well as events throughout the University that Gnarls attends. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Graphic-Designer-For-Recreation---Commuter-Engagement--FWS-_JR108997": {"title": "Graphic Designer For Recreation & Commuter Engagement (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-06\nPart time\n\nThe Office of Student Engagement oversees community building programs, and seeks a student worker to support programming for commuter students. This position reports to the Associate Director for Recreation & Commuter Engagement. The New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education.\u00a0 Reporting to the Associate Director of Recreation, & Commuter Engagement, the Graphic Designer for Recreation and Commuter Engagement will play a key role in designing, marketing, and promoting department initiatives and programs.\u00a0 RESPONSIBILITIES Craft visual and written content for OSE digital and physical platforms to promote Recreation and Commuter Engagement initiatives, including any event Gnarls attends.\u00a0 Assist in the marketing plan and promotion of OSE programs, including review and maintenance of websites and social media accounts. (Examples include but aren\u2019t limited to: Narwhal Nation, Instagram, Twitter, WhatsAPP) Create, organize, and coordinate re-usable assets utilized for specific events, programs, and series put on by OSE and Recreation & Commuter Engagement (templates, guidelines, standards, logos) Collaborate and coordinate with students and staff to work collaboratively on short-term and long-term projects that require graphic design. Maintain a confidential work environment. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS This Position requires a Federal Work-Study Award Proficiency in Adobe Creative Suite, Canva, Google Suite, and Microsoft Office applications. Can work with The New School\u2019s branding and style guides to create a suite of coherent collateral across Student Success PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated commitment to inclusivity and social justice. An understanding of intersectionality and universally accessible design.\u00a0 Ability to creatively problem solve, multi-task, and function in a collaborative work environment. Capacity to provide and receive constructive critique. Understanding and interest in contemporary and emerging digital trends WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0 #LI-DNI Hourly Rate: $18-$20 / hr We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Questions can be emailed to recreation@newschool.edu Responsibilities: The Graphic Designer for Recreation and Commuter Engagement will play a key role in designing, marketing, and promoting department initiatives and programs.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Fitness-Instructor--FWS-_JR108999": {"title": "Fitness Instructor (FWS)", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-06\nPart time\n\nThe Office of Student Engagement oversees community building programs, and seeks a student worker to support programming for commuter students. This position reports to the Associate Director for Recreation & Commuter Engagement. The New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education.\u00a0 Reporting to the Associate Director of Recreation, & Commuter Engagement, the Fitness Instructor will be responsible for leading fitness classes, creating content for social media accounts, and acting as the point-person for inquiries and class sign ups. RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a student fitness instructor during a TNS fitness class.\u00a0 Create content for the Commuter Group\u2019s NarwhalNation and Instagram pages.\u00a0 Reply to student inquiries related to the fitness class in question. Lead events in question with a fitness course/lesson plan.\u00a0 Support students in signing up for the class. Take class attendance per session. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Currently enrolled New School student who qualifies for Federal Work Study Demonstrated commitment to community building and teamwork Reliable, organized, and communicative Past fitness experience Ability to lead fitness sessions Able to manage a hybrid schedule with majority in-person hours PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience/certification working as a fitness instructor is preferred.\u00a0 WORK MODE Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled , consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI SALARY RANGE $19 - $21 / hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) Questions can be emailed to recreation@newschool.edu but applications must be submitted through the New School WorkDay Jobs Portal for an interview to be scheduled.\u00a0 Responsibilities: The Fitness Instructor will be responsible for leading fitness classes, creating content for social media accounts, and acting as the point-person for inquiries and class sign ups. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Part-Time-Faculty---Parsons-School-of-Design_JR109030": {"title": "Part-Time Faculty | Parsons School of Design", "description": "Posted: 2026-07-02\nPart time\n\nThe New School , a university known for its progressive approach to education, comprises seven outstanding Divisions bound by a common purpose: To educate and inspire undergraduate, graduate, and adult learners to make significant contributions to their own fields and to the world at large. Located in historic Greenwich Village, the New School launches the careers of world citizens whose ideas, innovations, and accomplishments forge new paths in design, humanities, public policy, the social sciences, and the performing arts. RESPONSIBILITIES Parsons School of Design \u00a0at The New School is accepting applications for possible Part-Time Faculty positions in all five design schools for upcoming semesters. Class times may be evenings and/or weekends. Duties may include the following: Teaching graduate and/or undergraduate students within a defined field of expertise; Developing and managing course syllabi and ensuring that syllabi meet Program and School standards; Planning and creating lectures, discussions, and course assignments; Assessing student learning outcomes and aiding student success; Submitting grades in a timely manner. Part-time faculty at The New School bargain collectively through Academics Come Together,\u00a0ACT-UAW, Local 7902. QUALIFICATIONS Vary by disciplinary area, but generally require: A terminal degree in the field of specialty is preferred (MFA, PH.D,\u00a0ABD, or equivalent); 2-3 years of teaching experience with a variety of teaching methods and curricular perspectives, preferably in a College, University, and/or Art and Design school settings; Ability to work with diverse students and colleagues; Proven success creating inclusive classrooms; Ability to adhere to University\u00a0COVID-19 Policy and requirements. HOURLY RATE Studio Hourly Rate : $135/contact hr Seminar Hourly Rate : $156/contact hr WORK MODE On-Campus/Online #LI-DNI SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Please prepare a Resume/CV and Cover Letter outlining your previous relevant experience. Please include any relevant portfolio materials and/or a link to your professional website if applicable. The New School is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application! We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/HML-Part-Time-Program-Administrator_JR108963": {"title": "HML Part Time Program Administrator", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-18\nPart time\n\nParsons the New School for Design seeks a responsible, detail-oriented Part-Time Program Administrator to provide administrative support to Healthy Materials Lab Directors and staff on grant-related activities that are 100% effort (21hrs/week). This role is supported by funds from Friends of Healthy Materials Initiative. This position provides a flexible work schedule with a high expectation of reliability. Must be available to work at least three days a week. This is a fixed term, part-time position for 21 hours a week and will be benefit-eligible. HML Related Activities ( 100% effort) \u00a0 Work in the Lab is fast-paced and complex and applicants should be able and willing to multi-task. The candidate must possess strong organizational and communication skills including excellent writing skills. They must be punctual, logical, and self-sufficient and possess a high level of technical computer literacy. This position provides a flexible work schedule with a high expectation of reliability. This is a union administrative position governed by a collective bargaining agreement between Local 1205 Teamsters and the University. The New School is strongly committed to diversity and inclusion in the workplace and particularly seeks applications from members of underrepresented groups, as well as candidates who share this commitment. \u00a0 Responsibilities: Oversight and coordination of public programs, private events, and internal/external\u00a0meetings. Includes adjusting work schedule on days with evening events. Schedule HML communications, media promotion, and public relations, including mailing list maintenance and upkeep. Undertake writing projects to disseminate the work of the Lab. Assist in updating students through Canvas and/or distributing a range of digital communications for HML\u2019s eLearning programs. Administration of academic partnerships including project management. Creation, implementation, and management of program-level communications and calendar. Oversee expense tracking and reconciliation, and coordinate with relevant university offices as needed. Qualifications :\u00a0 Bachelor\u2019s Degree with equivalent experience required. Exceptionally professional and proactive with an interest in Material Health. Highly organized and detail-oriented. Demonstrated ability to prioritize, problem-solve, manage diverse activities, and meet deadlines. Exceptional communication, interpersonal, and collaboration skills. Pleasant demeanor and positive, can-do attitude. Work mode: This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. #LI-Onsite Hourly Range: $32.98/hr - $36.42 \u00a0 \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/FWS-Student-Assistant---Financial-Aid_JR108971": {"title": "FWS Student Assistant - Financial Aid", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-18\nPart time\n\nReporting to the Associate Director of Financial Aid, the Federal Work-Study Student Assistants will work collaboratively with other colleagues within Enrollment Management to guide students and their families through the financial aid application process and their status regarding financial aid. The Financial Aid Student Assistant will act as a first responder for the financial aid office, providing welcoming, courteous, prompt, and accurate information, as well as providing support with data collection, research, and reporting. Students in this position must have received a Federal Work Study award. Responsibilities: \u25cf Respond to phone calls & emails from prospective/returning students and their families, providing friendly, accurate, and timely information about their financial aid \u25cf Develop knowledge of the different colleges within the new school, degree programs, and majors \u25cf Assist students with the application process, providing information on application deadlines and requirements \u25cfEscalate calls and email inquiries appropriately \u25cfProvide prospective/returning students and families with Financial Aid information \u25cfAssist newly/returning admitted students and appropriately triage inquiries within the FA department and to other university departments (admissions, student accounts, registrar, open campus, housing) \u25cfProvide research support on various operations projects \u25cfLight filing, distribution of emails, and forms to financial aid representatives \u25cfAdditional projects and ad hoc reports as needed \u25cfAssist in managing our Virtual Zoom Meeting Room and in-person traffic \u00a0 Qualifications: \u25cf Experience working in a professional office setting \u25cf Customer service and visitor experience are a must \u25cf Interest in higher education and office experience a plus \u25cf Friendly and kind demeanor, attention to detail \u25cf Excellent verbal and written communication skills \u25cf Strong understanding of The New School\u2019s programs \u25cf Familiarity with campus (i.e., offices, computer centers, location of departments, etc.). \u25cf Ability to manage in a diverse population \u25cf Proficiency in Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, and Excel \u25cf An ability to perform multiple tasks \u25cf Must be able to work a minimum of 10-15 hours per week on campus \u25cf This is a Federal Work-Study position. Only apply if you are eligible for Federal Work-Study. Hourly Rate:\u00a0 $17.00-$19.00 Work Mode:\u00a0 Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus and also work some of the time remotely.\u00a0#LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Please Note: This is a Federal Work Study Position for students who were awarded a Federal Work Study (FWS) award, please upload a copy of your FWS Award letter in addition to your other application documents to be considered for this role. Responsibilities: This role assists prospective and returning students with financial aid, application questions, and university resources through phone, email, Zoom, and in-person support. Responsibilities include providing accurate information, directing inquiries to the appropriate departments, and supporting student services. The position also involves administrative tasks, research projects, and other operational support as needed We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Staff-Pianist_JR108967": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Staff Pianist", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-18\nPart time\n\nINTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Mannes School of Music at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Temporary Overhire Staff Pianists to join its team. Reporting to the Performance and Ensemble Manager, the Overhire Staff Pianists are directly responsible for staff piano services throughout the Mannes School of Music, including performing in students\u2019 lessons, masterclasses, juries, and more. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Mannes School of Music department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Accompany all student performance opportunities, including lessons, masterclasses, classroom activities, annual concerto competition, juries, and more Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with students, faculty, staff, and audience members Respond to inquiries from students, faculty, and staff in a timely manner regarding private lessons, masterclasses, and/or other performance experiences Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items Additional responsibilities as requested QUALIFICATIONS Master\u2019s Degree and/or equivalent training in collaborative piano Excellent collaborative piano skills and artistic capabilities Ability to prepare music quickly on an as-needed basis Noted recognition in the collaborative piano field WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Hourly Rate: $25.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. The Performance and Ensemble Manager will be in touch in order to schedule an audition with you. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Part-Time-Faculty_JR108930": {"title": "Part-Time Faculty", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-08\nPart time\n\nThe New School , a university known for its progressive approach to education, its divisions bound by a common purpose: To educate and inspire undergraduate, graduate, and adult learners to make significant contributions to their own fields and to the world at large. Located in historic Greenwich Village, the New School launches the careers of world citizens whose ideas, innovations, and accomplishments forge new paths in design, humanities, public policy, the social sciences, and the performing arts. Eugene Lang College of Liberal Arts at The New School is accepting applications for possible Part-Time Faculty positions. Class times may be evenings and/or weekends. Duties may include the following: Teaching undergraduate students within a defined field of expertise; Developing and managing course syllabi and ensuring that syllabi meet Program and School standards; Planning and creating lectures, discussions, and course assignments; Assessing student learning outcomes and aiding student success; Submitting grades in a timely manner. \u00a0 QUALIFICATIONS VARY BY DEPARTMENT A terminal degree or equivalent experience in the field of specialty is preferred. 2-3 years of teaching experience with a variety of teaching methods and curricular perspectives, preferably in a College, University, or school setting. Ability to work with diverse students and colleagues; Proven success creating inclusive classrooms; Compensation Studio Hourly Rate: $ 135/contact hr Seminar Hourly Rare: $156/contact hr Work Mode : On Site #LI-DNI Please prepare a Resume/CV and Cover Letter outlining your previous relevant experience. Please include any relevant portfolio materials and/or a link to your professional website if applicable. Part-time faculty at The New School bargain collectively through Academics Come Together, ACT-UAW, Local 7902. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/D---6-East-16th-Street/Part-Time-Faculty_JR108929": {"title": "Part-Time Faculty", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-04\nPart time\n\nThe New School , a university known for its progressive approach to education, its divisions bound by a common purpose: To educate and inspire undergraduate, graduate, and adult learners to make significant contributions to their own fields and to the world at large. Located in historic Greenwich Village, the New School launches the careers of world citizens whose ideas, innovations, and accomplishments forge new paths in design, humanities, public policy, the social sciences, and the performing arts. The New School for Social Research \u00a0at The New School is accepting applications for possible Part-Time Faculty positions. Class times may be evenings and/or weekends. Duties may include the following: Teaching graduate\u00a0 students within a defined field of expertise; Developing and managing course syllabi and ensuring that syllabi meet Program and School standards; Planning and creating lectures, discussions, and course assignments; Assessing student learning outcomes and aiding student success; Submitting grades in a timely manner Part-time faculty at The New School bargain collectively through Academics Come Together, ACT-UAW, Local 7902. QUALIFICATIONS VARY BY DEPARTMENT A terminal degree or equivalent experience in the field of specialty is preferred. 2-3 years of teaching experience with a variety of teaching methods and curricular perspectives, preferably in a College, University, or school setting. Ability to work with diverse students and colleagues; Proven success creating inclusive classrooms; Hourly Rate: STUDIO HOURLY RATE $135/contact hr SEMINAR HOURLY RATE $156/contact hr Work Mode: On Site #LI-DNI Please prepare a Resume/CV and Cover Letter outlining your previous relevant experience. Please include any relevant portfolio materials and/or a link to your professional website if applicable. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/First-Year-Mentor---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR108924": {"title": "First Year Mentor - Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-04\nPart time\n\nThe First Year Mentor program is an exciting leadership opportunity for Lang students to build upon their own academic, pedagogical, and activist work, and to inspire first year students to become part of a community at Lang that values and practices a commitment to care, critical thinking, and social justice. Following a training and orientation in August and early September, First Year Mentors support a group of incoming first year students to help them make a smooth transition to Lang and engage with the campus community.\u00a0 \u200b Responsibilities:\u00a0 \u00a0 Training involves an intensive introduction to the TNS/Lang campus ecology, offering First Year Mentors an opportunity:\u00a0 \u25cf Meet with various campus partners from offices, departments, and programs from across the university; \u25cf Learn with and from Lang faculty about best pedagogical practices in and across the liberal arts;\u00a0 \u25cf Build cohort community and develop a collective programmatic vision for the upcoming academic year; and \u25cf Construct preliminary workshop curricula centered on the connections between social justice, interdisciplinarity, and the liberal arts. \u00a0 At Orientation, First Year Mentors: \u25cf offer informal support to new first year students and facilitate their arrival onto campus; \u25cf lead an introductory seminar discussion for first year seminar students with first year seminar faculty; \u25cf take first year seminar students on a luncheon walking tour of TNS; and \u25cf lead a panel discussion (\u201cHow I Decided\u201d) on major and course selection processes.\u00a0 \u00a0 Minimum Qualifications: \u2028 \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Completed at least one full semester of coursework at Eugene Lang College \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 In good academic standing \u00a0 Preferred Qualifications: \u00a0\u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Facilitates conversations with ease and interacts respectfully with others \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Demonstrates (active) listening for understanding and an openness to feedback, learning, and growth \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Displays effective communication across lines of identity and difference, and exhibits awareness of how positionality conditions both what and how communication transpires\u00a0 \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Displays a commitment to supporting first year students and a working understanding of the various challenges and opportunities that first year students at Lang can encounter \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Possesses a broad-based investment and interest in Lang/TNS culture and community \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Committed to (and/or open to) learning about social justice struggles and to dismantling interpersonal, institutional, and structural violence at TNS and beyond\u00a0 \u00b7 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Committed to seminar learning, process-oriented pedagogy, and the relay between academic, social, and holistic wellness\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 Hourly Rate: $20 \u00a0 Work Mode: On-campus position #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: The First Year Mentor program is a leadership opportunity for Eugene Lang students to support incoming first-year students as they transition to college, fostering a community grounded in care, critical thinking, and social justice. Mentors participate in extensive training, collaborate with faculty and campus partners, develop workshop curricula, and help facilitate Orientation activities, including seminars, campus tours, and academic advising panels. Ideal candidates are strong communicators who are committed to student support, inclusive community building, social justice, and the academic mission of Lang and The New School. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Ambassador-MA-Media-Studies---Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR108908": {"title": "Student Ambassador MA Media Studies - Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-01\nPart time\n\nSTUDENT ASSISTANT SPECIALIST - Student Ambassador The MA, Media Studies program is seeking a motivated and detail-oriented Student Assistant Specialists, student ambassador.\u00a0 They serve as\u00a0 the point of contact between the Program Director, Admissions staff, and prospective and admitted students.\u00a0 They check a dedicated email to field questions about courses, viable lines of study and research, student life, etc.\u00a0 Questions about admission policies, procedures, and requirements should be referred to the Office of Admissions. RESPONSIBILITIES The Student Assistant Specialist will: Respond to emails sent to the ambassador account for the program.\u00a0 It is expected that accounts will be checked a minimum of every other day. Upon request, the ambassador will meet with prospective students, either in person by phone or virtually, at a mutually convenient time.\u00a0 Responsible for arranging class visits for prospective students who are interested in them.\u00a0 Attend Open House/Information Sessions, including admitted student events, to provide information from the student\u2019s perspective to prospective students (typically once per month).\u00a0 Attend monthly ambassador meetings with Admissions point of contact. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Current student in good standing at The New School. Strong organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple tasks and meet deadlines. Effective written and verbal communication skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience in communications work. Prior experience in news writing and/or digital publishing. Design skills for print and digital media. ELIGIBILITY & ENROLLMENT In order to be eligible a student position in the MA, Media Studies at The New School, students must meet all the following eligibility requirements in the semester for which they would like to apply: Must be a current graduate level student. Must be registered for full-time status (9 credits/3 courses) in the semester the student would like to be considered for a position. Must be within the time limit for attaining your degree. Must be a continuing student and have achieved a minimum cumulative GPA of 3.0. Must not have more than 33% of attempted course credits as incompletes. Must not be employed at The New School as administrative staff. Must file new hire paperwork form at the University if you are a first time student employee (if selected for a position). International students must complete foreign national forms. All selected students must be authorized to work in the United States and must obtain a social security number. All students must adhere to the 20 hours per week work limit. The 20 hours includes the total amount of time for all on-campus jobs. Federal regulations limit international students to no more than 20 hours of employment per week. All students must have a social security number before obtaining employment. Hourly Rate : $20/hour. This position is 5 hours per week during the academic semester. Work Mode: Hybrid/Split \u2013 Employees hired for this position can work remotely for the majority of tasks.\u00a0 Some new student events are in-person.\u00a0#LI-DNI \u00a0We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) n/a \u00a0 Responsibilities: Respond to emails sent to the ambassador account for the program.\u00a0 It is expected that accounts will be checked a minimum of every other day. Upon request, the ambassador will meet with prospective students, either in person by phone or virtually, at a mutually convenient time.\u00a0Responsible for arranging class visits for prospective students who are interested in them.\u00a0Attend Open House/Information Sessions, including admitted student events, to provide information from the student\u2019s perspective to prospective students.\u00a0Attend monthly ambassador meetings with Admissions point of contact. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/JJ---39-West-13th-Street/Parsons-Fashion-Study-Collection---Student-Assistant--Specialist-_JR108897-1": {"title": "Parsons Fashion Study Collection - Student Assistant (Specialist)", "description": "Posted: 2026-06-01\nPart time\n\nParsons\u2019 School of Fashion (SoF) leads the industry into a future where access, equity and sustainability are the standard. We offer a Bachelor of Fine Arts degree in Fashion Design, Associate Degrees in Fashion Design as well as in Fashion Marketing and Communication, a Master of Fine Arts in Textiles, a Master of Professional Studies in Fashion Management, and a Master of Fine Arts in Fashion Design and Society. Our students, faculty, and staff are committed to supporting Indigenous resurgence; honoring the beauty of all bodies; and living in harmony with animals and the earth in ways that advance climate justice. The School of Art and Design History and Theory (ADHT) engages students at all degree levels in the analysis of art and design as essential to the formation of knowledge and culture. ADHT offers the first-year undergraduate curriculum to all Parsons students as well as a new BFA program, Design History and Practice. Graduate programs from ADHT include the MA Fashion Studies and the MA History of Design and Curatorial Studies programs. These two part-time positions and associated work is in relation to the Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC). The collection of approximately 4,000 items extends from the late 19th century to the latter half of the 20th century including noted designers: Sophie Gimbel, Claire McCardell, Donald Brooks, Norman Norell, Geoffrey Beene, Giorgio di Sant'angelo and Yves Saint Laurent. The ideal candidates will demonstrate a commitment to curatorial work, handling archival fashion items, collection management and administrative tasks associated with the Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC), including preparing /facilitating class visits from courses within ADHT and SoF.\u00a0The candidates will report directly to the Associate Dean in the School of Fashion. RESPONSIBILITIES Primary job duties and responsibilities will include: Liaising with faculty for Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC)\u00a0 visits Selecting garments based on requests in preparation for class visits Facilitating class visits to the collection, schedule-permitting Returning garments to the collection at the conclusion of class visits Cataloging/Image management: Reconciling images of archived garments with the existing database Documentation of remaining images of the collection via photography\u00a0 Uploading images to collection management system Past Perfect \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 Curating onsite exhibits in the School of Fashion Hub as needed \u00a0 MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Enrolled in one of the following undergraduate /graduate programs:\u00a0 BFA Fashion Design, AAS Fashion Design, MA Fashion Studies, MFA Fashion Design and Society, MA History of Design and Curatorial Studies Strong verbal and written communication skills\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience working with collections and/or archives\u00a0 Experience working with Past Perfect\u00a0 WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI \u00a0 Availability: Office Hours are subject to change Hourly Rate: This position is part-time and paid on an hourly basis at $20 per hour.\u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS * ( if applicable) \u00a0Resume / Cover Letter outlining expression of interest and suitability for the position Responsibilities: The primary job duties and responsibilities will include liaising with faculty for Parsons Fashion Study Collection (PFSC)\u00a0 visits and cataloging/image management using Past Perfect system. It also includes curating onsite exhibitions for the School of Fashion Hub as needed. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/H---72-Fifth-Avenue/OCSE-Student-Assistant---Welcome-Center_JR108841": {"title": "OCSE Student Assistant - Welcome Center", "description": "Posted: 2026-05-04\nPart time\n\nThe New School is looking for enthusiastic, outgoing students to join our team as Student Assistants within the University Welcome Center. Our Student Assistants facilitate in-person campus tours and participate in virtual programs and events designed to help prospective students and their families learn more about The New School\u2019s academic programs, social life, and application requirements. They also serve as a first point of contact for visitors to campus as receptionists staffing the Welcome Center front desk and contribute content to the Welcome Center Instagram account, online admitted student communities, and New School Parent and Family Hub. Responsibilities: Acting as representatives of the university, warmly welcoming all campus visitors and establishing a quality first impression.\u00a0 Displaying pride and passion for The New School Maintaining up-to-date information about our academic programs, student services, and admission requirements Consistently representing themselves and the university in a genuine, professional, and inclusive manner. Hourly Rate : The rate for this position is $19/hr. Work Mode : \u00a0Hybrid/Split - Employees hired for this position have regularly scheduled, consistent percentage of time on campus (ex 1-4x/week) and also work some of the time remotely. #LI-DNI We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce.\u200b Responsibilities: Our Student Assistants facilitate in-person campus tours and participate in virtual programs and events designed to help prospective students and their families learn more about The New School\u2019s academic programs, social life, and application requirements. They also serve as a first point of contact for visitors to campus as receptionists staffing the Welcome Center front desk and contribute content to the Welcome Center Instagram account, online admitted student communities, and New School Parent and Family Hub. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Gig-Performer--Senior-Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR108561": {"title": "Gig Performer, Senior Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-04-15\nPart time\n\nPOSITION TITLE Gig Performer, Senior Student Assistant Specialist College of Performing Arts Position Availability: This position is available starting immediately and will accept applications on a rolling basis. Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries, offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts. Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School seeks highly motivated student musicians to serve as Gig Performer, Senior Student Assistant Specialists. We\u2019re looking for energetic, creative, and responsible individuals to support live performance initiatives across all divisions at the College of Performing Arts. This is an ideal position for students at the College of Performing Arts who are interested in a career as a working musician. Performance opportunities through the School of Jazz & Contemporary Music and The College of Performing Gig Office will be scheduled through the Program & Administration Manager, School of Jazz & Contemporary Music and the Part-Time Operations Manager, School of Jazz & Contemporary Music. Performance opportunities through the Mannes School of Music will be scheduled through the Dean, Mannes School of Music; Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, Mannes School of Music; Program and Administration Manager, Mannes School of Music; and/or the Performance and Ensemble Manager. Performance opportunities through Mannes Prep will be scheduled through the Director, Mannes Prep; and the Assistant Director, Mannes Prep. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES School of Jazz & Contemporary Music and The College of Performing Gig Office: Provide instrumental and/or vocal performance services for public or private events. Analyze assigned music prior to rehearsal and performances (e.g., correct tempos, challenging transitions, dynamics) to ensure efficient use of rehearsal time. Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with internal and external parties. Respond to inquiries from students or staff in a timely manner regarding performance opportunities. Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items. Mannes School of Music: Perform for curricular and extracurricular performance opportunities as assigned, including but not limited to: Collaborative Skills courses, Techniques of Conducting courses, Mannes Outreach Events, Admitted Students Events, Staff Piano Auditions, Commencement Activities, and more. Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with internal and external parties. Prepare music as assigned by Mannes School of Music faculty, staff, and leadership, ensuring efficient use of class/rehearsal time, and success of performances. Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items. Mannes Prep: Attend Mannes Prep rehearsals and performances as directed. Learn assigned music prior to all services with Mannes Prep ensembles. Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with students, faculty, staff, and audience members. Respond to inquiries from the Mannes Prep office in a timely manner regarding rehearsal and performance schedules.\u00a0 Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items. QUALIFICATIONS Enrolled in a degree program at the College of Performing Arts. Ability to provide evidence of advanced musicianship. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Performances may occur in offsite venues. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $20.00 - $100.00, variable depending on the gig. Hours per Week: Scheduled on an as-needed basis, variable commitment with no guaranteed hours. Not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their interest, skills, and experience pertaining to this position.\u00a0Applicants will be contacted if the need for their services arises. Responsibilities: Provide instrumental and/or vocal performance services for events, preparing assigned music in advance to ensure productive rehearsals and high-quality performances. Maintain professionalism in all interactions, respond promptly to inquiries, and comply with departmental policies and administrative requirements. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist--BFA-Photo-Events-End-of-Year-_JR108796": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist (BFA Photo Events/End of Year)", "description": "Posted: 2026-04-13\nPart time\n\nThe BFA Photography program is seeking a knowledgeable, dedicated, and enthusiastic student to join our team supporting programmatic events and End of Year festivities. This person will be working closely with program leadership and the program administrator, supporting the program on internal curricular events, including year-based Critique Days and the end-of-year Thesis Exhibition. RESPONSIBILITIES Primary duties for this position include: Support the BFA Photography Program in all public events, including the Visiting Artist lecture series and End of Year festivities. Help with setup and breakdown of events. Create visual marketing materials (posters) for public and curricular events. Design and print wall labels for BFA Photography Thesis Exhibition. Update the BFA Photography Thesis website with student works. Support BFA Photography Thesis Exhibition installation and de-installation. Help to direct students in the gallery space during drop-off and pick-up. Attend, when possible, programmatic events. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Fluency in the Adobe Creative Suite (particularly InDesign). Design skills to create posters / flyers for public and program events. Strong communication skills and responsiveness to emails. Timely submission of work. Strong work ethic. \u00a0 \u00a0WORK MODE \u25cf\u00a0 Hybrid - Employees hired for this position primarily work remotely with the occasional on-campus presence required with advanced notice. Employees in this role will also be expected to use their best judgment to be on campus when necessary in order to best fulfill the responsibilities of their job descriptions. #LI-DNI \u00a0 HOURLY RATE $21 per hour 5-10 hours a week for 5 weeks \u00a0 We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: In this position the responsibilities include building out the BFA Photo Thesis website pages for each graduating student and uploading the images and text. As well as utilizing InDesign to support the production of materials for the BFA Photography Thesis exhibition.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR108778": {"title": "Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-04-10\nPart time\n\nThe Student Assistant Specialist will help troubleshoot technical issues that arise and support the professor in demonstrating equipment usage. RESPONSIBILITIES Check out equipment at the beginning of class Charge batteries at the beginning of class Test batteries at the beginning of class Test cameras at the beginning of class Test sound equipment at the beginning of class Clean lenses throughout class Demonstrate use of equipment throughout class Return equipment at the end of class MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with camera equipment Familiarity with sound equipment Familiarity with lighting equipment Familiarity with editing software WORK MODE On-Campus Position #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $17 per hour We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Assisting the professor in demonstrating how to use the equipment, maintaining the equipment, and helping the students with editing questions. \u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Creative--Artistic--and-Production-Staff_JR108557": {"title": "Temporary Creative, Artistic, and Production Staff", "description": "Posted: 2026-04-02\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking highly qualified Temporary Creative, Artistic, and Production Staff to join its Production team. Reporting to the Vice Dean of Performing Arts and leadership of the Production & Performance Operations team, the College of Performing Arts\u2019 Temporary Creative, Artistic, and Production Staff are directly responsible for providing high-quality, professional theatrical services that guarantee the success of the College\u2019s curricular productions. Temporary Creative, Artistic, and Production Staff shape and execute the artistic vision of their assigned productions. They coordinate design, staging, and technical elements to ensure rehearsals and performances run smoothly. Through close collaboration with our students, staff, and faculty, Temporary Creative, Artistic, and Production Staff maintain consistency in artistic quality throughout all stages of the production process. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This position does not have regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Production department. Temporary Creative, Artistic, and Production Staff will receive a Memorandum of Understanding, outlining the terms and conditions for all services they are scheduled for. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide creative and/or logistical support to curricular theatrical productions as assigned. Ensure any relevant deliverables are generated meeting the Production\u2019s deadlines Coordinate with department heads and leadership as necessary. Communicate and collaborate with all stakeholders, attending all required meetings. Additional responsibilities, expectations, and deadlines tailored to each individual engagement will be outlined in a Memorandum of Understanding. QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated experience in theatrical production, stage management, design, direction, or related support roles. Strong organizational, communication, and collaboration skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities in fast-paced, deadline-driven environments. Knowledge of standard theatrical practices, safety protocols, and production workflows. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Prior experience working in academic or higher education environments WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Hybrid work arrangements may be considered on a case-by-case basis, depending on the nature of the role and operational needs. Any such arrangement must be discussed and approved in advance by the appropriate division lead or their designee. SALARY RANGE $25.00-$50.00/hour, dependent upon the nature of the engagement WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Large-Ensemble-Ringer_JR108768": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Large Ensemble Ringer", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-30\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Performance Operations area at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Overhire Large Ensemble Ringers to join its team. Reporting to the Large Ensemble Manager, the Overhire Large Ensemble Ringer supports initiatives within the Performance Operations - Large Ensembles area on an as-needed basis. This includes Mannes Orchestra, Mannes American Composers Ensemble, Mannes Opera Orchestra, and other ensembles. This position reports to the Manager of Large Ensembles.\u00a0 Alumni of the College of Performing Arts are strongly encouraged to apply. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Large Ensemble department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Attend all the rehearsals and performances as scheduled with the assigned ensemble Bring one\u2019s own instrument(s) and gear as needed Analyze assigned music prior to rehearsal and performances (e.g., correct tempos, challenging transitions, dynamics) to ensure efficient use of rehearsal time Exhibit professionalism in all interactions with students, faculty, staff, and audience members Be prepared to play professionally with one\u2019s assigned part Adhere to departmental guidelines regarding reporting hours, payroll deadlines, and other items including attire.\u00a0 QUALIFICATIONS Classically trained musician Ability to play all assigned instruments, including auxiliaries Prior orchestral/large ensemble experience.\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with contemporary and non-traditional scores, including opera. Alumni of the College of Performing Arts WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Hours per Week: Up to 15 hours per week, per cycle, variable commitment with no guaranteed hours. Rehearsals are typically on weekdays 1 pm to 3:50 pm, with some nights and/ or weekends as needed. SALARY RANGE Hourly Rate: $55.00/hour Rehearsals (Up to 3 hours): $165.00 per service\u00a0 Dress Rehearsals (Up to 4 hours): $220.00 per service Concerts (Up to 4 hours): $220.00 per service Any work above minimum call is paid at $55.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Scenic-Artist_JR108766": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Scenic Artist", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-30\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Scenic Artist to join its Performance Operations \u2013 Scenic Department Staff. The Scenic Department is responsible for the creation, alteration, and sourcing of all scenery for productions in the Mannes School of Music, School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and School of Drama. The Overhire Scenic Artist is delegated as needed to work on completion and finishing of scenic builds. The Overhire Scenic Artist is a qualified and organized collaborator who can follow instructions, work independently, and thrive in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly team-oriented environment. The Overhire Scenic Artist reports to the Production Shops Manager.\u00a0\u00a0 This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Scenic department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a painter to assist the Scenic Charge Artist complete tasks as needed.\u00a0 Carry out duties assigned by Scenic Charge or Production Shops Manager. Maintain a clean paint area in the prop shop and clean up at end of day Work alongside carpenters for space management and build completion Apply painting techniques to scenery\u00a0 Ensure proper fire retardancy is being added to paint and scenery as needed/described by the Production shops manager\u00a0 Additional duties as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with theatrical painting techniques Comfortability working alone and asking questions for clarification Ability to read a scale rule Experience working as a theatrical scenic painter WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Some weekends and weeknights may be scheduled as needed and defined by the production tech schedules. #LI-Onsite Hourly Rate: $30.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, and an artistic portfolio or website of samples. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Z---151-Bank-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Driver---Trucking-Hand_JR108764": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Driver & Trucking Hand", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-27\nPart time\n\nAt The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Driver & Trucking Hand to join its Performance Operations \u2013 Scenic Department Staff. The Scenic Department is responsible for the creation, alteration, and sourcing of all scenery for productions in the Mannes School of Music, School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and School of Drama. The Overhire Driver & Trucking Hand is delegated as needed to work on storage pulls, and/ or shopping runs. The Overhire Driver & Trucking Hand is a qualified and organized collaborator who can follow instructions, work independently, and thrive in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly team-oriented environment. The Overhire Lead Driver reports to the Production Shops Manager.\u00a0 This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Scenic department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Operate truck for storage pulls and/or shopping runs or serve as a passenger in the truck to assist with loading and unloading Maintain clean usable area at storage unit\u00a0 Ensure buildings and rental equipment are well maintained during shifts Use safe moving practices to ensure no one is putting themselves at risk of injury in lifting and moving things Additional duties as assigned Track receipts as necessary QUALIFICATIONS Comfort driving a box truck in Manhattan Ability to lift 50lbs comfortably State issued Driver's License, if driving\u00a0 Knowledge of NYC driving regulations regarding commercial vehicles and parking\u00a0 PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Strong communication skills and ability to work with other crew members collaboratively WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-Onsite Work calls may include shifts scheduled for early in the morning and on weekends as needed, and may require flexibility to coordinate short term calls SALARY RANGE $27.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position, and a copy of a Driver License for administration to have on file.\u00a0 We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Jazz-Video---Lights--Student-Assistant-Specialist_JR108664": {"title": "Jazz Video & Lights, Student Assistant Specialist", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-24\nPart time\n\nPOSITION TITLE Jazz Video & Lights, Student Assistant Specialist School of Jazz and Contemporary Music College of Performing Arts Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz and Contemporary Music at the College of Performing Arts seeks highly motivated students to serve as Jazz Video & Lights, Student Assistant Specialist . We\u2019re looking for an enthusiastic and reliable individual(s) to support initiatives within The School of Jazz and Contemporary Music . This position reports to the Part Time Operations Manager and is an ideal position for students who are interested in a career in lighting design, video production, or live event production. This position is open to student applicants with Federal Work Study awards, as well as student applicants without a Federal Work Study award. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Manage lighting setup and operation for live events and performances. Operate and manage live streaming for events hosted on the College of Performing Arts YouTube channel. Set up and break down audio and video equipment before and after performances. Collaborate with faculty, staff, and students to ensure the successful execution of performances and events. Maintain a professional and supportive presence during rehearsals and live shows. QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with video live streaming tools and techniques. Basic understanding of lighting setup and management. Strong organizational and file management skills. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to work flexible hours, including evenings and weekends as needed. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \u00a0 Recipient of Federal Work Study award. Previous experience in live sound, lighting, or video production. Knowledge of advanced lighting techniques and video production equipment. WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $18.00 Hours per Week : 3-20, or as needed. Not to exceed 20 hours in a single workweek. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit both a resume and cover letter outlining their interest, skills, and experience pertaining to this position; and a Federal Work Study award letter if applicable. Position Availability: This position is available starting 3/5/2026 and will be open until filled. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. Responsibilities: Manage lighting, audio-visual setup, and live streaming for College of Performing Arts events and performances, including equipment setup and breakdown. Collaborate with faculty, staff, and students to ensure the smooth and professional execution of rehearsals, live shows, and streamed events. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-House-Manager_JR108586-1": {"title": "Temporary Overhire House Manager", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-19\nPart time\n\nPOSITION TITLE Overhire House Manager Performance Operations - Front of House College of Performing Arts INTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The Front of House area at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking\u00a0 highly qualified Overhire House Managers to join its team. Reporting to the Manager of Event Operations, the Overhire House Manager is directly responsible for the patron experience at on-site events. This role oversees ushers on-site, liaises with production staff, fields patron and performance-related issues that arise, and ensures that venues are safe and clean. The House Manager is responsible for creating an excellent experience for patrons, serving as the point person for ushers and performers, and overseeing fire code compliance in performance spaces. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Front of House department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provide excellent customer service to patrons Maintain safe and clean public spaces, communicate with facilities and campus safety as necessary Ensure fire code compliance in performance spaces and provide leadership in emergency situations Serve as the point person for ushers\u2019 scheduling and event-specific duties Coordinate performance timelines with relevant parties (stage management, performers, faculty, audio/video staff, etc) Oversee audience check in, distribution of programs, and audience seating Work with production staff to meet event-specific needs Resolve on-site issues and conflicts, documenting incidents as needed Complete a house report for each event worked Additional responsibilities as assigned QUALIFICATIONS 2 years of experience in live performance events Previous experience in customer service roles A background in the performing arts, especially music and/or theater Demonstrated commitment to Equity, Inclusion, Social Justice, and the transformative power of the arts and education PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \u00a0 Bachelor\u2019s Degree or equivalent professional experience. Previous House Management experience Active F-03 Fireguard Certification WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Nights and weekends are required. SALARY RANGE $22.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume and a cover letter outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/New-School-New-Books-Presenter_JR108748": {"title": "New School New Books Presenter", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-19\nPart time\n\nBased on contributions to a New School publication, participation in this year's iteration of New School New Books , the annual reading series fourth edition on Friday, February 20, 2026, in Wollman Hall.\u00a0 New School New Books was introduced by the VLC in 2023 and most recently convened this past March, to celebrate recent publications by university centers, faculty, and students. The 2026 edition comes together with a slightly different focus\u2014highlighting various publishing platforms and periodicals directed by TNS faculty, students, and staff. We are choosing \"periodicals\" as a curatorial frame to respond to the setting: Wollman Hall will feature a new installation of 16 prints by legendary artist Robert Rauschenberg (1925\u20132008) that focus on and incorporate references to \"the news.\" Offering an apt prompt for the current moment, these prints are part of The New School Art Collection. The gathering will showcase the rich body of print and digital newspapers, journals, magazines, and other periodicals published by members of the university community. We invite representatives from these publications to join us for a series of informal, conversational panels throughout the day on the urgent need for journalism and how on-campus publishing platforms can offer timely, in-depth responses to some of the most socially, institutionally and politically pressing issues we face today. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 Key Responsibilities: Present a clear and engaging 5\u20138 minute overview of your publication, including its mission, editorial focus, and recent work. Represent your journal as a knowledgeable contributor, offering insight into its role within campus publishing and broader public discourse. Participate actively in panel discussions, contributing thoughtful perspectives on journalism and responding to audience or moderator questions. Collaborate with event organizers and fellow presenters to ensure smooth timing, coordination, and flow of the program. Engage professionally with attendees and remain present throughout the event to support its collaborative and conversational format. Qualifications : - Currently enrolled at TNS and on staff/contributor/editor at one of the journals\u00a0 or publications that were solicited for participation. Hourly Rate : $20/hr Work mode : Hybrid #LI-DNI Responsibilities: Present a clear and engaging 5\u20138 minute overview of your publication, including its mission, editorial focus, and recent work. Represent your journal as a knowledgeable contributor, offering insight into its role within campus publishing and broader public discourse. Participate actively in panel discussions, contributing thoughtful perspectives on journalism and responding to audience or moderator questions. Collaborate with event organizers and fellow presenters to ensure smooth timing, coordination, and flow of the program. Engage professionally with attendees and remain present throughout the event to support its collaborative and conversational format. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Illustration-Senior-Show-Student-Workers_JR108683": {"title": "Illustration Senior Show Student Workers", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-19\nPart time\n\nThe New School was founded by forward-thinking educators in 1919 to defy the intellectual constraints of a traditional college education. BFA Illustration is seeking a student employee to support the BFA Illustration Senior Show. Applicants must be seniors in the Illustration program. In addition, students must have strong organizational skills and attention to detail; be able to carefully transport and handle artwork and materials; print posters; be familiar with Google Spreadsheets; and communicate responsibly with Professors Jordin Isip and Rodger Stevens, the Senior Show Coordinators. Support the Senior Show Coordinating Faculty Jordin Isip and Rodger Stevens. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with the intake of Seniors\u2019 artwork and promotional materials on Fri, March 6 Catalog the received student exhibition materials on Google Spreadsheets Transfer artwork and exhibition materials from the Illustration Office to Kellen Gallery Prepare artwork and materials for installation Assist with the installation of artwork, exhibition materials, promotional cards, labels, statements, and exhibition signage to the walls Create and print promotions, posters and flyers for the exhibition Set up the catering for the\u00a0 Tue, March 17th opening reception Assist with the de-installation of artwork and other exhibition materials MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Provide a bulleted list of the minimum qualifications that are necessary for someone to be considered for the position. All qualifications must comply with applicable state and Federal legislation. Acceptable qualifications include: Education, specialized knowledge Skills, Abilities, and other professional attributes that are relevant to the work Professional Certification Experience Schedule requirements outside of regular business hours PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS You may also wish to indicate additional qualifications beyond those above that are not absolutely required but that will help candidates distinguish themselves \u2013 specific skills, advanced degrees, etc. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI Hourly Rate:\u00a0 21.00/hr Responsibilities: The students will assist with the intake of Seniors\u2019 artwork and promotional materials for Senior Show, catalog the received student exhibition materials on Google Spreadsheets, transfer artwork and exhibition materials from the Illustration Office to Kellen Gallery, prepare artwork and materials for installation, assist with the installation of artwork, exhibition materials, promotional cards, labels, statements, and exhibition signage to the walls, create and print promotions, posters and flyers for the exhibition, set up the catering for the opening reception and assist with the de-installation of artwork and other exhibition materials We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/L---2-West-13th-Street/Senior-Student-Assistant-Specialist-SJDC-Galleries_JR108549": {"title": "Senior Student Assistant Specialist SJDC Galleries", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-18\nPart time\n\nPOSITION TITLE: Senior Student Assistant Specialist SJDC Galleries The Sheila C. Johnson Design Center (SJDC) galleries at Parsons/The New School are looking for a\u00a0Senior Student Assistant Specialist\u00a0to help with exhibition planning and installation across our two campus galleries. Reporting to the Assistant Director, this role includes hands-on support with layout, installation, and preparing all gallery text and print materials. The work involves using special printers and professional grade paper, and may include decisions about how the gallery space is used for artwork. We\u2019re looking for someone who has experience or coursework in art installation, curating, or gallery work and who can work independently in a fast-paced, collaborative environment. RESPONSIBILITIES Help plan exhibition layouts alongside the Assistant Director. Support decisions about artwork placement and how the space flows. Install artworks using safe and proper handling methods. Use specialized printers and paper to prepare wall labels, text, and printed materials. Assist with mounting and installing all printed items in the gallery. Help with production, fabrication, and overall install process. Provide support with schedules, logistics, and general gallery needs during install. Take on other duties related to exhibitions as needed. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Experience working in galleries or with exhibition installation. Able to use hand tools (like drills, levels, saws, etc.) safely. Comfortable making layout and setup decisions independently. Knows how to format and print professional materials for gallery use. Able to work flexible hours, including evenings or weekends during installs. Works well both alone and with a team. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience or coursework in art installation, curating, or gallery operations. Confidence using tools, ladders, and handling delicate artworks. Ability to work both independently and as part of a team. Interest in exhibitions, design, and how shows come together. WORK MODE On-Campus Position - Employees in this position are expected to work on-campus during regularly scheduled work hours due to the nature of the work being that which is not feasible, or not in the best interest of the organization, to be performed remotely. #LI-DNI HOURLY RATE $24.13 per hour SPECIAL INSTRUCTION TO APPLICANTS If you require additional documents outside of a Resume or Cover Letter (i.e. a writing sample), please include details specifying what those documents are. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. \u00a0 Responsibilities: This student role supports exhibition planning and installation by assisting with layout decisions, artwork placement, and ensuring safe handling of works. The position also involves preparing and installing printed materials, supporting production and logistics during install, and helping with overall gallery operations as needed. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Carpenter_JR108733": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Carpenter", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-17\nPart time\n\nPOSITION TITLE Overhire Carpenter Performance Operations - Theater & Opera College of Performing Arts INTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The College of Performing Arts at The New School is seeking a highly qualified Overhire Carpenter to join its Performance Operations \u2013 Scenic Department Staff. The Scenic Department is responsible for the creation, alteration, and sourcing of all scenery for productions in the Mannes School of Music, School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and School of Drama. The Overhire Carpenter is delegated as needed to work on builds, load ins and strikes.\u00a0 The Overhire Carpenter is a qualified and organized collaborator who can follow instructions, work independently, and thrive in a dynamic, fast-paced, and highly team-oriented environment. The Overhire Carpenter reports to the Production Shops Manager.\u00a0 This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the Scenic department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Serve as a general hand called in to work with the Scenic Department and carry out tasks as assigned including cross-department work as assigned by the Production Shops Manager or Temporary Lead Carpenter Assist loading and unloading scenery into and out of performance venues Work in production shops to construct scenery Use ladders and genies in performance spaces to hang soft goods Occasionally use sewing machines as needed for scenic soft good repairs and builds Maintain a safe and clean work environment during build and load in processes\u00a0 Additional duties as assigned QUALIFICATIONS Familiarity with some or many tools in a wood working setting Ability to lift 50lbs comfortably\u00a0 Experience as a stagehand or carpenter in other institutions WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. Some weekends and weeknights may be scheduled as needed and defined by the ongoing production tech schedules SALARY RANGE $27.00/hour WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Jazz-A2_JR108656": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Jazz A2", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-05\nPart time\n\nPOSITION TITLE Overhire Jazz A2 School of Jazz and Contemporary Music College of Performing Arts INTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz and Contemporary Music at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Overhire Jazz A2 to join its team. Reporting to the Part Time Operations Manager, the Overhire Jazz A2 is responsible for supporting the Audio and Video needs of academic performances and partnership events within the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music.\u00a0 The Overhire Jazz A2 provides crucial support in the successful execution of concerts, panel discussions, and festivals at the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music. This position is responsible for assisting with setup, operation, and breakdown of audio systems, as well as serving as a key crew member during events. The Overhire Jazz A2 works under the direction of the Overhire Jazz A1 and reports to the Part-Time Operations Manager, School of Jazz and Contemporary Music. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Assist with the setup, operation, and strike of all audio equipment for performances and events Support the Overhire Jazz A1 during soundchecks and live events, including managing microphones, monitors, playback, and communication systems Maintain and troubleshoot wired and wireless microphones, IEM systems, and backline as needed Follow and execute technical documentation including input lists, stage plots, signal flow diagrams, and run-of-show documents Provide professional and courteous support to performers, faculty, and production staff Maintain organization and cleanliness of audio equipment and performance spaces Mix smaller events independently, including recording sessions, and rehearsals as scheduled\u00a0 Additional responsibilities as assigned by the Overhire Jazz A1, or the Part-Time Operations Manager QUALIFICATIONS At least 1 year of experience working on live events in a support role (A2, Audio Technician, etc.) Familiarity with live sound systems, microphones, cabling, and digital consoles (Yamaha, Allen & Heath, Midas, Behringer) Working knowledge of basic audio signal flow and gain structure Some experience with audio-related software (QLab, Wireless Workbench, Dante Controller, Dante Virtual Soundcard) Ability to read and follow event documentation including input lists and stage plots Strong communication, reliability, and collaborative skills Ability to work calmly and efficiently under pressure during live events Comfortable working with students, faculty, and a diverse production team Ability to adhere to university COVID-19 policy and requirements Commitment to Equity, Inclusion, Social Justice, and the transformative power of the arts and education PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS \u00a0 Experience working in music, theater, or academic environments Familiarity with Dante networks Comfortable working at heights and using ladders or lifts FO3 Fireguard Certification Ability to lift up to 50 lbs Self-motivated and detail-oriented WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE $28.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Jazz-A1_JR108655": {"title": "Temporary Overhire Jazz A1", "description": "Posted: 2026-03-05\nPart time\n\nPOSITION TITLE Overhire Jazz A1 School of Jazz and Contemporary Music College of Performing Arts INTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH Join The New School\u00a0 At The New School, we redefine the boundaries of education, creativity, and innovation. Founded in 1919 and located in the heart of New York City, we are home to over 9,000 students from over 100 countries , offering more than 135 degree and diploma programs across our renowned colleges, including Parsons School of Design, The New School for Social Research, Eugene Lang college of Liberal Arts, Parsons Paris, and the College of Performing Arts . Our progressive, interdisciplinary approach has earned us recognition as one of the most forward-thinking institutions in the world. POSITION/ROLE OVERVIEW The School of Jazz and Contemporary Music at The New School\u2019s College of Performing Arts is seeking highly qualified Overhire Jazz A1 to join its team. Reporting to the Part Time Operations Manager, the Overhire Jazz A1is responsible for supporting the Audio and Video needs of academic performances and partnership events within the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music.\u00a0 The A1 is responsible for the overall sound quality of live events and live streams, including concerts, panel discussions, and festivals across all schools within the College of Performing Arts. The A1 also leads and directs the audio crew during crew calls as needed. This position reports to the Part-Time Operations Manager, School of Jazz and Contemporary Music. Formed in 2015, the College of Performing Arts draws together the Mannes School of Music, the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music, and the School of Drama. College of Performing Arts students are actors, musicians, performers, writers, improvisers, creative technologists, entrepreneurs, composers, arts managers, and multidisciplinary artists. Our community collaborates with colleagues across The New School in a wide array of disciplines, from the visual arts and fashion design, to the social sciences, public policy, advocacy, and more. This is an overhire position without regular or guaranteed weekly hours. Opportunities for work will be scheduled on an as-needed basis by the School of Jazz and Contemporary Music department. This position may be renewed pending institutional need and availability of funding. CORE CAPABILITIES, PRIMARY DUTIES, AND RESPONSIBILITIES Understand and execute the technical aspects of each design, including interpreting audio needs, signal flow plans, I/O lists, and stage plots. Mix Front of House (FOH), monitors, and streaming audio feeds, occasionally simultaneously on the same console. Provide professional and courteous service to all parties. Fulfill the role of System Technician, Console Operator, or both, depending on the size and scope of the event. Manage setup, operation, and strike of equipment before, during, and after events. This includes operating audio consoles, playback systems, and all related audio gear. Supervise and direct Audio Technicians to ensure completion of required tasks and delivery of the CoPA standard. Provide guidance and leadership as needed. Perform additional responsibilities as assigned. QUALIFICATIONS At least 1 year of experience as a live sound engineer for concerts, corporate events\u00a0 or theater Live-mixing skills and working knowledge of digital consoles, specifically, Yamaha, Allen & Heath, Midas, and Behringer Excellent knowledge of proper gain structure and signal flow Working knowledge of audio related software, such as Qlab 4&5, Wireless Workbench 6, Pro Tools, Logic. Dante Virtual Soundcard, Dante Controller, etc. Ability to interpret event technical documents including technical riders, signal flow diagrams, input lists, stage plots, run-of-show, and cue lists. Strong verbal and written communication, organization, and time-management skills Ability to communicate with other departments and event coordinator to ensure a smooth show. Punctuality, professionalism, and a positive collaborative attitude when working with Students, Faculty, Staff, and Team Members.\u00a0 Experienced or able to learn and develop fluency in The New School\u2019s suite of applications including Google Suite, Microsoft Office, and WorkDay. Ability to adhere to university COVID-19 policy and requirements Demonstrated commitment to Equity, Inclusion, Social Justice, and the transformative power of the arts and education PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Corporate, Special Events, Festival Sound, Line by Line Musical Mixing, and Broadcast experience are all plusses\u00a0 Self-motivated with ability to work with minimal supervision Ability to safely climb ladders and scaffolding, operate Genies, and comfortable working at heights Able to lift up to 50lbs WORK MODE This position is primarily in-person and is based on campus to support effective in-person collaboration and engagement. SALARY RANGE $35.00/hour\u00a0 WHY JOIN US? This is an extraordinary opportunity to work closely with a visionary leader and contribute meaningfully to a forward-thinking institution that\u2019s reshaping the future of higher education. You\u2019ll be at the nexus of strategy, operations, and innovation in a role that grows with your impact. We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The New School recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS TO APPLICANTS Applicants should submit a resume outlining their skills and experience concerning this position. We look forward to receiving your application!"}}, "rows": {"https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/German-Language-Tutor_JR109695": {"school_id": 974, "posting_url": "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/B---65-West-11th-Street/German-Language-Tutor_JR109695", "job_title_in_post": "German Language Tutor", "position_type": "Non-academic", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": "2026-10-19"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-General-Student-Assistant-FWS_JR109286": {"school_id": 974, "posting_url": "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/E---25-East-13th-Street/E4-General-Student-Assistant-FWS_JR109286", "job_title_in_post": "E4 General Student Assistant FWS", "position_type": "Unclassified", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_school": "", "area_key_words": "", "deadline_of_application": "", "position_start_date": "2026-10-16"}, "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Stage-Manager_JR109692": {"school_id": 974, "posting_url": "https://newschool.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/I---55-West-13th-Street/Temporary-Overhire-Stage-Manager_JR109692", "job_title_in_post": "Temporary Overhire Stage Manager", "position_type": "Non-academic", "job_term": "Part-time", "department_or_s